  "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229": [
    {
      "text": "However, psychiatry&amp;#8217;s biological basis is currently limited to a few rare genetic disorders, such as certain forms of autism with intellectual disability. The first part of this article presents doubts expressed by leaders in American psychiatry regarding the ability of neuroscience to reliably inform mental health practices in the foreseeable future. Overall, mental health is not solely a biomedical issue; it is a broader public health concern.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "For most somatic diseases, diagnosis and treatment are guided by biological knowledge.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "For other mental disorders, no biomarkers have yet been validated to aid diagnosis.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Likewise, most psychotropic medications in use were discovered in the 1950s and 1960s through chance observations or are derived from these early molecules.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Between these doubts and the promises of spectacular advances widely reported in the media, there is a considerable gap.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "The second part examines these media misrepresentations, their causes, and their consequences.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "The third part highlights the specificities of psychiatry, particularly regarding diagnostic practices.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Finally, the fourth part discusses primary prevention policies that could help reduce the economic and social costs of mental disorders.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "While not exhaustive, these include reducing the rate of premature births and teenage motherhood, strengthening educational support for primary school children, limiting children&amp;#8217;s screen time, and reducing income and wealth inequalities.",
      "paperTitle": "[Not Available].",
      "doi": "10.3917/spub.hs1.2026.0229",
      "source": "Sante publique (Vandoeuvre-les-Nancy, France)",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    }
  ],
  "10.1093/aje/kwag234": [
    {
      "text": "Addressing unhealthy movement behaviours in adolescence could reduce socioeconomic inequalities in adult cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk. G-formula-based causal decomposition quantified changes in socioeconomic inequalities under two interventional scenarios: (1) eliminating unhealthy movement behaviours across the population, (2) equalizing the low-SEP group's movement behaviour distributions to match the high-SEP group. Whole-population interventions targeting unhealthy adolescent movement behaviours, particularly physical activity, may reduce absolute socioeconomic inequalities in adult CVD risk.",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Among 6608 adolescents enrolled in the Add Health cohort (1994-1995) and followed into adulthood (2016-2018), movement behaviours (physical activity, screen time, sleep) were classified as healthy or unhealthy based on adherence to the 24-Hour Movement Guidelines.",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Parental education and family financial hardship captured socioeconomic position (SEP).",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "The outcome was 30-year CVD risk in adulthood.",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Adolescents whose parents did not graduate from college/university had a higher adult CVD risk than those whose parents did (RD: 6.9%, RR: 1.73).",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Eliminating all unhealthy movement behaviours reduced the RD to 4.9% (-2.0% absolute change), while the RR remained similar (1.76).",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Eliminating unhealthy levels of physical activity produced the largest absolute reduction (-1.7%), with negligible reductions for screen time (-0.2%) and sleep (-0.2%).",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "The equalization scenario had minimal impact, and results were similar for family financial hardship.",
      "paperTitle": "Contribution of Adolescent Movement Behaviours to Socioeconomic Inequalities in Adult Cardiovascular Disease Risk: A Causal Decomposition in a Population-Based Cohort.",
      "doi": "10.1093/aje/kwag234",
      "source": "American journal of epidemiology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    }
  ],
  "10.1002/ajcp.70106": [
    {
      "text": "Outdoor play contributes to children's development and well-being, yet engagement in outdoor play has declined and there is some evidence of disparities in outdoor play across sociodemographic groups. This study examined individual, family, and community factors associated with outdoor play among children (3-5 years) in the United States. Outdoor play was significantly negatively associated with race and ethnicity (Asian, Black or African American, Hispanic, and multiracial relative to non-Hispanic White children), urbanicity, household income, and screen time. Community-level interventions that reduce structural barriers and improve equitable access to safe outdoor spaces may increase outdoor play and support healthy child development.",
      "paperTitle": "Pathways to play: Race, neighborhood safety, and other correlates of outdoor play in early childhood.",
      "doi": "10.1002/ajcp.70106",
      "source": "American journal of community psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Using structural equation modeling, we analyzed cross-sectional data from 35,178 parents in the National Survey of Children's Health.",
      "paperTitle": "Pathways to play: Race, neighborhood safety, and other correlates of outdoor play in early childhood.",
      "doi": "10.1002/ajcp.70106",
      "source": "American journal of community psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "It was positively associated with neighborhood safety, daycare usage, and child health.",
      "paperTitle": "Pathways to play: Race, neighborhood safety, and other correlates of outdoor play in early childhood.",
      "doi": "10.1002/ajcp.70106",
      "source": "American journal of community psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "The racial and ethnic disparities were partly mediated by neighborhood safety and socioeconomic factors, although significant direct associations remained.",
      "paperTitle": "Pathways to play: Race, neighborhood safety, and other correlates of outdoor play in early childhood.",
      "doi": "10.1002/ajcp.70106",
      "source": "American journal of community psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Contrary to expectations, urbanicity did not moderate the association between income and outdoor play.",
      "paperTitle": "Pathways to play: Race, neighborhood safety, and other correlates of outdoor play in early childhood.",
      "doi": "10.1002/ajcp.70106",
      "source": "American journal of community psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "These findings underscore the importance of multilevel influences on early childhood play.",
      "paperTitle": "Pathways to play: Race, neighborhood safety, and other correlates of outdoor play in early childhood.",
      "doi": "10.1002/ajcp.70106",
      "source": "American journal of community psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Future research should integrate neighborhood-level and surveillance data to better understand how safety, environmental hazards, and access contribute to disparities.",
      "paperTitle": "Pathways to play: Race, neighborhood safety, and other correlates of outdoor play in early childhood.",
      "doi": "10.1002/ajcp.70106",
      "source": "American journal of community psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    }
  ],
