  "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219": [
    {
      "text": "Smartphone use has become nearly universal among adolescents, raising concerns about its potential effects on attention, well-being, and learning. Over the past decade, rapid growth in smartphone ownership and social media engagement among youth has prompted increasing interest in how digital technologies influence cognitive and psychological development. This integrative narrative review synthesizes current evidence on smartphone use among adolescents, focusing on three primary domains: psychological well-being, cognitive functioning and learning, and emerging policy responses such as school cellphone bans.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "At the same time, schools and youth organizations have begun implementing policies that restrict smartphone use in an effort to reduce distraction and improve student engagement.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Despite these efforts, the broader impacts of smartphone use and restriction policies on adolescent development remain a topic of active debate.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Research across observational studies, experimental work, and systematic reviews suggests that excessive smartphone use is associated with modest but consistent relationships with sleep disruption, reduced attentional capacity, and increased internalizing symptoms among some adolescents.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "However, these effects are heterogeneous and appear to be moderated by factors including type of digital activity, timing of use, and individual vulnerability.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Evidence examining the effects of school-based smartphone bans indicates improvements in classroom attention and engagement, although broader impacts on mental health and long-term digital habits remain unclear.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Furthermore, adolescent views on smart phone bans are mixed, with students recognizing that there are positive and negative effects of both smartphones and bans.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Together, these findings suggest that smartphone use represents a complex developmental exposure rather than a uniformly harmful or beneficial influence.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "Future research should examine how structured phone-free environments, including school and extracurricular settings, influence adolescents' social interaction, digital behavior, and well-being over time.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital distraction and adolescent development: a narrative review of smartphone use, cognitive effects, and policy interventions.",
      "doi": "10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1832219",
      "source": "Frontiers in psychology",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    }
  ],
  "10.1590/1413-8123202631.13912024": [
    {
      "text": "Our study aimed to explore factors influencing the timing of food intake, including physiological, behavioral, personal preferences, cultural, and environmental dynamics. Chronotype emerged as the primary predictor across all outcomes: as chronotype shifted towards eveningness, the frequency of breakfast skipping increased and food intake timings delayed. While depression, obesity, evening screen time, smoking, caffeine consumption, having dinner as the largest meal, short sleep duration, and working remotely were associated with a later eating midpoint, age, female sex, exercising, evening alcohol consumption, diet quality, working in-office, and higher education were associated with an earlier eating midpoint. Insights into modifiable factors influencing eating timing can inform public health interventions aimed at promoting healthier eating habits aligned with the circadian system, tailored to diverse regional and individual contexts.",
      "paperTitle": "Chrononutrition: exploring determinants and feature importance of eating timing among Brazilian adults.",
      "doi": "10.1590/1413-8123202631.13912024",
      "source": "Ciencia & saude coletiva",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Using data from 5,260 Brazilian adults collected through a virtual, exploratory, population-based survey (Sonar-Brazil/2023-24), Lasso regression identified the determinants of the timing of the first and last food intake, lunch, eating midpoint, and weekly breakfast skipping.",
      "paperTitle": "Chrononutrition: exploring determinants and feature importance of eating timing among Brazilian adults.",
      "doi": "10.1590/1413-8123202631.13912024",
      "source": "Ciencia & saude coletiva",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Random Forest analysis was used to assess the feature importance for the eating midpoint.",
      "paperTitle": "Chrononutrition: exploring determinants and feature importance of eating timing among Brazilian adults.",
      "doi": "10.1590/1413-8123202631.13912024",
      "source": "Ciencia & saude coletiva",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    }
  ],
  "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196": [
    {
      "text": "Data from the large, diverse nationwide Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development Study cohort (N = 5686) were analyzed to investigate the association between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents. Generalized additive mixed effects models were used to evaluate the association between gaming behaviors and cortical morphology, adjusting for demographics, genetic ancestry, socioeconomic status, non-gaming screen time, pubertal stage, body mass index percentile, scanner characteristics, and familial relationships. Our results demonstrate that different gaming behaviors are associated with distinct patterns of cortical morphology.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Video gaming is highly prevalent in adolescence, yet its relationship with cortical morphology in early adolescence remains unclear.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Adolescents (mean: 14.2 years old) reported time spent playing single and multi-player games each day and their frequency of playing mature-rated games.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Measures of cortical volume, surface area, and thickness of regions defined by the Desikan-Killiany atlas were obtained from 3T MRI scanners.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "In addition to regional analysis, vertexwise analysis was performed using the Fast and Efficient Mixed Effects Algorithm, which provides higher spatial resolution.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Higher total gaming time was associated with lower cortical surface area and thickness across multiple regions.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Single-player but not multi-player gaming was associated with altered morphology in regions involved in language processing and social cognition, which may be due to the different processes engaged by each game type.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Mature-rated gaming frequency was also associated with lower cortical thickness in several regions and lower surface area in the right parahippocampal gyrus.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "Decreases in cortical surface area and thickness are a normative feature of adolescent development, and future research on long-term outcomes is required to determine whether these differences reflect variation outside of normative developmental trajectories and the directionality of this association.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations between video gaming behaviors and cortical morphology in adolescents.",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122196",
      "source": "NeuroImage",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    }
  ],
  "10.1007/s12098-026-06466-5": [
    {
      "text": "Adolescent obesity is one of the most pressing pediatric public health challenges of this generation, with a near tripling of prevalence over three decades. Most adolescents with obesity have an exogenous cause, but a small proportion harbor pathological causes that warrant timely recognition. The Indian context is distinctive as complications develop at lower BMI thresholds, low birth weight and a family history of type 2 diabetes are key predictors of metabolic risk, and metabolically healthy obesity is observed in around 40% of obese children. Management combines structured lifestyle change with judicious pharmacotherapy and bariatric surgery in carefully selected adolescents.",
      "paperTitle": "Adolescent Obesity: A Stepwise Clinical Approach.",
      "doi": "10.1007/s12098-026-06466-5",
      "source": "Indian journal of pediatrics",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Stepwise evaluation distinguishing exogenous from pathological causes, followed by screening for metabolic and mechanical complications, provides cost-effective assessment.",
      "paperTitle": "Adolescent Obesity: A Stepwise Clinical Approach.",
      "doi": "10.1007/s12098-026-06466-5",
      "source": "Indian journal of pediatrics",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Prevention strategies anchored in early-life nutrition, physical activity, sleep, and screen-time regulation remain the cornerstone of long-term control.",
      "paperTitle": "Adolescent Obesity: A Stepwise Clinical Approach.",
      "doi": "10.1007/s12098-026-06466-5",
      "source": "Indian journal of pediatrics",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    }
  ],
  "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659": [
    {
      "text": "Healthcare workers (HCWs) in China are at a high risk of self-reported dry eye symptoms due to occupational exposures, yet population-based epidemiological data on this issue among Chinese HCWs remain scarce. This multicenter cross-sectional study aimed to quantify the prevalence of frequent dry eye symptoms, their diagnostic status, and independent occupational risk factors among Chinese HCWs. From September 2024 to December 2025, 269 active HCWs from 5 tertiary hospitals in Guangdong completed anonymous questionnaires covering demographics, occupational/lifestyle factors, and ocular symptoms. Chinese HCWs face a high burden of frequent dry eye symptoms, which remain severely underdiagnosed.",
      "paperTitle": "Prevalence and occupational risk factors of dry eye symptoms among Chinese healthcare workers: A cross-sectional study and management implications.",
      "doi": "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659",
      "source": "Medicine",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Multivariate logistic regression identified independent risk factors for frequent dry eye symptoms (≥3 times/wk).",
      "paperTitle": "Prevalence and occupational risk factors of dry eye symptoms among Chinese healthcare workers: A cross-sectional study and management implications.",
      "doi": "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659",
      "source": "Medicine",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "The prevalence of frequent dry eye symptoms among participants (mean age = 38.5 ± 8.2 years, 59.9% female, 74.4% were physicians) was 54.3%, but only 6.3% had a formal clinical diagnosis.",
      "paperTitle": "Prevalence and occupational risk factors of dry eye symptoms among Chinese healthcare workers: A cross-sectional study and management implications.",
      "doi": "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659",
      "source": "Medicine",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Daily screen time of 7 hours or more emerged as the strongest modifiable risk factor (odds ratio [OR] = 2.31, P = .005).",
      "paperTitle": "Prevalence and occupational risk factors of dry eye symptoms among Chinese healthcare workers: A cross-sectional study and management implications.",
      "doi": "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659",
      "source": "Medicine",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Other independent risk factors included perceived dry work environment (OR = 1.98), frequent night work (OR = 2.15), and myopia (OR = 1.89; all P < .01).",
      "paperTitle": "Prevalence and occupational risk factors of dry eye symptoms among Chinese healthcare workers: A cross-sectional study and management implications.",
      "doi": "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659",
      "source": "Medicine",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "A striking gap between knowledge, belief, and behavior was observed: 92.2% recognized screen-use risks, yet only 2.6% performed regular eye exercises.",
      "paperTitle": "Prevalence and occupational risk factors of dry eye symptoms among Chinese healthcare workers: A cross-sectional study and management implications.",
      "doi": "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659",
      "source": "Medicine",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "The identified screen-time threshold (≥7 hours/d) is lower than conventional occupational health recommendations and should be considered a modifiable target for intervention.",
      "paperTitle": "Prevalence and occupational risk factors of dry eye symptoms among Chinese healthcare workers: A cross-sectional study and management implications.",
      "doi": "10.1097/MD.0000000000050659",
      "source": "Medicine",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Importantly, current occupational health checkups for HCWs lack ocular surface screening, representing a systemic gap.",
