    {
      "text": "Following previous literature (Uddin & Hasan,2023), we categorized screen time into light digital media user (<2 hours per day), moderate user (2–3 hours per day), and heavy users (\\(\\ge\\)4 hours per day).Physical activityChildren’s physical activity status was measured by asking parents/caregivers the following question: “During the past week, on how many days did this child exercise, play a sport, or participate in physical activity for at least 60 minutes, age 6–17 years?”, with the four answers: 0 days; 1–3 days; 4–6 days; and every day.Short sleep durationChildren’s sleep duration was assessed by asking parents/caregivers the following question: “During the past week, how many hours of sleep did this child get on most weeknights?”, with seven answers: less than 6 hours; 6 hours; 7 hours; 8 hours; 9 hours; 10 hours; and 11 or more hours. A new variable named short sleep duration was generated based on the appropriate sleep duration recommended by the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (Paruthi et al.,2016). Specifically, children aged 6–12 years who slept less than nine hours and children aged 13–17 years who slept less than eight hours were defined as having short sleep duration.Irregular bedtimeChildren’s bedtime regularity was collected by asking parents/caregivers, “How often does this child go to bed at about the same time on weeknights?” The answers were “1 = Always; 2 = Usually; 3 = Sometimes; 4 = Rarely or Never”.Mental health problemsChildren’s mental and developmental health was measured by asking parents/caregivers, “Has a doctor, other health care provider, or educator ever told you that this child has anxiety problems, depression, behavioral and conduct problem, or attention deficit disorder or attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD)?” Parents/caregivers separately answered these questions with “yes/no” answers. This study only included anxiety problems, depression, behavioral/conduct problems, and ADHD as the main outcomes.CovariatesThe following eleven variables were treated as covariates based on previous research findings (Qu et al.,2023; Uddin & Hasan,2023): survey year, child age in years, child gender, ethnicity, insurance type, whether the child was born in the US, primary language spoken at home, parent/caregiver highest education level, household income level, family structure, and family resilience and connection index (FRCI). Logistic regression was conducted to obtain odds ratios (OR) for the association between screen time and children’s mental health problems, respectively.The simple mediating effects of physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime were separately investigated using the “ExactMed” package (Caubet et al.,2023), which is an extension of Baron and Kenny’s method (Baron & Kenny,1986). Screen time was entered as a predictor, and each mental health outcome were separately entered as an outcome variable. The natural direct effect (NDE) and natural indirect effects (NIE) of each mediator for children and adolescent mental health and ADHD outcome were calculated using their respective logistic models with 95% bootstrap confidence intervals (CI) in 1000 replications. In simple mediation analyses, the proportion of mediating effects of each mediator was calculated following the inverse odds weighting (IOW) approach with the formula:\\(\\frac{O{R}^{{NDE}}* ({{OR}}^{{NIE}}-1)}{{{OR}}^{{NDE}}* {{OR}}^{{NIE}}-1}* 100\\,\\)(Nguyen et al.,2015).The parallel mediating effects of physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime were tested using the Structural Equation Modelling (SEM) approach with the “LAVAAN” package (Rosseel,2012). The proportion of each mediator in parallel mediation analyses was calculated using the following formula: the sum of all indirect effects/total effects*100 (Rijnhart et al.,2019). Screen time, physical activity, short sleep duration, bedtime regularity, and child mental health were treated as categorical or binary variables in all mediation models, as appropriate.Given that different age groups have different circadian rhythm development and physical activity levels, multi-group parallel mediation was further conducted to explore how the mediating effects of physical activity and sleep on child mental health differ across different age groups (6–10 years, 11–13 years, and 14–17 years). A statistically significant result (p < 0.05) indicated that the constrained model was significantly different from the unconstrained model and thus the mediation parameters were not equivalent across groups. A total of 31.7% of children had at least 4 hours of daily screen time, and 21.6% of children had daily physical activities\\(\\ge\\)60 minutes. Regarding sleep patterns, 34.4% of children had adequate sleep duration, and 24.7% of children always went to bed at the same time during weekdays (see Table1). The survey-weighted proportion of current anxiety, depression, behavior/conduct problems, and ADHD were 11.1% (95% CI: 10.6–11.6%), 4.9% (95% CI: 4.5–5.3%), 8.5% (95% CI: 7.9–9.0%), and 11.1% (95% CI: 10.5–11.6%), respectively.Table 1 Demographic characteristics of children aged 6–17 years in the NSCH2020-2021 survey (n= 50231).Full size tableAssociation between screen time and mental health and ADHDUnivariate logistic regression found that screen time was positively associated with anxiety, depression, behavior/conduct problems, and ADHD (see Table2, unadjusted models). In adjusted models, the effects of screen time were attenuated but remained statistically significant when covariates were included (see Table2, adjusted models).",
      "paperTitle": "Excessive screen time is associated with mental health problems in US children and adolescents: physical activity and sleep as parallel mediators",
      "doi": "10.1057/s41599-026-06609-1",
      "source": "openalex",
