    {
      "text": "Heavy screen time (≥ 4 hours per day) was positively associated with worse mental health in children and adolescents, with adjusted ORs ranging between 1.21 for ADHD and 1.61 for depression.Table 2 Logistic regression of screen time on children’s mental health.Full size tableSimple mediation modelingAfter controlling for covariates, simple mediation analyses show that physical activity generally exhibits the strongest mediating effects on the relationship between screen time and mental health, followed by irregular bedtime, and short sleep duration (see Table3). For example, 33.2% of the total effect of screen time on adolescent anxiety is mediated through physical activity (OR for natural indirect effect = 1.31, 95% bootstrap CI: 1.25–1.36), while the mediating proportions of short sleep duration and irregular bedtime in this association are approximately 2.77% and 18.2%, respectively.Table 3 Simple mediation analysis of the association between screen time and mental health mediated by physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime, respectively, NSCH 2020–21.Full size tableParallel mediation modelingThe mediation effects of physical activity and sleep on the relationship between screen time and anxiety are shown in Fig.1. The indirect effects of the pathways through physical activity (a1*b1 OR = 1.03, 95% bootstrap CI: 1.03, 1.04), and irregular bedtime (a3*b3 OR = 1.03, 95% bootstrap CI: 1.02, 1.04) are significant mediators in the relation between screen time and anxiety. The pathway through short sleep duration was not significant (a2*b2 OR = 1.00, 95% bootstrap CI: 0.99, 1.01). These results indicate that physical activity and irregular bedtime both partially mediate the relationship between screen time and anxiety.Fig. 1: Parallel mediation of screen time and anxiety by physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime.Full size imageThe three mediators together explained 40.37% of the association between screen time and anxiety. ***p< 0.001.Regarding screen time and depression, behavior/conduct problems, and ADHD, the structural equation modeling consistently showed that the indirect effects in all parallel mediation models were statistically significant (see Figs.2–4). This indicates that physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime were parallel mediators for the associations of screen time with mental health. These mediators jointly explained 25.6% to 50.5% of the association between screen time and depression and ADHD, respectively.Fig. 2: Parallel mediation of screen time and depression by physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime.Full size imageThe three mediators together explained 25.65% of the association between screen time and depression. 3: Parallel mediation of screen time and behavior/conduct problems by physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime.Full size imageThe three mediators together explained 54.63% of the association between screen time and behavior/conduct problems. 4: Parallel mediation of screen time and ADHD by physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime.Full size imageThe three mediators together explained 52.04% of the association between screen time and ADHD. *p< 0.05; **p< 0.001; ***p< 0.001.Multi-group parallel mediation modelingMulti-group mediation analysis reveals that across the three age groups (6–10 years, 11–13 years, and 14–17 years), screen time consistently reduces physical activity and sleep duration, and increases irregular bedtime (Supplementary Figs.2–5, path coefficients for a1 and a2). Both physical activity and irregular bedtime consistently mediate the relationship between screen time and mental health (Supplementary Figs.2–5, path coefficients for a1*b1, and a3*b3). Physical activity generally has a protective effect against poor mental health, whereas irregular bedtime exacerbates these conditions. Compared to physical activity and bedtime, the mediating effects of sleep duration on screen time and mental health were substantially smaller across all age groups and even became non-significant in the 11 - 13 years age group (Supplementary Figs.2–5, path coefficients for a2*b2). The direct effect of screen time on anxiety is significant and positive across all groups, with the total effect most pronounced in the oldest group (14–17 years), followed by the middle (11–13 years) and youngest (6–10 years) groups. However, the direct effects of screen time on depression, behavior/conduct problems, and ADHD were only significant in the oldest age group (14–17 years). Chi-squared difference tests showed that the multi-group models were significantly different from the constrained equal-loading models, indicating that the mediation parameters were different across age groups (see Table4).Table 4 Chi-square difference tests for multi-group mediation versus constrained model.Full size tableDiscussionUsing the latest population-representative US NSCH 2020-21 dataset, this study aimed to explore the mediating role of physical activity, sleep duration, and bedtime regularity in the relationship between screen time and mental health problems among children and adolescents. The findings showed that screen use was positively associated with worse mental health. Physical activity, short sleep duration, and irregular bedtime were parallel mediators in this relationship, in which physical activity had the most profound mediation effects.Direct effects of screen time on mental healthConsistent with prior studies using earlier NSCH datasets (Leung & Torres,2021; Uddin & Hasan,2023), the current study found that screen time use was positively associated with child and adolescent anxiety and depression.",
      "paperTitle": "Excessive screen time is associated with mental health problems in US children and adolescents: physical activity and sleep as parallel mediators",
