      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Measures included daily screen time, proportion of educational versus recreational content, parental mediation practices, and standardized assessments of vocabulary, verbal fluency, grammatical competence, and pragmatic communication.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Digital Content Quality, and Parental Mediation as Predictors of Linguistic and Pragmatic Development: Implications for Pediatric and Preventive Health",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Analyses included descriptive statistics, Pearson correlations, 4 × 3 factorial ANOVAs (age group × screen-time category), and multiple linear regression.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Digital Content Quality, and Parental Mediation as Predictors of Linguistic and Pragmatic Development: Implications for Pediatric and Preventive Health",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Results: Higher levels of screen exposure were consistently associated with lower performance across all linguistic and pragmatic domains (r = −0.19 to −0.28, all p < 0.01).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Digital Content Quality, and Parental Mediation as Predictors of Linguistic and Pragmatic Development: Implications for Pediatric and Preventive Health",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Participants viewing >2 h/day showed significantly weaker outcomes than those with ≤1 h/day, particularly in semantic and phonemic fluency and pragmatic communication (p < 0.001).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Digital Content Quality, and Parental Mediation as Predictors of Linguistic and Pragmatic Development: Implications for Pediatric and Preventive Health",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Educational content correlated positively with linguistic scores, whereas recreational content showed negative associations.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Digital Content Quality, and Parental Mediation as Predictors of Linguistic and Pragmatic Development: Implications for Pediatric and Preventive Health",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Parental mediation emerged as a significant positive predictor.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Digital Content Quality, and Parental Mediation as Predictors of Linguistic and Pragmatic Development: Implications for Pediatric and Preventive Health",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "In the regression model (R2 = 0.42), age (β = 0.47), parental mediation (β = 0.21), and educational content (β = 0.18) predicted better linguistic performance, while screen time (β = −0.29) predicted lower performance.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Digital Content Quality, and Parental Mediation as Predictors of Linguistic and Pragmatic Development: Implications for Pediatric and Preventive Health",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13010157",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    },
    {
