  "10.3389/feduc.2026.1742813": [
    {
      "text": "Digital EducationVolume 11 - 2026 |https://doi.org/10.3389/feduc.2026.1742813Digital tools and screen time management in early childhood education: parents’ and educators’ perspectivesDADalal Aldhilan1SRShahid Rafiq2*AAAyesha Afzal31.Northern Border University, Arar, Saudi Arabia2.Emerson University Multan, Multan, Pakistan3.University of Management and Technology, Lahore, PakistanSee moreArticle metricsView detailsAbstractIntroduction:This study examines the integration of digital tools in early childhood education and explores how parents and educators manage children’s screen time in urban Lahore, Pakistan.Methods:A mixed-methods design was employed. Quantitative data from 30 educators and 30 parents were analyzed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM). Qualitative data from 20 semi-structured interviews were analyzed using thematic analysis.Results:Socio-economic status (SES) (β = 0.42,p= 0.002), perceived benefits of digital tools (PBDT) (β = 0.55,p< 0.001), and frequency of digital tool usage (FDTU) (β = 0.35,p= 0.007) significantly predicted screen time management (STM), explaining 52% of its variance (R2= 0.52). Qualitative findings revealed themes of enhanced engagement, improved learning outcomes, socio-economic disparities, limited teacher training, and structured management strategies such as co-viewing and time limits.Discussion:Digital tools are perceived as valuable educational resources but require structured, developmentally appropriate management. Socio-economic disparities and limited institutional support remain key challenges, underscoring the need for equitable access and targeted professional development.IntroductionThe rapid proliferation of digital technology has transformed various sectors, including education, where digital tools have become an integral part of learning environments. In early childhood education (ECE), digital devices, educational apps, and interactive platforms are increasingly used to support cognitive and social development. This shift has brought significant benefits, such as increased accessibility to learning resources, personalized learning experiences, and the potential for engaging students in innovative ways. However, these advancements also pose challenges, particularly regarding the appropriate amount of screen time for young children and the developmental appropriateness of the content on digital platforms. Globally, the use of digital tools in early childhood classrooms has surged, particularly in response to the COVID-19 pandemic. Many early education settings had to adapt quickly to online or hybrid learning models. During this period, digital tools proved essential for maintaining educational continuity and keeping children engaged while at home. Educational apps, video platforms, and interactive games became central to daily lessons, providing a bridge between the traditional classroom experience and the need for remote learning (Huang et al., 2020). In many parts of the world, including Pakistan, this shift toward digital integration in ECE was accompanied by both opportunities and challenges. According toClemente-Suárez et al. (2024), educational technology in early childhood can facilitate learning, but it must be implemented with caution to avoid cognitive overload, attention issues, or the displacement of critical face-to-face interaction that fosters social and emotional development. Early childhood educators and caregivers now face the challenge of discerning which digital tools are developmentally appropriate and how to balance the use of technology with more traditional learning approaches (American Academy of Pediatrics, 2018).One of the primary concerns with the growing use of digital tools in early childhood education is the impact of screen time on children’s development. In 2018, the American Academy of Pediatrics provided age-specific recommendations regarding media use in early childhood. For children younger than 18–24 months, the American Academy of Pediatrics advises avoiding digital media use, except for video chatting. For children aged 2–5 years, screen time should be limited to approximately 1 h per day of high-quality programming, ideally co-viewed with caregivers to enhance comprehension and engagement. Given that the present study focuses on children aged 3–5 years, these guidelines are particularly relevant in evaluating appropriate screen time management strategies. Concerns regarding early and excessive screen exposure have also been raised in developmental research. Several studies have reported associations between prolonged early screen exposure and developmental screening outcomes, including attention difficulties, language delays, sleep disturbances, and alterations in scores on screening tools such as the Modified Checklist for Autism in Toddlers (M-CHAT-R/F). It is therefore important to approach the integration of digital tools with developmental sensitivity, ensuring that screen use is structured, limited, and complemented by interactive, play-based, and socially mediated learning experiences.The challenge of balancing digital tools with developmentally appropriate practices requires collaboration between educators, parents, and policymakers. Educators play a critical role in selecting and implementing digital tools that align with the developmental needs of young learners. Theories of constructivism and socio-cultural learning, such as those proposed by Piaget and Vygotsky, emphasize the importance of hands-on, interactive learning experiences in early childhood (Berk, 2022). Digital tools, when thoughtfully chosen, can complement these theories by providing interactive simulations or collaborative activities that mimic real-life learning experiences. Moreover, parents and caregivers must be educated about the appropriate use of technology in the home. A 2020 study by Livingstone et al. showed that parents often struggle to manage their children’s screen time due to the ubiquity of digital devices in everyday life.",
      "paperTitle": "Digital tools and screen time management in early childhood education: parents’ and educators’ perspectives",
      "doi": "10.3389/feduc.2026.1742813",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "This underscores the importance of developing guidelines that help families integrate technology in ways that enhance, rather than hinder, developmental outcomes. Technological literacy is becoming a key focus in early childhood education, emphasizing the need to introduce children to digital tools not only as users but also as creators. Early exposure to coding, robotics, and interactive design fosters essential 21st-century skills through play and exploration (Bers, 2022). However, these digital experiences must be balanced with hands-on, social learning to support holistic development. Policymakers, educators, and parents should collaborate to create equitable, developmentally appropriate guidelines for technology integration. The goal is not to replace traditional play-based learning but to enhance it, using digital tools as supportive resources that promote learning while preserving children’s overall growth.This study focuses specifically on early childhood education settings in Lahore, one of Pakistan’s largest metropolitan cities. Lahore presents a relevant context due to rapid digital penetration, increasing smartphone access, and persistent socio-economic disparities that shape educational access. While the findings are context-specific, they may offer insights applicable to similar urban settings in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) experiencing comparable digital transitions.Literature reviewThe use of digital tools in early childhood education (ECE) has sparked a dynamic discourse in educational research, driven by the rapid proliferation of technology and the increasing presence of digital devices in children’s daily lives. From educational apps to interactive games and multimedia platforms, digital tools are now positioned as essential components of the modern classroom. However, their integration raises important questions regarding the developmental appropriateness of these tools, the potential risks of screen time, and the strategies educators and parents can employ to ensure a balanced approach. This literature review explores key themes within the existing research, focusing on the potential benefits and drawbacks of digital tools in ECE, the impact of screen time on children’s development, and the role of developmentally appropriate practices in shaping the effective use of technology in early learning environments.Benefits and challenges of digital tools in early childhood educationDigital tools in early childhood education offer both benefits and challenges. When used appropriately, they can enhance engagement, learning, and skill development by incorporating multimedia features such as sound, animation, and interactivity (Hirsh-Pasek et al., 2015). Research shows that educational apps and games support early literacy and numeracy through interactive storybooks, phonics activities, and puzzles, providing immediate feedback and fostering self-paced learning (Neumann and Neumann, 2017). Such tools also promote cognitive development, creativity, and problem-solving through simulated, hands-on digital experiences (Bers, 2022), while digital storytelling effectively supports language and literacy growth (Rai et al., 2022). Additionally, collaborative platforms encourage peer interaction, benefiting children’s social development, particularly among those from lower-income families who gain access to educational resources otherwise unavailable (Chiong and Shuler, 2010). However, excessive screen use poses risks, with the American Academy of Pediatrics advising strict limits for young children. Overexposure can lead to reduced attention, delayed language, behavioral issues, and less physical or social play (Hermawati et al., 2018;Christakis, 2009). Many apps lack sound educational design, emphasizing memorization over higher-order thinking (Vaala and Takeuchi, 2017;Hirsh-Pasek et al., 2015), while prolonged screen time also contributes to obesity and poor sleep quality (Duch et al., 2013).The role of developmentally appropriate practices in guiding digital tool useThe concept of developmentally appropriate practices (DAP) is central to early childhood education. As defined by the National Association for the Education of Young Children (Levy and Wasmuth, 2025), DAP emphasizes teaching practices that align with children’s developmental stages and individual needs, focusing on hands-on learning, social interaction, and physical activity. The integration of digital tools in early education must follow DAP principles to ensure they enhance rather than replace traditional learning. Rooted in Piaget’s constructivist and Vygotsky’s socio-cultural theories, active and experiential learning remains vital for cognitive and social–emotional growth (Berk, 2022).Veresov et al. (2024)highlights that digital tools should support, not undermine, children’s development, such as using touchscreens alongside physical play and real-world exploration. Parental and educator roles are also key in managing screen time.Livingstone and Blum-Ross (2020)note that parents often struggle to balance children’s digital engagement, underscoring the need for clear guidelines and cooperation with educators. Teachers face similar challenges in selecting tools aligned with curriculum goals. (2014), educators trained in developmentally appropriate technology integration use digital tools more effectively, supporting professional development as essential for quality implementation.Literature stresses the importance of balance in digital integration.Pant (2025)recommends limiting screen time while using digital tools to foster active learning and social interaction. Similarly,Naida et al.",
