      "text": "(2024)argue that children benefit most when digital tools complement traditional approaches, such as creative play, hands-on exploration, and group collaboration, ensuring holistic development across physical, cognitive, and emotional domains.Research questions and hypothesesBased on the integrated theoretical framework drawing from Constructivist Theory, Sociocultural Theory, and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), this study investigates the relationships between socio-economic status (SES), perceived benefits of digital tools (PBDT), frequency of digital tool usage (FDTU), and screen time management (STM).The following research questions guided the quantitative phase of the study:RQ1: What is the relationship between socio-economic status and screen time management in early childhood education settings?RQ2: How do perceived benefits of digital tools influence screen time management strategies?RQ3: Does the frequency of digital tool usage predict structured screen time management?In line with these research questions and prior literature, the following hypotheses were developed:H1: Socio-economic status (SES) positively influences screen time management (STM).H2: Perceived benefits of digital tools (PBDT) positively influence screen time management (STM).H3: Frequency of digital tool usage (FDTU) positively influences screen time management (STM).These hypotheses were tested using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM).Theoretical frameworkThe integration of digital tools in early childhood education can be understood through a framework combining Piaget’s Constructivist Theory, Vygotsky’s Sociocultural Theory, and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). Piaget’s Constructivist Theory emphasizes that children build knowledge through active engagement and hands-on experiences. During the preoperational stage (ages 2–7), interactive and exploratory digital tools, such as educational games or storytelling apps, can enhance learning if balanced with physical play to support cognitive growth. Vygotsky’s Sociocultural Theory highlights the importance of social interaction in learning, particularly through the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD), where guidance from adults or peers enhances understanding. Digital tools can serve as mediators in this process, promoting collaboration when supported by effective adult scaffolding. The Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) explains educators’ and parents’ willingness to adopt digital tools, based on Perceived Usefulness (PU) and Perceived Ease of Use (PEOU). Tools that are valuable for learning and simple are more likely to be embraced in early education. This integrated framework underscores the need for developmentally appropriate practices (DAP), ensuring digital tools promote active, social, and meaningful learning experiences that foster children’s cognitive, social, and emotional development in balanced, age-appropriate ways, seeFigure 1.Figure 1Theoretical framework.Research designThis study employed a mixed-methods research design to investigate the use of digital tools in early childhood education. The quantitative component involved administering structured surveys to 30 early childhood educators and 30 parents of children aged 3–5 years during parent-teacher meetings. These surveys assessed the frequency and types of digital tools used in classrooms, as well as educators’ and parents’ perceptions of their impacts on children’s cognitive and social development. In the qualitative phase, 10 educators and 10 parents were selected from the quantitative sample for in-depth, semi-structured interviews. These interviews took place face-to-face during the following Parents-Teacher Meeting, allowing for nuanced discussions about their experiences with digital tool integration and their strategies for managing screen time, as shown inTable 1.Table 1Participant groupSampling methodSample sizeDemographic characteristicsQuantitative (Educators)Purposive sampling30Early childhood educators from public and private institutions in Lahore; experience teaching children aged 3–5 years.Quantitative (Parents)Convenience sampling30Parents of children aged 3–5 years enrolled in early childhood education institutions; diverse socio-economic backgrounds; surveys completed on Parents-Teacher Meeting Day.Qualitative (Educators)Purposive sampling10Selected educators from the quantitative sample; varied teaching experiences and perspectives on digital tools.Qualitative (Parents)Purposive sampling10Selected parents from the quantitative sample; diverse insights on the integration of digital tools and screen time management; interviews conducted during the next Parents-Teacher Meeting.Sampling frame.This mixed-methods design facilitated a detailed exploration of both statistical trends and personal insights, providing a comprehensive understanding of how digital tools are implemented in early childhood settings and the factors that influence their effectiveness.Data collection methodsData was collected through the following methods:Quantitative phase: survey instrumentThe quantitative phase used a structured survey to gather data from educators and parents on digital tool use and screen time management in early childhood education. The survey collected demographic details (e.g., age, education, experience) and examined the types and frequency of digital tool use through a Likert scale. It also measured children’s average daily screen time and assessed the perceived effectiveness of management strategies (time limits, co-viewing, educational content) on a 1–5 scale. Participants rated the impact of screen time on cognitive and social development using agreement scales. A pilot test with five educators and five parents ensured clarity and reliability before full distribution.Construct operationalization and measurementAll latent constructs included in the PLS-SEM model were measured using multi-item Likert-type scales. The items were developed based on prior literature and adapted to the early childhood education context in Lahore, Pakistan.",
