      "text": "A pilot test with five educators and five parents was conducted to ensure clarity and contextual appropriateness.Socio-economic status (SES)Socio-economic status was modeled as a reflective construct composed of three indicators: parental education level, household income category, and occupational status. Higher scores indicated higher socio-economic status.Perceived benefits of digital tools (PBDT)Perceived benefits of digital tools were measured using four items adapted from prior research on educational technology acceptance (Hirsh-Pasek et al., 2020;Blackwell et al., 2014). The scale assessed participants’ perceptions of the educational value of digital tools in supporting engagement, learning outcomes, and motivation.Sample item:“Digital tools enhance children’s understanding of academic concepts.”“Interactive apps improve children’s engagement during learning activities.”Higher scores indicated stronger perceived benefits.Frequency of digital tool usage (FDTU)Frequency of digital tool usage was measured using three items assessing how often digital tools were used in educational settings or at home. Participants indicated usage frequency on a 5-point scale (1 = Never, 5 = Daily).Sample item:“Digital tools are integrated into learning activities on a regular basis.”Higher scores reflected more frequent use of digital tools.Screen time management (STM)Screen time management strategies were measured using five items assessing structured approaches to regulating children’s digital exposure. Items evaluated practices such as setting time limits, co-viewing, monitoring content, and using digital tools selectively for educational purposes.Sample items:“I set clear limits on the amount of time children spend using digital devices.”“Digital content is selected carefully to ensure it is developmentally appropriate.”“Children’s screen time is supervised or co-viewed.”Higher scores indicated more structured and effective screen time management strategies.Measurement of daily screen timeDaily screen time was assessed using a self-report item in which parents estimated their child’s average daily exposure to digital devices over the previous 7 days. Responses were recorded in hours and minutes and categorized for descriptive analysis. This variable was used for descriptive and comparative purposes but was not included as a latent construct in the structural model, seeTable 2.Table 2ConstructNo. of itemsScale typeExample itemSource/adaptationSES3 indicatorsReflective (3 indicators)Education, income, occupationAdapted from educational SES indicesPBDT4 items5-point Likert“Digital tools enhance understanding.”Hirsh-Pasek et al. (2020),Blackwell et al. (2014)FDTU3 items5-point Likert“Digital tools are used regularly.”Adapted from technology usage studiesSTM5 items5-point Likert“I set clear screen time limits.”Adapted from digital parenting researchSummary of measurement scales.Qualitative phase: interview guideThe qualitative phase employed a semi-structured interview guide for in-depth interviews with selected educators and parents, balancing essential topics with flexibility. Participants shared their experiences with specific digital tools, teaching practices, and their perceived effectiveness. Discussions included the benefits and challenges of integrating these tools, such as concerns about screen time, technology access, and training needs. Additionally, participants provided insights on strategies for balancing screen time with developmentally appropriate practices and the impact of digital tools on children’s learning and social development. Interviews were conversational, encouraging elaboration and personal anecdotes, and were audio-recorded with consent for accurate transcription and analysis.Interview procedureSemi-structured interviews were conducted face-to-face during the subsequent Parent-Teacher Meeting. Each interview lasted approximately 30–45 min and was audio-recorded with participants’ consent. Interviews were conducted in a conversational format to encourage in-depth responses and allow participants to elaborate on their experiences. Field notes were also taken to capture contextual observations.All interviews were transcribed verbatim prior to analysis. Transcripts were reviewed multiple times to ensure accuracy and familiarity with the data.Qualitative data analysis procedureQualitative data were analyzed using thematic analysis following the six-phase framework proposed byBraun and Clarke (2006). This approach was selected due to its flexibility and suitability for identifying patterns across participants’ perspectives.The analysis followed these steps:Familiarization: Transcripts were read multiple times to gain an in-depth understanding of participants’ experiences.Initial coding: Meaningful segments of text were systematically coded using a data-driven (inductive) approach.Searching for themes: Related codes were grouped into preliminary themes reflecting shared ideas.Reviewing themes: Themes were reviewed and refined to ensure internal coherence and distinction between categories.Defining and naming themes: Final themes were clearly defined and aligned with the research objectives.Reporting: Representative quotations were selected to illustrate each theme.To enhance credibility, coding was conducted independently by the primary researcher and reviewed by a second researcher familiar with qualitative methods. This collaborative approach strengthened analytic rigor and reduced subjective bias.Data saturation was considered achieved when no new themes emerged from subsequent interviews. By the twentieth interview, thematic patterns were repetitive and stable, indicating sufficient depth of data collection.Data analysisData analysis was conducted separately for the quantitative and qualitative components of the study. Quantitative data collected through surveys was analyzed using statistical software (SPSS). Descriptive statistics summarized demographic information and digital tool usage patterns, while inferential statistics, such ast-tests and ANOVA, examined differences in perceptions of digital tools and screen time management among various demographic groups (e.g., educators vs. parents).",
