      "text": "Qualitative data from interviews were analyzed to identify key themes and patterns regarding the integration of digital tools and screen time management, synthesizing participants’ insights to understand their experiences and perspectives.Ethical considerationsThis study adhered to internationally recognized ethical research standards to safeguard participants’ rights, privacy, and wellbeing. Ethical approval was obtained from the Office of Research, Innovation, and Commercialization of the University of Management and Technology Lahore, which reviewed and approved the study protocol in accordance with the principles outlined in the Declaration of Helsinki.Although the primary institutional affiliation of the researchers was based outside Pakistan, formal permission to conduct the study was also obtained from the participating early childhood education institutions in Lahore. School administrators reviewed the study objectives, procedures, and consent protocols before granting access. Data collection complied with local institutional requirements and educational guidelines applicable within the Lahore context. Informed consent was obtained from all adult participants (educators and parents) before data collection. Participants were informed of the study’s purpose, confidentiality measures, and their right to withdraw at any time without penalty. The study did not involve direct interaction with children, and no child-identifiable data was collected.The results inTable 3show that a large majority of educators are female (85%) and most hold a bachelor’s degree (93%). A significant portion have between 6 and 10 years of teaching experience (40%), indicating a fairly experienced group.Table 3Demographic variableFrequencyPercentageGenderFemale2585%Male515%Educational backgroundBachelor’s degree2893%Master’s degree27%Years of experience1–5 years1033%6–10 years1240%11+ years827%Demographics of educators.The results inTable 4indicate that most parents are female (60%) and hold at least a bachelor’s degree (63%). The majority belong to the middle socio-economic group (50%), reflecting a relatively educated and stable economic background.Table 4Demographic variableFrequencyPercentageGenderFemale1860%Male1240%Educational backgroundHigh school517%Bachelor’s degree1963%Master’s degree620%Socio-economic statusLow1033%Middle1550%High517%Demographics of parents.Digital tools usageThe analysis included the types of digital tools used by educators and the frequency of use.Table 5shows that educational apps and online videos are the most frequently used digital tools among educators, while learning management systems are used less often. These findings suggest a preference for accessible and interactive digital resources in early childhood classrooms.Table 5Type of digital toolFrequencyPercentageEducational apps2583%Online videos (YouTube)2067%Learning management systems517%E-books1550%Digital tools used by educators.The results inTable 6indicate that a majority of educators integrate digital tools frequently in early childhood classrooms, with 40% using them daily and 35% using them two to three times a week. This suggests that digital tools have become a regular component of teaching practices in early childhood education.Table 6Frequency of usePercentageDaily40%2–3 times a week35%Weekly25%Frequency of use.TheT-test inTable 7revealed a significant difference (t(58) = 2.75,p< 0.01) between educators’ (M= 4.3) and parents’ (M= 3.8) perceptions of the benefits of digital tools, indicating that educators perceived greater benefits from digital tools than parents.Table 7GroupMean scoreSDt-valuep-valueEducators4.30.62.750.009Parents3.80.7T-test: comparing educators’ and parents’ perceptions of digital tool benefits.Table 8of one-way ANOVA revealed statistically significant differences in screen time management strategies across socio-economic status groups,F(2, 57) = 15.20,p< 0.001. This suggests that socio-economic status accounts for approximately 34% of the variance in screen time management scores.Table 8SourceSum of SquaresdfMean squareFp-valueη2Between groups18.7529.3815.20<0.0010.34Within groups36.00570.63Total54.7559ANOVA: differences in screen time management strategies by socio-economic status.Post-hoc comparisons using Tukey’s HSD test inTable 9indicated that the high SES group (M= 4.00, SD = 0.50) demonstrated significantly stronger screen time management strategies than both the middle SES group (M= 3.20, SD = 0.60,p< 0.01) and the low SES group (M= 2.50, SD = 0.80,p< 0.001). Additionally, the middle SES group scored significantly higher than the low SES group (p< 0.05).Table 9ComparisonMean differencep-valueHigh vs. middle0.80<0.01High vs. low1.50<0.001Middle vs. low0.70<0.05Tukey HSD post-hoc comparisons.These findings indicate that socio-economic status plays a substantial role in shaping structured approaches to managing children’s digital exposure.Measurement model assessmentPrior to evaluating the structural model, the measurement model was assessed to ensure reliability and validity of the constructs, following the guidelines ofHair et al. The analysis was conducted using SmartPLS 4 with 5,000 bootstrap resamples to assess path significance and indicator stability.Internal consistency reliabilityInternal consistency was evaluated using Cronbach’s alpha and Composite Reliability (CR). All constructs demonstrated acceptable reliability, with Cronbach’s alpha values exceeding the recommended threshold of 0.70. Composite Reliability values also exceeded 0.70, indicating satisfactory internal consistency.Convergent validityConvergent validity was assessed using outer loadings and Average Variance Extracted (AVE). All indicator loadings exceeded the recommended threshold of 0.70, and AVE values were above 0.50 for all constructs, indicating that each construct explained more than 50% of the variance in its indicators.Discriminant validityDiscriminant validity was assessed using the Heterotrait–Monotrait ratio (HTMT). All HTMT values were below the conservative threshold of 0.85, confirming adequate discriminant validity between constructs.Multicollinearity assessmentVariance Inflation Factor (VIF) values were examined to assess multicollinearity.",
