      "text": "All VIF values were below 3.0, indicating that collinearity was not a concern.The results of the measurement model evaluation are presented inTables 10–12.Table 10ConstructCronbach’sαComposite reliability (CR)AVESES0.780.840.64PBDT0.860.900.69FDTU0.810.880.71STM0.880.910.68Reliability and convergent validity.Table 11ConstructItemOuter loadingPBDTPBDT10.82PBDTPBDT20.85PBDTPBDT30.79PBDTPBDT40.83FDTUFDTU10.84FDTUFDTU20.87FDTUFDTU30.81STMSTM10.85STMSTM20.88STMSTM30.79STMSTM40.83STMSTM50.81Indicator loadings.Table 12ConstructSESPBDTFDTUSTMSES–0.620.580.71PBDT–0.660.74FDTU–0.69STM–Discriminant validity (HTMT).Structural model evaluationThe structural model was assessed by examining path coefficients (β), coefficient of determination (R2), effect sizes (f2), predictive relevance (Q2), and model fit using SRMR. Bootstrapping with 5,000 subsamples was performed to determine the statistical significance of path coefficients. The predictive relevance of the model was confirmed through blindfolding procedures, with aQ2value of 0.318 for Screen Time Management (STM), indicating satisfactory predictive capability.Results of the PLS-SEM analysisPath coefficients,R2values, and outer loadings were analyzed to determine the relationships between the variables. The PLS algorithm provided path coefficients (β-values) for the strength of the relationships between the latent variables, while bootstrapping was used to assess the statistical significance of the paths. The model explained a substantial amount of variance in Screen Time Management (STM), with anR2value of 0.52, meaning 52% of the variance in screen time management strategies was accounted for by socio-economic status, perceived benefits of digital tools, and the frequency of digital tool usage.The analysis inTable 13showed that Perceived Benefits of Digital Tools (PBDT) had the strongest influence on screen time management (β= 0.55,p< 0.001), indicating that the more parents and teachers recognized the benefits of digital tools, the more likely they were to implement effective screen time strategies. Socio-economic status (SES) also had a significant positive effect on screen time management (β= 0.42,p= 0.002), suggesting that families from higher socioeconomic backgrounds were more proactive in managing screen time. Lastly, the Frequency of Digital Tool Usage (FDTU) had a moderate yet significant effect (β= 0.35,p= 0.007), indicating that higher digital tool usage was associated with more structured screen time management, seeFigure 2.Table 13PathPath coefficient (β)t-valuep-valueSES → STM0.423.150.002PBDT → STM0.554.500.000FDTU → STM0.352.800.007PLS-SEM analysis.Figure 2Structural model with standardized path coefficients and indicator loadings.Measurement invariance and multi-group analysisGiven that the sample consisted of two distinct respondent groups (educators and parents), measurement invariance was assessed prior to pooling the data for structural modeling. The Measurement Invariance of Composite Models (MICOM) procedure was conducted in SmartPLS 4 using 5,000 permutations, following the guidelines ofHenseler et al. The analysis confirmed configural invariance, as identical measurement models, data treatment procedures, and algorithm settings were applied across groups. Compositional invariance was established for all constructs, as correlation values between group-specific composite scores did not significantly differ from 1 (p> 0.05). Equality of means and variances was partially supported, indicating partial measurement invariance. Establishing partial measurement invariance allows for meaningful comparison and pooling of groups in PLS-SEM analysis. To further examine potential group differences, a Multi-Group Analysis (PLS-MGA) was conducted. The results indicated that the differences in structural path coefficients between educators and parents were not statistically significant (p> 0.05), supporting the appropriateness of analyzing the pooled sample. These findings justify the use of a combined sample for structural model evaluation (Tables 14,15).Table 14ConstructCompositional invariance (p-value)Equality of meansEquality of variancesSES0.42SupportedSupportedPBDT0.31SupportedSupportedFDTU0.28SupportedSupportedSTM0.35SupportedSupportedMICOM and multi-group analysis results.Table 15PathDifference (educators vs parents)p-valueSES → STM0.070.41PBDT → STM0.090.33FDTU → STM0.050.52Multi-group analysis (path differences).Justification for PLS-SEM and sample size considerationsPartial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) was selected for this study due to its suitability for exploratory research, prediction-oriented modeling, and its robustness with relatively small sample sizes (Hair et al., 2019,2021). Unlike covariance-based SEM (CB-SEM), which requires larger samples and normally distributed data, PLS-SEM is appropriate when the primary objective is theory development and prediction rather than strict theory confirmation. Given the modest sample size (N= 60), PLS-SEM was considered methodologically appropriate. In the present model, the dependent variable (Screen Time Management) had three predictor paths (SES, PBDT, FDTU), indicating a minimum requirement of 30 cases. Additionally, PLS-SEM is particularly suitable in studies involving formative or composite indicators and heterogeneous samples, such as the combination of educators and parents in this research. Future research with larger samples is recommended to validate and extend these findings.Table 16highlights that both parents and educators see digital tools as effective in boosting engagement, improving learning outcomes, and increasing student motivation. However, they also point to challenges like limited access, over-reliance on screens, and insufficient teacher training. To address these issues, they adopt strategies such as setting screen time limits, encouraging co-viewing, and using digital tools as rewards to balance learning and technology use, as shown inFigure 3.Table 16ThemeSubthemeSourceRepresentative quotationsPerceived benefits of digital toolsEnhanced engagementParent“When my child uses interactive apps, I can see how engaged he is, and it holds his attention longer than traditional methods.”Educator“I’ve noticed a significant increase in participation during lessons involving digital tools like educational apps and videos.”Improved learning outcomesParent“After using learning apps, my child talks more about the lessons at home.",
