      "text": "I can see the improvement in how she understands things.”Educator“Children seem to grasp concepts better when we integrate videos or apps. They remember details more clearly after using digital tools.”Motivation for learningParent“My daughter is more eager to study when she knows she can use her tablet as part of the learning process.”Educator“Digital tools make students more enthusiastic about learning, especially when they can interact with the material directly.”Challenges in digital tool integrationLimited access to technologyParent“Many families in my neighborhood cannot afford digital devices, which makes it hard for our children to benefit from such tools.”Educator“Some students do not have access to technology at home, which creates a divide between those who can continue learning digitally and those who cannot.”Over-reliance on screensParent“I worry that too much screen time will make my child dependent on it and reduce her interest in other activities like outdoor play.”Educator“We must balance digital learning with physical activities. Children tend to rely too much on screens if not monitored properly.”Lack of training for teachersParent“Sometimes I feel teachers aren’t equipped enough to use these digital tools effectively in the classroom.”Educator“I’ve not received enough training on how to use digital tools for teaching. We need more workshops on this.”Screen time management strategiesSetting screen time limitsParent“We try to limit screen time to an hour a day. It’s been helpful in balancing my child’s activities and not overloading her.”Educator“In class, I make sure screen time does not exceed more than 15 min for a session, so the children stay focused on other tasks too.”Co-viewing and guided useParent“I make sure I sit with my child when he’s using learning apps. That way I know he’s using it for the right things.”Educator“We encourage parents to co-view digital content with their children to make sure they are engaging with educational material appropriately.”Using digital tools as a rewardParent“We’ve started using digital apps as a reward for completing homework. It helps to limit screen use while also motivating learning.”Educator“I sometimes allow students to use tablets as a reward for completing their tasks. This helps motivate them and keeps usage controlled.”Thematic analysis.Figure 3Thematic analysis mind-map.ThemesExtracted through thematic analysisThree overarching themes emerged: perceived benefits of digital tools, challenges in digital integration, and screen time management strategies. Both parents and educators reported increased engagement, improved learning outcomes, and heightened motivation when digital tools were integrated appropriately. However, socio-economic disparities limited access for some families, and concerns about over-reliance on screens were frequently expressed. Educators also highlighted insufficient training and institutional support. To manage screen exposure, participants described strategies such as setting time limits, co-viewing digital content, and using digital tools selectively as rewards. These findings reinforce the need for balanced, developmentally appropriate technology integration.FindingsBoth parents and educators recognized that digital tools like educational apps and videos enhance children’s engagement and learning outcomes, increasing motivation both at home and in classrooms. However, access to technology varied by socio-economic status, with lower-income families facing barriers. Concerns about excessive screen time led to boundaries, typically around 1 h daily, with parents using co-viewing and educators promoting guided use. Educators’ limited training in technology integration also reduced effectiveness. Higher socio-economic status correlated with better access, structured screen time management (β= 0.42,p= 0.002), and greater awareness of digital tools’ educational value (β= 0.55,p< 0.001). Structured screen time was linked to frequent digital tool use (β= 0.35,p= 0.007), and group differences were significant (F= 15.20,p< 0.001). Overall, while digital tools positively impact learning, addressing inequities in access, training, and screen time management remains essential.DiscussionThe findings of this study contribute to the growing body of literature on the integration of digital tools in early childhood education and highlight the multifaceted nature of screen time management. In line with recent research, this study found that digital tools are increasingly seen as valuable resources for enhancing learning, fostering engagement, and supporting early cognitive development (Dong et al., 2020). Educators and parents alike perceive these tools as essential in keeping children engaged, especially in a post-pandemic era where digital literacy has become more critical than ever (Matsumoto, 2025). One of the key findings from both the quantitative and qualitative data is the strong relationship between socio-economic status (SES) and screen time management. Families from higher SES backgrounds demonstrated more structured approaches to managing screen time, supported by greater access to technology and educational resources. This aligns with prior studies that emphasized socio-economic disparities in technology access and usage (Livingstone et al., 2019). It reinforces the need for equitable access to digital tools in early childhood settings to prevent widening the digital divide.The perceived benefits of digital tools also emerged as a significant predictor of how screen time is managed. This is consistent with findings fromHirsh-Pasek et al. (2020), who argue that the perceived educational value of digital tools greatly influences parental attitudes toward screen time.",
