    {
      "text": "The findings from both quantitative and qualitative analyses underscore that while digital tools are valued for their educational benefits, their effective use is contingent on access, socio-economic factors, and proper training for educators.Socio-economic status emerged as a key determinant of screen time management, with families from higher socio-economic backgrounds demonstrating more structured and proactive approaches. Perceived benefits of digital tools strongly influenced screen time strategies, suggesting that both awareness and understanding of the advantages of technology are critical for its effective integration in educational settings. Balancing the benefits of digital tools with concerns over screen time requires thoughtful and context-sensitive strategies from both parents and educators. Addressing these challenges through improved access to technology and targeted training for educators can maximize the positive impact of digital tools on young children’s development. Future research should expand this inquiry to rural regions and other provinces to determine whether the observed patterns hold across diverse socio-cultural and economic contexts within Pakistan.StatementsData availability statementThe raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation.Ethics statementThe studies involving humans were approved by Deanship of Scientific Research Ref No: NBU-DSR/IRB/2024/128. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study.Author contributionsDA: Visualization, Resources, Writing – review & editing. AA: Conceptualization, Investigation, Writing – review & editing, Supervision.FundingThe author(s) declared that financial support was not received for this work and/or its publication.Conflict of interestThe author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.Generative AI statementThe author(s) declared that Generative AI was not used in the creation of this manuscript.Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside figures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artificial intelligence and reasonable efforts have been made to ensure accuracy, including review by the authors wherever possible. If you identify any issues, please contact us.Publisher’s noteAll claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their affiliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher.References1American Academy of Pediatrics(2018).Children and media tips from the American Academy of Pediatrics.Itasca:American Academy of Pediatrics.Google Scholar2BerkL. E.(2022).Development through the Lifespan.7thEdn.Thousand Oaks:Sage Publications.Google Scholar3BersM. (2022).Coding as a Playground: Programming and Computational Thinking in the Early Childhood Classroom.2ndEdn.Cambridge:Routledge.Google Scholar4BlackwellC. K.LauricellaA. (2014).Factors influencing digital technology use in early childhood education.Comput. doi:10.1016/j.compedu.2014.04.013CrossRefGoogle Scholar5BraunV.ClarkeV. (2006).Using thematic analysis in psychology.Qualitative research in psychology,3,77–101. doi:10.1191/1478088706qp063oaCrossRefGoogle Scholar6ChiongC.ShulerC.(2010). “Learning: is there an app for that,” inInvestigations of young Children’s Usage and Learning with mobile Devices and Apps. (2009).The effects of infant media usage: what do we know and what should we learn?.Acta paediatrica,98,8–16. doi:10.1111/j.1651-2227.2008.01027CrossRefGoogle Scholar8Clemente-SuárezV. (2024).Digital device usage and childhood cognitive development: exploring effects on cognitive abiliti