  "10.2147/jmdh.s582301": [
    {
      "text": "Screen time matters: The impact of gadget use duration and intensity o | JMDH | Dove Medical PressJavascript is currently disabled in your browser. Several features of this site will not function whilst javascript is disabled.Skip to main contentAdvanced searchOpen access to scientific and medical researchBack toJournals»Journal of Multidisciplinary Healthcare»Volume 19ListenOriginal ResearchScreen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional StudyFulltextMetricsGet PermissionCite this articleAuthorsNurhidayah I,Salsabilla T,Hastuti EAReceived17 November 2025Accepted for publication11 February 2026Published12 March 2026 Volume 2026:19 582301DOIhttps://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S582301Checked for plagiarismYesReview bySingle anonymous peer reviewPeer reviewer comments4Editor who approved publication:Professor Charles V PollackDownload Article[PDF]Ikeu Nurhidayah,1Thalia Salsabilla,2Emma Aprilia Hastuti21Pediatric Nursing Department, Faculty of Nursing, Universitas Padjadjaran, Sumedang, Indonesia;2Mental Health and Psychiatric Nursing, Dharma Husada Institute of Health, Bandung, IndonesiaCorrespondence: Ikeu Nurhidayah, Pediatric Nursing Department, Faculty of Nursing, Universitas Padjadjaran, Sumedang, 45363, Indonesia, Fax +62 22 7795596, Email[email protected]Background:The rapid increase in screen time among preschool children has become a growing public health concern, as digital gadgets are increasingly integrated into children’s daily lives. Excessive screen exposure may reduce opportunities for direct social interaction and experiential learning, thereby increasing the risk of adverse personal–social development outcomes. Personal–social development is a critical component of early childhood growth, enabling children to interact effectively with peers and their environment. This study aimed to examine the association between the duration and intensity of screen time and the risk of suboptimal personal–social development among preschool-aged children.Methods:A quantitative cross-sectional study was conducted involving 98 parents of preschool children recruited from a single preschool setting in Bandung, Indonesia. Data were collected using structured, parent-reported questionnaires assessing screen time duration and frequency, and the risk of suboptimal personal–social development, based on a previously applied personal–social development instrument in the Indonesian context. Personal–social development scores were categorized into optimal (lower risk) and suboptimal (higher risk) based on an established cut-off. Screen time variables were classified according to risk-based thresholds. Descriptive statistics, chi-square tests, and multivariable analyses were performed to examine associations while adjusting for child age and parental education.Results:The findings showed a statistically significant association between screen time duration and the risk of suboptimal personal–social development (p < 0.001), as well as between the intensity of gadget use and the risk of suboptimal personal–social development (p < 0.001). Children with prolonged daily screen time (≥ 120 minutes) had a higher risk of suboptimal personal–social development (53.1%) compared with those with limited screen exposure. Similarly, children who used gadgets frequently (> 3 times per day) were at greater risk of suboptimal personal–social development than those with moderate or minimal use.Conclusion:Prolonged duration and high intensity of screen time are significantly associated with an increased risk of suboptimal personal–social development among preschool children. These findings highlight the importance of parental monitoring and the establishment of appropriate limits on screen use during early childhood. Health professionals, educators, and policymakers should work collaboratively to promote balanced digital habits and screen time awareness as part of early childhood development strategies in the digital era. This study contributes to achieving SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being) and SDG 4 (Quality Education), particularly Target 4.2 on early childhood development.Plain Language Summary:Provides new empirical evidence from Indonesia, a developing country context with rapidly increasing digital exposure among children.Longer screen time and more frequent gadget use were associated with a higher risk of suboptimal personal–social development among preschool children.Highlights the importance of parental guidance and structured screen-time management to support healthy digital device use among children.Keywords:screen time, personal–social development, preschool children, early childhood, digital media use, SDG 3, SDG 4BackgroundScreen time has become an integral part of young children’s daily lives worldwide, including in low- and middle-income countries such as Indonesia.1,2Screen time refers to the amount of time spent using screen-based devices, including smartphones, tablets, televisions, and computers, for activities such as watching videos, playing games, or using applications.3Although digital devices may offer educational and entertainment value, excessive or poorly regulated screen exposure during early childhood has raised concerns regarding developmental outcomes.2Early childhood represents a critical developmental period during which foundational personal–social skills are established.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "During the preschool years, children develop independence, emotional regulation, empathy, cooperation, and the capacity for social interactions.4These early competencies are critical, as behavioural and social tendencies formed in this period often persist and shape later developmental trajectories.5The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that preschool children aged 2–5 years should engage in no more than one hour of screen time per day, emphasizing that less is better for optimal health and development.3,6However, international evidence indicates that only 25–35% of preschool-aged children meet these recommendations.7Although some scholars have questioned the strength of the evidence underlying existing guidelines,8there is growing consensus that further empirical research is needed, particularly focusing on specific developmental domains and contextual factors.Previous studies have linked higher screen time with behavioural difficulties, emotional dysregulation, and sleep problems among preschool-aged children.8,9Evidence also indicates that poorer personal–social development may be associated with longer screen time through caregivers’ permissive attitudes and difficulties in limit setting.2The increasing presence of screen-based devices in children’s daily lives has therefore raised growing concern about their potential developmental risks. In Indonesia, the proportion of five-year-old children using screen-based devices increased from 38% in 2011 to 80% in 2015,10indicating a rapid shift toward digital use from an early age. While educational applications may enhance creativity and support cognitive development when used appropriately, excessive or unsupervised screen time has been associated with lower levels of physical activity, reduced attention, and fewer opportunities for meaningful face-to-face social interaction.8,11Personal–social development is fundamental to school readiness and long-term mental well-being.12In contemporary literature, personal–social development is understood as a component of personal, social, and emotional development (PSED).13PSED is one of the three prime areas within the Early Years Foundation Stage (EYFS).13According to the EYFS framework, children’s personal, social, and emotional development is essential for leading healthy and happy lives and serves as a fundamental foundation for their cognitive development.13The development of personal and social skills enables children to benefit optimally from learning experiences within their environments.14Through this developmental process, children gain a better understanding of themselves and demonstrate increasing self-regulation.15Other aspects of personal and social development necessary for effective functioning in everyday life, including the school context, include children’s interactions with others and their social problem-solving abilities.13,16Delays in this domain may impair communication, behavioural regulation, and emotional functioning, thereby limiting children’s participation in learning and social activities.17Globally, developmental disparities in early childhood remain a critical concern. The World Health Organization (WHO) estimates that approximately 250 million children in low- and middle-income countries, equivalent to 43% of the population, do not achieve age-appropriate developmental milestones due to poverty, inadequate stimulation, and limited access to early interventions.6In Indonesia, data from the 2018 Indonesian Basic Health Research Survey indicated that 69.9% of children achieved expected personal–social developmental milestones. Although this represents an improvement, the proportion remains lower than that reported in several neighbouring countries, including Vietnam (91.2%), Kazakhstan (82.1%), and Thailand (79.4%).18Recent studies suggest that excessive screen time and exposure to digital technology may increase the risk of suboptimal personal–social development in children.2,8,11,19Specifically, screen time intensity, defined as the frequency of daily screen-based device use, may influence developmental outcomes alongside total duration.20Muppalla et al suggested that increased screen time may reduce the quantity and quality of interactions between children and their caregivers, which, in turn, may influence language development and contribute to suboptimal social and emotional development in early childhood.11Unregulated exposure to digital media may reduce opportunities for direct social interaction and hands-on learning experiences.2In Indonesia, digital engagement has become embedded in everyday family life, with approximately 79.7% of the population actively using social media, exceeding rates in neighbouring countries such as the Philippines (78%), Malaysia (72%), and China (67%).21,22The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) recommends that children aged 2–5 years engage in no more than one hour of high-quality screen time per day and only with active parental supervision.23,24However, in many low- and middle-income settings, including Indonesia, these recommendations are challenging to implement due to evolving family routines and limited parental awareness.2The COVID-19 pandemic further intensified children’s reliance on digital devices, particularly among preschool-aged children.20,25The widespread adoption of remote and hybrid learning blurred the distinction between educational and recreational screen use, making it more difficult for caregivers to monitor duration and content.20,25Many young children reportedly spend more than three hours per day on digital devices, often for entertainment rather than educational purposes.20Most existing evidence on screen time and personal–social development has been generated in high-income countries.2Recent studies have shown that longer screen time among preschool children is associated with behavioural difficulties and poorer personal–social development, often mediated by caregivers’ permissive attitudes and challenges in setting limits.2In contrast, evidence from low- and middle-income countries such as Indonesia remains limited, despite a larger population of young children and rapidly increasing access to digital devices.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
