    {
      "text": "This highlights the need for context-specific research examining the risk of personal–social developmental outcomes associated with screen time among preschool children in Indonesia.Therefore, this study aimed to examine the risk of suboptimal personal–social developmental outcomes associated with the duration and intensity of screen time among preschool children in Indonesia. Specifically, this study investigated whether prolonged daily screen exposure and higher frequency of screen use increase the likelihood of personal–social developmental risk among preschool-aged children in Bandung, Indonesia. Based on previous empirical evidence, this study hypothesized that preschool children with longer daily screen time and higher screen use intensity have a greater risk of suboptimal personal–social development than those with shorter daily screen time and lower screen use intensity.MethodsStudy DesignThis study employed a quantitative cross-sectional design to examine the association between screen time exposure (duration and intensity) and personal–social developmental outcomes among preschool-aged children. Data on all variables were collected at a single point in time, allowing for the assessment of patterns and risk associations without inferring causality.26Setting and Study PeriodThe study was conducted at a preschool in Bandung, Indonesia, between July 2022 and February 2023. The selected preschool serves children from diverse urban families with varying socio-economic backgrounds, providing a relevant context for examining screen time exposure and personal–social development within an Indonesian urban setting.Participants and SamplingThe study population consisted of parents or primary caregivers of preschool children aged 3–6 years enrolled at the selected preschool. Participants were recruited using purposive sampling based on the following inclusion criteria:Being the parent or primary caregiver of a child aged 3–6 years,Having a child who used screen-based devices, andProviding written informed consent.Parents of children with diagnosed developmental disorders were excluded to minimize potential confounding effects on personal–social development outcomes.Data Collection ProceduresData were collected using structured, self-administered questionnaires completed by parents during school hours. Prior to data collection, the researcher explained the study objectives and procedures to all participants. Written informed consent was obtained, and participation was voluntary, with the option to withdraw at any time without penalty. Conducting data collection in person allowed parents to ask questions and seek clarification, thereby enhancing data accuracy and completeness.Instruments and MeasuresScreen Time DurationScreen time duration was assessed using a researcher-developed questionnaire informed by the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) screen time recommendations. Parents were asked:On a typical day, how long does your child spend using screen-based devices (such as smartphones, tablets, televisions, or computers)?Responses were reported in minutes per day and categorized into two groups: <120 minutes/day and ≥120 minutes/day. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends limiting screen time to no more than one hour per day for preschool children.27The present study applied a 120-minute cut-off as a risk-based threshold, consistent with previous studies indicating that screen exposure exceeding two hours per day is associated with increased developmental and behavioural risks among young children.1,19,28This categorization also reflects commonly observed screen use patterns in the local context and contextual feasibility in Indonesia, as supported by prior national studies reporting higher average screen exposure among preschool children.Screen Time IntensityScreen time intensity was operationalized as the frequency of daily screen-based device use. Parents were asked: “How many times per day does your child usually use screen-based devices?”. Frequent use is defined as device use occurring more than three times per day. This approach aligns with previous literature emphasizing the importance of habitual and repeated screen exposure in understanding developmental and behavioural outcomes in young children.1,20Screen-Based Application Type (Contextual Information)As part of the questionnaire, caregivers were also asked to report the types of screen-based applications their children most frequently use, such as video streaming platforms, games, or educational applications. This information was collected to provide a contextual understanding of children’s screen exposure patterns. Application type and content quality were not treated as independent variables and were not included in the statistical analysis.Personal-Social DevelopmentPersonal–social development was assessed using an 18-item parent-reported questionnaire adapted from a previous study.10The questionnaire has previously been applied and validated within the Indonesian population.10The instrument was adapted from earlier research conducted among Indonesian preschool children, ensuring its cultural relevance and contextual appropriateness for assessing personal–social development in local settings.10The questionnaire assesses multiple dimensions of personal–social development, including self-centred behaviour, power-related behaviours (defined as behaviours reflecting dominance, control over peers, or insistence on authority), defiance, conflict, cooperation, aggression, and teasing or mocking.Each item was rated on a four-point Likert scale (1 = strongly agree to 4 = strongly disagree), yielding total scores ranging from 18 to 72. Higher scores indicate greater risk of personal–social developmental problems. Scores ≥44.10 were classified as suboptimal personal–social development (higher risk), while scores <44.10 were classified as optimal personal–social development (lower risk).10This classification reflects relative developmental risk within a screening context.",
