      "text": "Reliability testing produced a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.768, indicating that the questionnaire is reliable.10The questionnaire underwent pilot testing with a small sample of parents (10) to ensure clarity, comprehension, and cultural appropriateness.Participants CharacteristicCaregiver and child characteristics, including caregiver age, education level, employment status, and child age and sex, were collected using a structured questionnaire. These variables were included as covariates in the analysis to account for potential confounding effects.Data AnalysisData were coded, cleaned, and analyzed using SPSS version 26. Data were coded, cleaned, and analyzed using descriptive and inferential statistics. Descriptive analyses were used to summarize participants’ characteristics and key study variables, including screen time exposure and personal–social development. Chi-square tests were conducted to examine the associations between screen time variables (duration and intensity) and categories of personal–social development, with p<0.05 set as the criterion for statistical significance. Binary regression analyses were performed to identify factors associated with personal–social development scores, adjusting for child age, sex, and parental education.Ethical ConsiderationsThis study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Dharma Husada Institute of Health Sciences (Approval No. 174/KEPK/SDHB/B/VIII/2022). Written informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to data collection. Participants were informed about the study’s purpose, assured of confidentiality, and advised that participation was voluntary and that they could withdraw at any time without consequences.ResultsParticipant CharacteristicsA total of 98 parents of preschool-aged children participated in this study. Detailed demographic characteristics are presented inTable 1.Table 1Demographic Characteristics of Respondents (n = 98)Screen Time Duration and IntensityMore than half of the children (52; 53.1%) were classified as having high screen time duration (≥120 minutes per day), while 46 children (46.9%) had low screen time duration (<120 minutes per day). Regarding screen time intensity, 43 children (43.9%) were categorized as frequent users (more than three times per day), 31 children (31.6%) as moderate users (two to three times per day), and 24 children (24.5%) as rare users (once per day or less). Smartphones were the most commonly used screen-based devices (85.7%), and most children shared devices with parents or siblings (65.3%). In terms of developmental outcomes, 46 children (46.9%) demonstrated optimal personal–social development (lower risk), while 52 children (53.1%) were categorized as having suboptimal personal–social development (higher risk) based on the established cut-off score (Table 2).Table 2Distribution of Screen Time Characteristics and Personal–Social Development Risk Outcomes Among Preschool Children (n = 98)Association Between Screen Time Duration and Personal–Social Development RiskAn assessment of personal–social development revealed a strong association with daily screen time duration (Table 3). All children in the low-duration group (<120 minutes/day) demonstrated optimal personal–social development (46/46; 100%). In contrast, all children in the high-duration group (≥120 minutes/day) were classified as having suboptimal personal–social development (52/52; 100%). Statistical analysis confirmed a highly significant association between screen time duration and risk of personal–social development (p < 0.001). Children with greater daily screen exposure were more likely to be categorized as having suboptimal personal–social development than those with lower screen time (Table 3).Table 3Association Between Screen Time Exposure and Personal–Social Development Risk (n=98)Association Between Screen Time Intensity and Personal–Social Development RiskA similar pattern was observed when screen time intensity was examined (Table 3). Among children with rare screen use (≤1 time/day), the majority demonstrated optimal personal–social development (21/24; 87.5%). In the moderate-use group (2–3 times/day), 11 of 31 children (35.5%) were classified as having suboptimal development. Among frequent users (more than three times per day), a large proportion of children (38/43; 88.4%) demonstrated suboptimal personal–social development. The association between screen time intensity and personal–social development risk was statistically significant (p < 0.001). Higher frequency of daily screen exposure was associated with an increased likelihood of suboptimal personal–social development (Table 3).Multivariable AnalysisBinary logistic regression was performed to examine whether screen time duration and frequency were associated with the risk of suboptimal personal–social development, adjusting for potential confounders (Table 4). After controlling for child age and parental education, both screen time duration and screen time frequency remained significantly associated with a higher risk of suboptimal personal–social development.Table 4Multivariate Analysis (n = 98)Children with high screen time (≥120 minutes/day) had nearly three times the odds of suboptimal personal–social development compared with those with lower screen time. Similarly, children with frequent screen use (>3 times/day) had significantly higher odds of suboptimal personal–social outcomes. In contrast, child age and parental education were not significantly associated with personal–social development risk in the adjusted model (p > 0.05). The overall model indicated that screen time duration and frequency were important correlates of personal–social development risk in this population.DiscussionThe findings of this study demonstrated a significant association between both duration and frequency of screen exposure and personal–social development among preschool children. Children with longer daily screen time and higher frequency of screen use were more likely to exhibit suboptimal personal–social development.",
