      "text": "These findings are consistent with previous studies suggesting that excessive screen exposure in early childhood is associated with challenges in social interaction, emotional regulation, and adaptive behaviors.11,19,20From a developmental perspective, early childhood represents a critical period during which personal–social skills are influenced through repeated, reciprocal interactions with caregivers, peers, and the surrounding environment.2More than half of the children in this study (53.1%) were exposed to screen time exceeding 120 minutes per day, and a similar proportion showed suboptimal personal–social development. This pattern aligns with existing recommendations, including those from the American Academy of Pediatrics, which advise limiting screen time for young children.27While the present study cannot establish causality, prolonged screen exposure may reduce opportunities for children to engage in direct social interaction with caregivers and peers, which are essential for developing cooperation, communication, and social understanding.23Screen time may function as a contextual factor that alters children’s daily interactional environments, thereby influencing developmental outcomes.11It is important to note that the perfect separation observed in screen time duration (<120 vs ≥120 min/day) may not fully reflect reality. While children whose parents reported <120 minutes/day appeared to all have optimal personal–social development, this may be influenced by potential parental reporting bias, the dichotomous scoring system used for the personal–social development instrument, or the relatively small, single-site sample. Future studies could incorporate observational methods or multiple informants to improve measurement accuracy and reduce the risk of reporting bias.Previous literature suggests that excessive screen use may reduce opportunities for meaningful parent–child interaction, particularly when digital devices are used to occupy or soothe children.24,29Face-to-face interaction, shared play, and verbal engagement have been shown to support emotional development and social adjustment in early childhood. A reduction in these interactions may help explain the association observed between higher screen time and poorer personal–social outcomes in this study, although caregiver behaviors were not directly measured.Although the present study did not formally assess the type or quality of digital content as analytical variables, descriptive observations indicated that children commonly accessed entertainment-based applications such as YouTube and gaming platforms. Prior studies have reported that frequent exposure to fast-paced, entertainment-focused, or aggressive content may be associated with increased irritability, impulsivity, and reduced self-regulation in young children.30Prior research suggests that the type of media content matters for children’s development. Passive, fast-paced digital content, such as watching videos without interaction, tends to offer limited opportunities for active engagement and language stimulation compared with interactive, child-directed media, which can support verbal expression and cognitive processing when combined with parental involvement.31,32Recreational and highly stimulating digital content has been associated with reduced self-regulation and increased irritability or impulsivity in young children, possibly because it promotes sustained attention to screens rather than real-world social interactions that are essential for social development.31,32These findings indicate that the quality and interactivity of screen content should be considered when assessing the developmental impacts of digital media exposure.Early childhood social development relies heavily on real-world interaction, including the ability to interpret facial expressions, tone of voice, and body language.23,33Excessive engagement with digital devices may limit these experiences and, over time, be associated with reduced opportunities to practice empathy and cooperative behaviors. While parents in this study reported supervising their children’s gadget use, supervision was largely passive and did not necessarily involve active engagement or guidance. This observation, although not quantitatively measured, is consistent with previous research indicating that passive supervision alone may be insufficient to mitigate the potential risks of excessive screen exposure.27Caregivers are encouraged to implement specific strategies rather than general limit-setting.34These include co-viewing digital content with children to guide understanding and interaction, establishing screen-free zones in the home to promote uninterrupted play and family interaction, and scheduling regular playdates or outdoor activities to encourage peer socialization.11Such structured approaches can help mitigate potential developmental risks associated with prolonged or high-frequency screen exposure while fostering personal–social skills.11Furthermore, community nurses and healthcare professionals can play a pivotal role by integrating screen-time counseling into well-child visits. During routine check-ups, nurses can assess screen use patterns, provide personalized guidance to caregivers, and educate families on the importance of balanced digital media use and active interaction with children to promote optimal personal–social development.Overall, this study’s findings highlight the relevance of screen time duration and intensity as correlates of personal–social development in preschool children. These results contribute to the growing body of evidence highlighting the need for balanced, developmentally appropriate use of digital media during early childhood. These findings are also relevant to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being) and SDG 4 (Quality Education).35By identifying excessive screen exposure as a modifiable factor associated with personal–social development, this study supports SDG Target 3.4 on promoting mental health and well-being, as well as SDG Target 4.2 on ensuring quality early childhood development and school readiness.",
