      "text": "Addressing screen time in early childhood may therefore contribute to broader efforts to enhance child health and developmental outcomes.Implications for Practice and RecommendationsThe findings of this study suggest several practical implications for parents, educators, and healthcare professionals. Rather than offering prescriptive guidelines, this study highlights the importance of awareness regarding the potential risks associated with prolonged and frequent screen time use in preschool children. Parents may benefit from guidance on monitoring screen time, encouraging alternative play activities, and engaging more actively with children during media use.Educators and early childhood practitioners can support social development by promoting peer interaction, cooperative play, and non-digital learning activities within preschool settings. From a healthcare perspective, nurses and health workers may consider incorporating brief discussions about screen use into routine child health visits, particularly to raise awareness and support informed decision-making by families. Future research using longitudinal or mixed-methods designs is needed to understand the directionality of these associations better and to explore how contextual factors, such as caregiver involvement and content type, influence child development outcomes.ConclusionsThis study demonstrates that both the duration and frequency of screen exposure are associated with a higher risk of suboptimal personal–social development among preschool children. Prolonged duration combined with frequent screen use may further increase this risk. Although parental supervision was not directly assessed, these findings may have implications for how screen time is managed in early childhood settings. These findings support integrating screen-time education into early childhood health programs, with nurses, educators, and caregivers promoting structured digital routines, co-viewing, screen-free zones, scheduled playdates, and alternative social and physical activities. Future research should explore caregiving strategies and interventions that mitigate the developmental risks associated with excessive or unstructured screen use.Strengths and Limitations of the StudyThis study provides valuable evidence from an Indonesian preschool population. A key strength is the examination of both screen time duration and frequency, allowing a more comprehensive assessment of children’s screen exposure patterns. The personal–social development instrument used has been previously applied in Indonesian populations and demonstrated acceptable internal consistency, although further refinement and validation are still needed. We recommend that future studies employ more comprehensive, standardized developmental assessments and multi-method approaches to further validate and expand upon these findings. Screen time data were based on parental self-report, which may be subject to recall bias. The extreme separation observed in screen time duration (<120 vs ≥120 min/day) may reflect parental reporting bias, the rigid cut-off score for personal–social development, or the small, single-site sample. Future research should include larger, multi-site samples and triangulate parental reports with objective measures to provide more robust estimates of the relationship between screen time and early childhood development. In addition, caregiving practices and parental supervision were not directly measured, and information on application type and content was limited to description.AbbreviationsAAP, American Academy of Pediatrics; CI, Confidence Interval; AOR, Adjusted Odds Ratio; SE, Standard Error.Data Sharing StatementThe datasets used and analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.Ethics Approval and Consent to ParticipateThis study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Dharma Husada Institute of Health Sciences with approval number 174/KEPK/SDHB/B/VIII/2022. The participants provided written informed consent before data collection. All participants were informed about the study’s purpose, assured of confidentiality, and provided written consent prior to participation. Participation was voluntary, and respondents could withdraw at any time without consequence.AcknowledgmentsThe authors would like to express their sincere gratitude to the preschool administrators, teachers, and parents who participated in this study. This publication charge is funded by Universitas Padjadjaran through the Indonesian Endowment Fund for Education (LPDP) on behalf of the Indonesian Ministry of Higher Education, Science and Technology.Author ContributionsAll authors made a significant contribution to the work reported, whether that is in the conception, study design, execution, acquisition of data, analysis and interpretation, or in all these areas; took part in drafting, revising or critically reviewing the article; gave final approval of the version to be published; have agreed on the journal to which the article has been submitted; and agree to be accountable for all aspects of the work.FundingThis study was funded by Universitas Padjadjaran.DisclosureThe authors declare that they have no competing interests for this work.References1.McArthur BA, Volkova V, Tomopoulos S, Madigan S. Global prevalence of meeting screen time guidelines among children 5 years and younger.JAMA Pediatr. 2022;176(4):373. doi:10.1001/jamapediatrics.2021.63862.Axelsson EL, Metse A, Nanthakumar S, et al. Screen time and behaviour in preschool-aged children: relationships with caregiver perceptions.Australas J Early Child. 2025:1–12. 10.1177/18369391251376711.3.World Health Organization (WHO). 2015;58(1):1–4. doi:10.1159/0003755155.Junge C, Valkenburg PM, Deković M, Branje S. The building blocks of social competence: contributions of the Consortium of Individual Development.Dev Cogn Neurosci. 2020;45(September). doi:10.1016/j.dcn.2020.1008616.UNICEF. Investing in early childhood development essential to helping more children and communities thrive, new lancet series finds.",
