      "text": "Based on classifications proposed by Putriana et al,20frequency was categorized as rare (once per day), moderate (two to three times per day), and frequent (more than three times per day).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "The term “frequent” was chosen instead of absolute descriptors (eg, “always”) to better reflect variability in daily use patterns.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 11
    },
    {
      "text": "Scores were categorized based on the mean cut-off from the original instrument.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 12
    },
    {
      "text": "The validity test showed r-values ranging from 0.515 to 1.000, indicating that all 18 items are valid.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 13
    },
    {
      "text": "All study procedures were conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki (2013) and relevant national guidelines and regulations.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 14
    },
    {
      "text": "Most respondents were mothers (87.8%), the majority of whom were homemakers, while fathers were predominantly private employees (38.8%) or self-employed (41.8%).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 15
    },
    {
      "text": "Most parents had completed senior high or vocational education (mothers: 68.4%; fathers: 63.3%).The children’s ages ranged from 3 to 6 years, with a mean age of 4.96 years (SD = 0.96).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 16
    },
    {
      "text": "The largest proportion of children was five years old (38.8%), followed by six years old (33.7%).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 17
    },
    {
      "text": "YouTube was the most frequently accessed application (57.1%), followed by TikTok and online games.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 18
    },
    {
      "text": "Gaming-related videos constituted the most commonly viewed content.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 19
    },
    {
      "text": "However, several limitations should be acknowledged.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Matters: The Impact of Screen Time on the Risk of Personal–Social Development Outcomes Among Preschool Children in Indonesia: A Cross-Sectional Study",
      "doi": "10.2147/jmdh.s582301",
