  "10.2196/83228": [
    {
      "text": "Objective: The first aim was to conduct a systematic review of articles examining screen time and mental health outcomes among children and adolescents during the COVID-19 pandemic from 2020 to 2023. The second aim was to determine the combined effect sizes for the associations of screen time and depression and/or anxiety among children and adolescents during the COVID-19 pandemic from 2020 to 2023 and whether gender or age influenced outcomes. Study inclusion criteria included children ages 0 to <18 years, the effects of screen time on children during the COVID-19 pandemic, screen time and depression and/or anxiety, articles written in English, and articles, including quantitative and qualitative studies, published between 2020 and 2023. Conclusions: During the COVID-19 pandemic, children and adolescents with higher levels of screen time had increased depression and/or anxiety.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Background: In response to the COVID-19 pandemic, governments around the world enforced stay-at-home orders and social distancing guidelines that amplified the use of screen time among pediatric populations.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Excessive screen time may negatively impact mental health by increasing depression and anxiety.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Methods: Bibliographic databases were searched including MEDLINE (Ovid), Embase (Elsevier), Cochrane Library (Wiley), CINAHL Complete (EBSCO), and PsycINFO (EBSCO).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "There were a total of 6462 nonduplicate studies that were screened.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "A total of 452 articles underwent full-text review with 23 articles meeting criteria for final article extraction.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Results: A total of 23 studies totaling 29,581 children and adolescents were included in the study.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Results showed that most studies reported a positive association between screen time and depression and/or anxiety (r=0.175, 95% CI 0.124-0.226, P<.001 and r=0.157, 95% CI 0.0994-0.214, P<.001, respectively) during COVID-19.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "Meta-regression revealed that screen time measured in problematic use of electronic devices had a 0.15 higher correlation with anxiety compared to screen time measured in duration of electronic device use.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    },
    {
      "text": "Findings suggest the need for ongoing parent, professional, and self-monitoring of youth screen behaviors and habits as well as activities that promote social connectedness during global or national health emergencies.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time, Child Depression, and Anxiety During the COVID-19 Pandemic: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis",
      "doi": "10.2196/83228",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 9
    }
  ],
  "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420": [
    {
      "text": "The objectives of this study were to examine (1) screen time patterns and (2) the associations between screen time patterns and cognitive development in preschool children. Screen time patterns (type: show/movie/video, electronic game, communication; content: parent-defined educational or entertainment, researcher-defined educational or entertainment; device: TV, mobile [tablet/smartphone]; context: co-use with an adult, evening) were parent reported using a 2-week online daily diary. Some patterns of screen time appeared to be more detrimental to cognitive development than others.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "INTRODUCTION: Major gaps limit the comprehensive understanding of the associations between screen time and cognitive development in preschool years.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "METHODS: This cross-sectional study used baseline data, collected between October 2022 and December 2023 and analyzed in 2025, from the TECHnology and Development in Early Childhood project.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Participants were 359 preschool children (aged 3, 3.5, or 4 years ± 2 weeks) and their parents from Western Canada.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Cognitive development (language, response inhibition, working memory, self-control) was assessed through 4 established short games administered by researchers during a recorded virtual meeting.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Regression models adjusted for covariates were conducted.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "RESULTS: Based on 4,774 days of data, children had an average of 77 min/day of screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "The most prevalent type and content of screen time across devices were show/movie/video (69 min/day) and entertainment (parent defined; 55 min/day) screen time, respectively.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "Higher levels of show/movie/video, mobile device, and parent-defined entertainment screen time (10 min/day) were consistently associated with less advanced executive function scores (response inhibition: B= -0.45 to -0.62; working memory: B= -0.02 to -0.03; self-control: B= -0.86, -1.05).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    },
    {
      "text": "For language, engaging in versus not engaging in communication screen time (B=1.71; p=0.037) and co-use with an adult (B=1.88; p=0.039) was associated with more advanced scores, only when nontypical and typical days were included.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 9
    },
    {
      "text": "CONCLUSIONS: Entertainment shows/movies/videos were common among preschool children.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "Findings can help inform more nuanced screen time recommendations and interventions that aim to minimize harm and maximize the benefits of this societal reality.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time Patterns and Cognitive Development Among Preschool Children",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.amepre.2026.108420",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 11
