      "text": "This study examined associations between screen time and physical activity with cardiovascular risk factors in a large, diverse sample of early adolescents. Adjusted linear and Poisson regression models examined joint associations of screen time and step count with cardiovascular health outcomes. Both high screen time (adjusted risk ratio [ARR] 1.88, 95% CI 1.42 to 2.56) and low step count (ARR 2.35, 95% CI 1.88 to 2.94) were associated with increased risk of hypertensive-range blood pressure. CONCLUSIONS: Higher screen time and lower physical activity were associated with poorer cardiovascular health, supporting guidelines and early interventions promoting increased physical activity and reduced screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Higher recreational screen time and lower step count are associated with higher cardiovascular disease risk in early adolescence",
      "doi": "10.1186/s12889-026-26756-z",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
