  "10.1111/apa.70524": [
    {
      "text": "AIM: This study tested the associations between screen time and anxiety and depression, while accounting for physical activity, sleep and socioeconomic background. Screen time and symptoms of anxiety and depression were measured using the short 25-item child-reported Revised Children's Anxiety and Depression Scale, along with physical activity, sleep, sex and age. The scores for anxiety and depression nearly doubled when the daily screen time increased from approximately two hours to seven hours or more. CONCLUSION: Screen time that exceeded about two hours a day was associated with higher levels of anxiety and depression among Swedish children who took part in this population-based study.",
      "paperTitle": "Survey Showed That Limiting Daily Screen Time Could Help to Avoid Mental Health Issues in Children Aged 8–17Years",
      "doi": "10.1111/apa.70524",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "METHODS: We analysed repeated cross-sectional survey data from the Swedish population-based Generation Pep Study, which was collected in 2021-2022 and comprised children aged 8-17 years.",
      "paperTitle": "Survey Showed That Limiting Daily Screen Time Could Help to Avoid Mental Health Issues in Children Aged 8–17Years",
      "doi": "10.1111/apa.70524",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "The parents provided some of the basic details and helped younger children under 12 complete the questionnaire, as required.",
      "paperTitle": "Survey Showed That Limiting Daily Screen Time Could Help to Avoid Mental Health Issues in Children Aged 8–17Years",
      "doi": "10.1111/apa.70524",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "RESULTS: The 4002 children (50.9% boys) had a mean age of 12.2 ± 2.7 years.",
      "paperTitle": "Survey Showed That Limiting Daily Screen Time Could Help to Avoid Mental Health Issues in Children Aged 8–17Years",
      "doi": "10.1111/apa.70524",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "However, statistically significant marginal effects were only observed when screen time exceeded two hours per day.",
      "paperTitle": "Survey Showed That Limiting Daily Screen Time Could Help to Avoid Mental Health Issues in Children Aged 8–17Years",
      "doi": "10.1111/apa.70524",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "The results remained significant after adjusting for the plausible displacement effect of physical activity, sleep and socioeconomic status.",
      "paperTitle": "Survey Showed That Limiting Daily Screen Time Could Help to Avoid Mental Health Issues in Children Aged 8–17Years",
      "doi": "10.1111/apa.70524",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    }
  ],
  "10.3390/children13040446": [
    {
      "text": "For this reason, the interest of the education community in proposing programs for reducing screen time has grown, which could be of interest for families and professionals in early childhood development and care for children adhering to a healthy lifestyle. For this reason, the objective of this study is to compile programs including families that have tried to reduce preschool-aged children’s time in front of screens. Conclusions: The results reveal that programs based on training parents in addition to performing games with children have positive effects for reducing screen time in children up to six years old, even in a specific population.",
      "paperTitle": "The Effects of Family-Based Programs on Preschool Children’s Screen Time: A Systematic Review",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13040446",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Background: The impact of screen time is having serious adverse effects on people’s lives.",
      "paperTitle": "The Effects of Family-Based Programs on Preschool Children’s Screen Time: A Systematic Review",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13040446",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Unfortunately, early childhood is the most vulnerable stage in the lifespan, and most children are using television, computers, parents’ and mothers’ mobile phones, or tablets, for longer than recommended.",
      "paperTitle": "The Effects of Family-Based Programs on Preschool Children’s Screen Time: A Systematic Review",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13040446",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Method: The search strategy is designed based on the PICOS framework.",
      "paperTitle": "The Effects of Family-Based Programs on Preschool Children’s Screen Time: A Systematic Review",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13040446",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "A review was conducted in three databases (PubMed, Web of Science, and ProQuest Central) on 11 October 2024, following the PRISMA guidelines.",
      "paperTitle": "The Effects of Family-Based Programs on Preschool Children’s Screen Time: A Systematic Review",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13040446",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "The systematic review is registered in PROSPERO.",
      "paperTitle": "The Effects of Family-Based Programs on Preschool Children’s Screen Time: A Systematic Review",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13040446",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Results: A total of 287 articles were initially found, and 15 met all inclusion criteria.",
      "paperTitle": "The Effects of Family-Based Programs on Preschool Children’s Screen Time: A Systematic Review",
      "doi": "10.3390/children13040446",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    }
  ],
  "10.1007/s10916-026-02338-9": [
    {
      "text": "We tested the hypothesis that an intervention to reduce work-related screen time during a weekend off reduces stress in healthcare workers. A three-pronged educational intervention targeting work-related screen time among healthcare workers doubled stress reduction during a non-work weekend. Stress reduction in the intervention group was mediated by reduced screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Reducing Work-Related Screen-Time in Healthcare Workers During Leisure Time (REDUCE SCREEN) – A Randomized Controlled Trial",
      "doi": "10.1007/s10916-026-02338-9",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "The ubiquitous availability of work-related applications on personal devices makes healthcare workers prone to working during leisure time.",
      "paperTitle": "Reducing Work-Related Screen-Time in Healthcare Workers During Leisure Time (REDUCE SCREEN) – A Randomized Controlled Trial",
      "doi": "10.1007/s10916-026-02338-9",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
