      "chunk_index_in_doc": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "This analytical strategy enabled the examination of indirect effects using bias-corrected bootstrapping procedures with 5000 resamples and a 95% confidence interval. All models were run with bootstrapped confidence intervals, and variables were standardized to allow for meaningful comparison of effect sizes.Prior to conducting regression analyses, the dataset was carefully screened to ensure that all statistical assumptions were met. The application of Holm–Bonferroni adjustments ensures that reported results are unlikely to be artifacts of multiple comparisons, while the consistent reporting of effect sizes and confidence intervals highlights the substantive importance of the associations.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 11
    },
    {
      "text": "Data were analyzed using Pearson correlations, multiple regression, and mediation/moderation models (Hayes PROCESS v4.0). In order to test the hypothesized mediation effects, the study employed the PROCESS macro (Model 4) developed by27, integrated within SPSS. A mediation analysis was conducted using PROCESS macro (v4.0, Model 4) with 5,000 bootstrapped samples and 95% confidence intervals. & Hayes, A. F. Asymptotic and resampling strategies for assessing and comparing indirect effects in multiple mediator models.Behav. Open.8(3), e250122–e250122 (2025).ArticlePubMedPubMed CentralGoogle ScholarHayes, A. F.Introduction To mediation, moderation, and Conditional Process Analysis: A regression-based Approach(The Guilford Press, 2013).Download referencesAcknowledgementsThe authors wish to express their sincere gratitude to all tertiary students who participated in this study.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 12
    },
    {
      "text": "A total of 970 students from public Colleges of Education across Ghana were selected through stratified random sampling. While self-selection and institutional communication patterns may have introduced some response bias, the extensive coverage of institutions, regions, and student demographics supports reasonable generalizability to the wider population of Ghanaian College of Education students. Given the geographically dispersed nature of the target population students from public Colleges of Education across all sixteen regions of Ghana an online format was selected as both an ethical and pragmatic method of reaching a large and diverse sample efficiently. Descriptive and inferential analyses were used to evaluate both direct and conditional effects, drawing on a sample of 970 tertiary students from Ghanaian Colleges of Education. Lastly, although the sample included respondents from multiple regions, the findings may not generalize to all tertiary institutions in Ghana, particularly private or specialized universities that operate under different digital and academic conditions.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 13
    },
    {
      "text": "Download PDFAbstractIn light of rising concerns over the psychological and academic consequences of excessive digital engagement among College of Education students, this study investigates the complex interplay between social media use, screen time, mental health, and academic outcomes. This study investigated the complex interplay between social media use, screen time, depression, mental health, assessment integrity, and internal assessment scores among tertiary students in Ghana using a cross-sectional correlational design. Findings revealed that increased social media use and screen time were significantly associated with poorer mental health outcomes (R2= 0.31,p< .001), with late-night screen usage (β = − 0.29,p< .001) and emotional investment in social media (β = − 0.30,p< .001) emerging as the strongest negative predictors of mental well-being. Depression significantly mediated the relationship between social media use and academic performance (indirect effect β = − 0.14, 95% CI [− 0.21, − 0.07],p< .001), accounting for 27% of the variance in depression and 23% in academic scores. The direct path from social media use to academic performance also remained significant (β = − 0.17,p< .001), while the total effect was stronger (β = − 0.31,p< .001), indicating partial mediation. Furthermore, depression significantly predicted increased assessment dishonesty (β = 0.29,p< .001), and this relationship was amplified among students with high non-academic screen time exposure. Correlation analysis showed significant negative associations between screen time and assessment scores (r= − .28 to − 0.32,p< .01), and between depression and academic integrity (r= .25 to 0.33,p< .01). The study concludes that digital behaviors, particularly excessive and emotionally driven screen use, detrimentally affect students’ mental health and ethical academic conduct. Psychological distress, especially depression, is statistically consistent with partial mediation, digital engagement to reduced academic performance and integrity. These findings highlight the need for institutional policies that promote balanced digital habits, mental health support, and integrity-centered assessment reforms in Ghanaian tertiary institutions.Similar content being viewed by othersSocial media use and associated mental health indicators among University students: a cross-sectional studyArticleOpen access19 March 2025The effects of social media addiction on depression and anxiety among university students: The mediating role of family environmentArticleOpen access24 March 2026Self-control and social support in the link between academic pressure anxiety depression and social media addiction in college studentsArticleOpen access05 February 2026Explore related subjectsDiscover the latest articles and news in related subjects.PsychologyRisk factorsInternet Use Disorders and Mental Health Among AdolescentsIntroductionIn recent years, the digital lives of tertiary students across the globe have expanded dramatically, with smartphones, laptops, and ubiquitous internet access becoming essential tools for both academic engagement and social interaction1,2,3,4. As digital technologies continue to transform higher education, the implications for students’ mental well-being, ethical behavior, and academic outcomes have come under increasing scrutiny. Globally, research indicates that young adults particularly College of Education students are among the most intensive users of digital media, spending several hours daily online for both academic and non-academic purposes6–9. In many high-income countries such as the United States, United Kingdom, and South Korea, studies have reported rising concerns about the mental and cognitive toll of prolonged digital engagement, particularly as it relates to distractions during study, screen-induced fatigue, and reduced physical and social activity3,6,8. Similarly, low- and middle-income countries are witnessing a digital surge, with increasing smartphone penetration and mobile data accessibility enabling young people to engage with platforms such as WhatsApp, Instagram, TikTok, Telegram, and Facebook at unprecedented rates2,10.Ghana, like many other nations in sub-Saharan Africa, has seen a rapid increase in both internet connectivity and social media use, particularly among tertiary students. According to the National Communications Authority, over 65% of youth aged 18–30 in Ghana actively use at least one social media platform daily. Social media use has been associated with both beneficial and detrimental impacts on the lives of College of Education students worldwide. Yet, a growing body of research warns against maladaptive patterns of social media use particularly compulsive checking, fear of missing out (FoMO), and passive scrolling which have been linked to increased symptoms of anxiety, depression, loneliness, and diminished academic motivation2,5,14. Tertiary students often report spending more than 3–5 h daily on social media platforms, not only for communication but also for entertainment and emotional coping (Boateng & Amankwah, 2021). This pattern of heavy digital engagement tends to displace time for academic reading, disrupt sleep cycles, and undermine self-regulation and concentration during study. Similar concerns are mirrored in other parts of the Global South, such as Nigeria, India, and Brazil, where College of Education students exhibit comparable digital habits, leading scholars to describe the phenomenon as a “global digital paradox” where tools meant to aid learning increasingly contribute to cognitive and emotional overload11,15,16.Screen time, which encompasses not only social media but also video streaming, gaming, online shopping, and general internet browsing, adds an additional layer of complexity to the discourse on student well-being and academic performance17,18,19,20.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 14
