      "text": "Unlike earlier technological eras, today’s screen-based activities are not compartmentalized they occur across overlapping domains of education, leisure, and social interaction. Globally, studies have consistently demonstrated that excessive screen exposure is associated with a range of adverse psychological and behavioral outcomes, including increased stress, diminished attention span, sleep disturbances, and emotional dysregulation6,21,22,23,24,25. The phenomenon of “digital multitasking” where students simultaneously switch between academic and entertainment platforms has been shown to impair cognitive processing and memory retention, ultimately affecting academic habits and outcomes5,21. In low- and middle-income countries, where digital infrastructure is rapidly evolving, screen time is often seen as a marker of technological progress and educational access. However, without adequate digital literacy and self-regulation strategies, screen time can quickly become excessive and counterproductive. In Ghana15, note that while online learning and digital tools have enhanced tertiary education, students increasingly report difficulties in balancing academic and non-academic screen use, leading to a decline in face-to-face communication skills, reduced study engagement, and heightened psychological strain. With remote and hybrid learning models becoming more normalized worldwide, the boundaries between study time and screen-based leisure have grown increasingly blurred. This blending of academic and recreational screen time has created what some researchers refer to as a “digitally saturated learning environment,” where students struggle to maintain focus, motivation, and healthy online habits26,27,28. Consequently, there is a pressing need to disentangle the academic utility of screen time from its leisure-based overuse, especially in developing contexts where digital learning infrastructure is still consolidating.Simultaneously, depression among College of Education students has emerged as a critical and escalating concern globally and within Ghana. In Ghanaian tertiary institutions, recent studies indicate that up to 40% of students may experience moderate to severe depressive symptoms driven by a constellation of factors including academic pressure, financial insecurity, family expectations, social isolation, and, increasingly, unhealthy digital behaviors30,31,32. These findings align with global patterns observed in countries such as India, South Africa, and Brazil, where depression rates among College of Education students are similarly elevated. Of particular relevance is the interplay between depression and digital media use, which research increasingly describes as cyclical and mutually reinforcing. Excessive screen time especially passive scrolling and binge-watching may serve as a temporary escape from stress, anxiety, or loneliness2,33. This dual role of screen time as both a coping mechanism and a contributing factor to poor mental health outcomes complicates efforts to address psychological well-being in educational settings. The academic implications of depression are equally significant. Students experiencing depressive symptoms often report lower motivation, impaired concentration, reduced attendance, and a decline in academic performance37,38. In the Ghanaian context, where mental health support systems are still developing, depression remains underdiagnosed and undertreated, compounding its negative effects on educational attainment. Thus, understanding how depression interacts with digital behaviors such as screen time and social media use is not merely a psychological inquiry it is also an educational imperative, one that speaks to institutional priorities around student success, integrity, and retention.Mental health is a central construct in this study, as it exerts a profound influence on students’ ethical decision-making, cognitive function, and academic engagement. Increasingly, mental well-being is recognized as a foundational component of student success not merely a personal issue but a systemic one that affects institutional outcomes such as retention, academic integrity, and graduation rates World Health Organization39. Research has consistently shown that students experiencing poor mental health manifesting as anxiety, depression, burnout, or chronic stress often struggle to maintain self-regulation, concentration, and motivation40,41,32,33. These impairments can lead to increased susceptibility to academic dishonesty, not necessarily out of malice but as a coping mechanism under psychological strain16,42,43,44. In high-pressure academic environments, mental distress can erode students’ ethical boundaries and rationalize behaviors such as plagiarism, collusion, or misrepresentation of work, particularly when digital tools make such misconduct easier to execute. Assessment integrity, defined as the adherence to ethical standards in the conduct and completion of academic evaluations, has become an increasingly fragile construct in the digital age. These concerns are magnified in contexts where students are grappling with high levels of psychological stress and digital distraction, factors that have been empirically linked to increased tendencies toward academic dishonesty38,47,48,49,50. Thus, fostering academic integrity requires not only stronger technological surveillance or punitive policies but also upstream interventions that address students’ mental well-being and digital literacy.Internal assessment scores including continuous coursework, class participation, group projects, quizzes, and class tests are widely used as indicators of students’ sustained engagement, comprehension, and academic progression throughout a semester. For instance, students suffering from depression or high anxiety levels may underperform, not due to lack of ability, but because of cognitive fatigue, sleep disruption, or emotional disengagement12,14,32,36,51. Similarly, screen overuse particularly for non-academic purposes can reduce attention span, delay assignment completion, and lead to poor time management, all of which are detrimental to academic performance8,23,36,52.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
