      "text": "In some cases, the pressure to maintain grades amidst declining mental health and pervasive digital temptations may prompt students to engage in dishonest practices, thus undermining the validity of assessment outcomes1,53,54,55. Against this complex backdrop, the present study proposed a multivariate and mediation model to explore the direct and indirect effects of social media use, overall screen time, and depression on three interconnected outcomes: mental health, assessment integrity, and internal assessment scores. The inclusion of mediation analysis allows for the examination of underlying mechanisms for example, whether depression mediates the relationship between social media use and academic integrity, or whether mental health mediates the impact of screen time on performance. This approach reflects a growing shift in educational research from linear, bivariate models to more holistic, systems-oriented frameworks that capture the interplay between behavioral, psychological, and academic factors56,57,58,59,60.While international studies have illuminated the complex ties between digital engagement, mental health, and academic behavior, evidence from Ghana’s tertiary context remains limited and fragmented. The dynamics of screen exposure, emotional well-being, and ethical conduct may manifest differently within local institutional and cultural settings, where digital learning is expanding faster than regulatory and psychosocial support systems. Within this context, the present study advances a nuanced model that traces how students’ emotional states shape the relationship between their digital habits and academic outcomes, while also considering variations in the intensity and timing of screen use. By focusing on the interconnected domains of mental health, assessment integrity, and internal performance, this study contributes to a more contextually grounded understanding of digital life and learning among Ghanaian tertiary students. By focusing on tertiary students in Ghana, the study addressed a significant and underexplored nexus in Sub-Saharan African higher education: the convergence of digital behavior, mental health, and academic ethics. The findings of this research are intended to support evidence-based policymaking, inform the development of campus-wide digital wellness programs, and enhance academic integrity policies that are attuned to both psychological realities and technological shifts in the 21st-century classroom.Theoretical framework: application of social learning theoryThis study is underpinned, in part, by Social Learning Theory (SLT) as advanced by Albert Bandura (1977), which posits that human behaviors are acquired through the observation, imitation, and modeling of others, particularly within social contexts. In the context of this study, SLT provides a useful lens for understanding how tertiary students in Ghana may acquire both adaptive and maladaptive digital behaviors through online social interaction. Similarly, students who observe their peers openly discussing their depressive symptoms or exhibiting signs of academic burnout may also come to perceive these states as normative responses to academic pressure, potentially reinforcing psychological distress and disengagement34. Additionally, emotional and behavioral contagion concepts aligned with SLT are highly relevant to the relationships among social media use, depression, and academic performance examined in this study. In summary, Social Learning Theory enriches the interpretation of this study’s findings by highlighting the socially transmitted nature of digital behaviors and mental health patterns. It underscores the role of peer influence, social modeling, and online cultural norms in shaping how students use social media, manage screen time, handle psychological distress, and navigate ethical academic conduct. This theoretical framing supports the argument that interventions targeting digital wellness and academic integrity must address not only individual behavior but also the social learning environments both virtual and physical in which these behaviors are developed and sustained.HypothesesHypothesis 1 (direct effect)H1: Higher levels of social media use and overall screen time will be significantly associated with poorer mental health among tertiary students.Hypothesis 2 (mediation)H2: Depression will mediate the relationship between social media use and internal assessment scores, such that greater social media use will predict higher depression, which in turn will predict lower academic performance.Hypothesis 3 (moderated direct effect)H3: Higher levels of depression will be significantly associated with increased likelihood of assessment dishonesty, especially among students with high screen time exposure.MethodologyResearch designThis study adopted a cross-sectional correlational research design to explore the relationships among social media use, screen time, depression, mental health, assessment integrity, and internal assessment scores among tertiary students in Ghana. The study specifically explored whether depression mediated the relationship between digital behaviors (i.e., screen time and social media use) and students’ academic integrity and internal assessment outcomes. Mediation analysis was integrated within the correlational framework to assess whether the effects of screen-related behaviors on academic performance and ethical conduct were channeled through psychological well-being. As such, causal inferences regarding whether digital behaviors cause changes in depression, which in turn influence academic outcomes, cannot be confirmed.",
