      "text": "By employing this approach, the study aimed to generate empirical insights into the interconnected dynamics of digital engagement, mental health, and educational performance, all of which are crucial for developing responsive institutional policies and support mechanisms within Ghanaian tertiary institutions.Population and samplingThe target population for this study comprised tertiary-level students enrolled in public Colleges of Education (CoEs) across all sixteen administrative regions of Ghana. This digital engagement, which spans academic, professional, and social activities, made these institutions particularly suitable for examining the intersection of digital behavior, mental health, and academic outcomes. Overall, the sampling strategy provides a robust framework for examining digital engagement, mental health, and academic outcomes within this context, while maintaining methodological transparency and rigor.InstrumentationThe survey instrument was a structured, multi-section questionnaire carefully designed to measure the key variables of interest in the study: social media use, screen time, depression, mental health, assessment integrity, and internal assessment scores. The questionnaire was developed in English, as it is the medium of instruction in all Colleges of Education in Ghana, and underwent expert review and pilot testing before full deployment.Social media useSocial media use was assessed using a modified version of the Social Media Engagement Questionnaire (SMEQ) developed by Frison and Eggermont (2016). The modified SMEQ demonstrated strong internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha = 0.84) during pilot testing, suggesting suitability for this population.Screen timeScreen time was measured through self-reported daily hours spent on digital devices (mobile phones, laptops, and tablets) for both academic and non-academic purposes. Items were drawn from the Media and Technology Usage and Attitudes Scale (MTUAS) developed by Rosen et al. A composite screen time score was calculated as a weighted average, and reliability analysis yielded a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.79.DepressionDepression levels were assessed using the Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9), a widely recognized diagnostic screening tool for identifying the severity of depressive symptoms. By distinguishing between these two constructs, the study enables a more nuanced analysis of students’ psychological experiences, allowing for differentiated insights into how digital behaviors relate to both acute depressive symptoms and more generalized mental health challenges.Assessment integrityAssessment integrity was measured using a researcher-developed instrument, adapted from42Academic Dishonesty Inventory. This composite measure captured the ethical dimension of academic performance and demonstrated an acceptable Cronbach’s alpha of 0.76.Internal assessment scoresInternal assessment scores were self-reported by participants for the most recent academic semester. The self-reported nature of these scores was cross-validated in the pilot study with randomly selected transcript data, and no significant discrepancies were found, supporting the validity of this approach.Table 1 Alignment of constructs, instruments, psychometric properties, and research focus.Full size tableTable 2 Psychometric properties and factor structure of the assessment integrity scale.Full size tableIn Table2, the Assessment Integrity scale exhibits strong psychometric properties. Internal consistency is satisfactory (Cronbach’s α = 0.76, McDonald’s ω = 0.78), indicating reliable measurement of academic dishonesty. The addition of multiple psychometric indices strengthens confidence in inferences regarding relationships with mental health, depression, and digital behaviors.Reliability and validityTo ensure the quality, trustworthiness, and precision of the data collected, the reliability and validity of the survey instrument were systematically addressed throughout its development and deployment. The modified Social Media Engagement Questionnaire (SMEQ), used to capture frequency and type of social media use, produced a Cronbach’s alpha above 0.80. Even the researcher-developed scale used to measure assessment integrity, which was adapted from61academic dishonesty inventory, yielded a respectable reliability coefficient of 0.76. For example, the relationship between digital behavior (screen time and social media use) and psychological outcomes (depression and mental health) was grounded in the Cognitive-Behavioral Model of Problematic Internet Use35, which posits that maladaptive digital behaviors can lead to adverse mental health effects. Likewise, the examination of academic integrity was informed by social learning theory and academic ethics literature, underscoring the psychological and environmental factors that associate dishonest behavior in assessment settings. The instruments demonstrated sound psychometric properties, making them suitable for examining the complex relationships among digital behaviors, psychological well-being, and academic performance in the context of Ghanaian tertiary education.Data collection procedureThe data collection process was conducted over a three-month period, from January to March 2025, utilizing a structured, self-administered online questionnaire hosted on Google Forms. The combination of stratified digital outreach, ethical administration, and responsive monitoring ensured a robust data collection process, resulting in a final sample size of 970 students adequate for the correlational and mediation analyses planned for this study.Data analysisThe collected data were systematically cleaned and analyzed using IBM SPSS Statistics (Version 28). This bivariate analysis provided insights into the direction and strength of the associations among social media use, screen time, depression, mental health, assessment integrity, and internal assessment scores.",
