      "text": "Following the correlational analysis, multivariate regression models were constructed to assess the addictive strength of digital behavior (i.e., screen time and social media use) on mental health outcomes and academic measures while controlling for potential confounding variables such as age, gender, and region. Depression was modeled as a mediator in the relationship between screen time and social media use (independent variables) and two academic outcomes: assessment integrity and internal assessment scores (dependent variables). This approach enhanced the robustness of the findings and allowed for a more nuanced understanding of the psychological mechanisms underlying digital engagement and academic performance. By integrating these ethical safeguards, the study upheld the principles of respect, beneficence, and justice, thereby ensuring the well-being and rights of all participants were fully protected.ResultsThis section presented the results of statistical analyses conducted to test the study’s three hypotheses regarding the relationships among social media use, screen time, depression, mental health, and academic outcomes. Subsequently, multiple regression models were used to examine the direct predictive effects of social media use and screen time on mental health (Hypothesis 1). Mediation analysis using PROCESS Model 4 tested whether depression served as a significant explanatory mechanism between social media use and academic performance (Hypothesis 2). Finally, moderated regression analysis assessed whether screen time amplified the relationship between depression and academic dishonesty (Hypothesis 3). Collectively, these data screening procedures confirm that the assumptions of linear regression were adequately met, supporting the reliability and interpretability of the reported results.Table 3 Pearson correlation matrix with subscales.Full size tableThe Pearson correlation matrix in Table3presents the relationships among six major constructs and their respective subscales: Social Media Use (SMU), Screen Time (ST), Depression (measured via PHQ-9), Mental Health (measured via GHQ-12), Assessment Integrity, and Assessment Score (Academic Performance). Starting with Social Media Use (SMU), the subscales frequency of use, purpose for entertainment, academic information use, and emotional investment are moderately to strongly correlated with one another (ranging fromr= .36 to 0.51). Notably, higher frequency and emotional dependence on social media are positively correlated with depressive symptoms (e.g.,r= .39 for cognitive-affective symptoms) and negatively correlated with indicators of mental health (e.g.,r= –.35 with resilience). Additionally, social media use for entertainment is positively associated with academic dishonesty (r= .34 with cheating in online tests) and negatively with academic performance (r= – .28 with GPA). These results imply that excessive or emotionally-driven social media use may compromise students’ mental wellbeing, ethical standards, and academic success. In terms of Screen Time (ST), the matrix shows strong intercorrelations among the subscales, including academic screen time, non-academic screen time, late-night screen time, and multitasking while studying (e.g.,r= .57 between non-academic and late-night screen time). Non-academic and late-night screen use are particularly associated with elevated levels of depression (e.g.,r= .40 with somatic symptoms) and reduced mental health (e.g.,r= – .35 with resilience). Similarly, multitasking during study correlates with higher emotional investment in social media (r= .34), increased depression (r= .34), and lower academic outcomes (r= – .31 with GPA). These findings indicate that not only the quantity but also the context and timing of screen use can adversely affect psychological and academic outcomes.Regarding Depression, the PHQ-9 subscales (somatic symptoms, cognitive-affective symptoms, academic disengagement, and suicidal ideation) are highly interrelated (ranging fromr= .74 to 0.87), reflecting the internal validity of the depression construct. Depression is strongly and negatively associated with mental health measures (e.g.,r= − .62 with emotional wellbeing), and positively associated with academic dishonesty (e.g.,r= .33 with moral disengagement). It also demonstrates a substantial negative correlation with academic performance (e.g.,r= − .45 with GPA), suggesting that students with elevated depressive symptoms are more likely to struggle academically and may engage in unethical behaviors to cope with performance demands. Mental health is negatively related to social media and screen time behaviors (e.g.,r= – .34 with emotional investment in SMU), and positively correlated with academic performance (e.g.,r= .38 with GPA). Better mental health is also associated with higher assessment integrity (e.g.,r= – .24 with plagiarism inclination), underscoring the role of psychological wellness in promoting ethical academic behavior and success. Assessment Integrity, encompassing plagiarism, cheating, misrepresentation, and moral disengagement, forms a tightly knit cluster (r= .79 to 0.84 among subscales). It is positively associated with problematic media behaviors (e.g.,r= .35 between moral disengagement and entertainment use) and negatively associated with both mental health and academic performance (e.g.,r= – .36 with GPA). This pattern suggests that students who experience digital distractions and lower psychological functioning may be more prone to compromising academic ethics. Finally, Assessment Score, comprising quizzes/tests, assignments, class engagement, and GPA, is highly internally consistent (r= .80 to 0.88 among subscales) and exhibits strong negative correlations with depression (e.g.,r= – .45 with GPA) and academic dishonesty (e.g.,r= – .37 with cheating).",
