      "text": "In contrast, it is positively correlated with mental health (e.g.,r= .38 with emotional wellbeing), highlighting the importance of emotional stability and ethical behavior in achieving academic excellence. In summary, the matrix illustrates a coherent and interdependent system where excessive and non-purposeful media use, emotional reliance on social platforms, and nighttime screen engagement are linked to elevated depression, diminished mental health, increased academic dishonesty, and poorer academic performance. Conversely, students with strong mental health profiles tend to uphold academic integrity and perform better. These findings underscore the need for holistic interventions that address not only academic skills but also media literacy, emotional resilience, and ethical awareness in the digital age.Hypothesis 1: direct effectH1: Higher levels of social media use and overall screen time will be significantly associated with poorer mental health among tertiary students.Table 4 Multiple regression analysis predicting mental health from social media use and screen time subscales.Full size tableIn Table4, hypothesis 1 posited that higher levels of social media use and overall screen time would be significantly associated with poorer mental health among tertiary students. The outcome variable was mental health (MH), and the predictors included various subscales of Social Media Use (SMU) and Screen Time (ST). Therefore, multicollinearity did not compromise the validity of the direct effect estimates of social media use and screen time subscales on mental health. Starting with Social Media Use (SMU), the frequency of use was a significant negative predictor of mental health (β = − 0.21,p< .001), with a 95% confidence interval ranging from − 0.29 to − 0.13. This suggests that more frequent social media use is associated with diminished mental health, possibly due to increased stress, distraction, or social comparison. Emotional investment in social media (e.g., mood-dependent use) was the strongest SMU predictor, with a beta coefficient of − 0.30 (p< .001), and confidence intervals between − 0.39 and − 0.21. This indicates that individuals who are emotionally tied to their social media use tend to report significantly poorer mental health. Even academic-related use of social media, typically assumed to be more purposeful, was negatively associated with mental health (β = − 0.11,p< .001), though the effect size was smaller. This may suggest that even academically motivated use can contribute to information overload or reduced sleep, ultimately impairing wellbeing.Regarding Screen Time (ST), non-academic screen time was a robust predictor of poor mental health (β = − 0.26,p< .001), indicating that recreational digital consumption likely including entertainment and passive scrolling is detrimental to emotional and psychological functioning. Late night screen usage, defined as screen time after 11 p.m., had one of the strongest negative associations with mental health (β = − 0.29,p< .001), with confidence intervals from − 0.38 to − 0.21. This finding is especially important given the potential of late-night screen exposure to disrupt sleep cycles and exacerbate emotional distress. Multitasking while studying, a form of divided attention involving concurrent screen use during academic tasks, was also significantly associated with lower mental health scores (β = − 0.24,p< .001). This could reflect cognitive overload and reduced academic satisfaction, both of which can affect wellbeing. Even academic screen time showed a significant negative relationship with mental health (β = − 0.08,p= .008), although the effect was relatively weaker, suggesting that excessive exposure to screens even for learning may still impair mental wellness if not well-managed. This demonstrates that both social media behaviors and screen time practices collectively contribute meaningfully to mental health outcomes among tertiary students. Higher engagement with social media and prolonged screen exposure particularly emotionally driven use, nighttime use, and multitasking are consistently linked to poorer mental health outcomes. These results underscore the importance of digital wellness strategies and time management interventions to mitigate the psychological impacts of screen-based behaviors in student populations.We retained the bimodal 0–0–1–1 scoring for the GHQ-12 to remain consistent with common screening practices for psychological distress, while treating the summed score as a continuous variable in regression analyses. We discuss this distinction in the manuscript and justify modeling both as separate predictors or mediators, noting that statistical overlap is accounted for while maintaining conceptual differentiation.We acknowledge that the study involved multiple statistical tests, including a large Pearson correlation matrix (Table2) encompassing several subscales across six major constructs, as well as multiple regression analyses (Table3) examining the predictive effects of social media use and screen time on mental health. For the correlation analyses, each set of subscales belonging to a particular construct for instance, the four subscales of Social Media Use or the four subscales of Depression was treated as a single family of tests, and Holm–Bonferroni-adjusted p-values were computed to ensure that the probability of false positives across each family remained controlled.",
