      "text": "Similarly, in the multiple regression analyses predicting mental health from seven subscales of social media use and screen time, all predictors were considered a single family, and Holm–Bonferroni adjustments were applied to their p-values. For example, the emotional investment subscale of social media use was negatively associated with mental health (β = − 0.30, 95% CI [–0.39, − 0.21]), indicating a moderate and statistically robust effect, while late-night screen time was similarly associated with poorer mental health (β = − 0.29, 95% CI [–0.38, − 0.21]). Consequently, the study provides a robust and reliable depiction of the relationships among social media behaviors, screen time, mental health, academic integrity, and academic performance. These measures collectively reinforce the credibility of the conclusions and support the practical implications for digital wellness interventions, emotional resilience strategies, and ethical academic conduct in tertiary education settings.Hypothesis 2: mediationH2: Depression will mediate the relationship between social media use and internal assessment scores.Table 5 Mediation analysis of depression in the relationship between social media use and academic performance.Full size tableHypothesis 2 proposed in Table5that depression would mediate the relationship between social media use (SMU) and internal assessment scores (academic performance) among tertiary students. The analysis confirmed a significant indirect effect of social media use on academic performance through depression, thereby supporting the hypothesis. The results showed a significant positive association between social media use and depression, with a beta coefficient of 0.41 (p< .001), indicating that higher levels of social media use are linked to elevated depressive symptoms (a-path). This finding aligns with existing research that implicates excessive or emotionally dependent social media engagement in negative mental health outcomes such as anxiety, loneliness, and depressive mood. In turn, depression was significantly negatively associated with academic performance, with a beta value of − 0.35 (p< .001), confirming that students experiencing higher levels of depression tend to perform worse on internal academic assessments (b-path). This suggests that depression functions as a detrimental psychological mechanism impairing students’ ability to focus, study effectively, and retain academic content.The direct effect of social media use on academic performance (c’-path) remained significant even after accounting for depression (β = − 0.17,p< .001), suggesting partial mediation. This confirms that depression explains a substantial portion of the negative impact of social media use on academic outcomes. The total effect (c-path), which represents the sum of direct and indirect relationship, was − 0.31 (p< .001), meaning that social media use has a moderately strong overall negative effect on students’ academic performance. Taken together, these findings provide compelling evidence that depression is a significant psychological pathway through which social media use undermines academic performance in tertiary students. While social media may serve social and informational functions, its overuse especially when emotionally driven appears to increase depressive symptoms, which in turn impair students’ academic functioning. The results underscore the importance of integrating mental health support and digital literacy interventions into academic environments to reduce the potential academic consequences of social media overuse.Hypothesis 3: moderated direct effectH3: Higher levels of depression will be associated with increased assessment dishonesty, especially among students with high screen time exposure.Table 6 Moderation analysis of screen time on the relationship between depression and assessment dishonesty.Full size tableIn Table6, hypothesis 3 tested whether higher levels of depression would be associated with increased assessment dishonesty, particularly among students with greater screen time exposure. To examine this, a moderated multiple regression analysis was conducted using centered variables and an interaction term between depression and screen time. The results provide significant support for the hypothesis, indicating that screen time moderates the relationship between depression and dishonest academic behavior. The analysis revealed a significant main effect of depression on assessment dishonesty (β = 0.29,p< .001), suggesting that students with higher depressive symptoms are more likely to engage in dishonest behaviors during assessments. Similarly, screen time independently predicted assessment dishonesty, with a beta value of 0.22 (p< .001), indicating that students who spend more time on screens particularly for non-academic purposes tend to report higher instances of dishonest academic practices. Crucially, the interaction effect between depression and screen time was also significant (β = 0.19,p= .007), confirming a moderation effect. This means that the positive association between depression and assessment dishonesty becomes stronger as screen time increases. In other words, moderates the association the likelihood that students experiencing depression will resort to dishonest academic behavior, perhaps as a maladaptive coping mechanism or due to reduced cognitive and emotional self-regulation.The overall model accounted for 24% of the variance in assessment dishonesty (R2= 0.24), and the interaction term alone explained an additional 4% of the variance (ΔR2= 0.04), which is a meaningful contribution in behavioral research. These findings offer important insights into the complex interplay between mental health, digital behavior, and academic integrity.",
