      "text": "Students experiencing depression may already be vulnerable to ethical lapses due to reduced motivation, increased anxiety, and feelings of helplessness. When combined with high screen time often linked to procrastination, distraction, or emotional disengagement the risk of engaging in dishonest academic behavior appears to escalate. This underscores the need for targeted interventions that not only address students’ mental health concerns but also promote healthy and mindful use of digital devices. Institutions may consider incorporating academic integrity modules, digital wellness campaigns, and psychological support services as part of a holistic strategy to curb academic dishonesty in the digital age.Table 7 Conditional effects of depression on assessment dishonesty, integrity, and mental health at varying levels of screen time.Full size tableTable7presents a detailed analysis of the conditional effects of depression on three key outcome variables assessment dishonesty, assessment integrity, and mental health at varying levels of screen time: low (− 1 SD), average (0 SD), and high (+ 1 SD). The results indicate that screen time not only moderates the relationship between depression and academic behavior but also amplifies the negative consequences of depressive symptoms on students’ mental health and integrity. At low levels of screen time, depression showed a weaker but still statistically significant effect on all outcomes. Specifically, it was modestly associated with increased assessment dishonesty (β = 0.17,p= .020) and significantly predicted decreased assessment integrity (β = − 0.21,p= .002). The effect of depression on mental health was stronger, with a beta of − 0.39 (p< .001), indicating that even at low screen time, depression substantially diminishes mental health. At the mean level of screen time, the effects of depression intensified. Assessment dishonesty increased more substantially (β = 0.29,p< .001), and assessment integrity declined further (β = − 0.33,p< .001).Depression’s negative impact on mental health also grew stronger (β = − 0.51), with a very large and significant effect size (f2= 0.26). These mid-level effects illustrate how typical screen use already magnifies the negative implications of depression on academic ethics and psychological well-being. Depression had a strong positive association with assessment dishonesty (β = 0.38,p< .001), indicating that students experiencing depression and high screen usage were much more likely to engage in dishonest assessment behaviors. The impact of depression on mental health peaked at this level (β = − 0.61), showing that frequent screen use exacerbates emotional strain. Effect sizes were largest at high screen exposure (e.g., f2= 0.38 to 0.44), indicating that screen time dramatically heightens the detrimental association of depression on students’ academic and psychological outcomes. Collectively, these findings confirm that screen time acts as a significant amplifier of the effects of depression. While depressive symptoms are detrimental in their own right, their impact becomes progressively worse as screen exposure increases. The escalation of assessment dishonesty and deterioration of mental health and integrity suggest a concerning interaction between psychological distress and digital behavior patterns. Educational institutions and mental health practitioners should consider screen time reduction and digital literacy as part of broader interventions aimed at supporting student well-being and academic integrity. Tailored strategies for students exhibiting depressive symptoms especially those heavily engaged with screens may be essential for mitigating risks associated with compromised ethical behavior and emotional burnout.Fig. 1Full size imageSimple slopes of depression on outcomes at varying levels of screen time.Figure1depicts the interaction between depression and screen time on three outcomes: assessment dishonesty, assessment integrity, and mental health. In the case of assessment dishonesty, the left panel shows that as depression increases, students’ engagement in dishonest behaviors also rises across all levels of screen time. This effect is most pronounced among students with high screen time (+ 1 SD), whose dishonesty increases sharply with rising depression, whereas those with low screen time (− 1 SD) exhibit a gentler slope. For assessment integrity, the middle panel indicates that higher levels of depression are associated with lower integrity in assessments. This negative relationship is strongest among students with high screen time, suggesting that excessive screen use amplifies the detrimental effect of depression on students’ adherence to ethical assessment practices. Across all levels of screen time, increasing depression predicts poorer mental health, with the decline being slightly more pronounced for students who engage in high screen use. Overall, the figure highlights that high screen time exacerbates the negative effects of depression on academic and mental health outcomes.Table 8 Results of harman’s single-factor test for common-method variance.Full size tableTo assess potential common-method variance, the study employed both Harman’s single-factor test and a marker-variable approach, which together provide a robust evaluation of bias arising from the measurement method. For the Harman’s test, all measured items across constructs social media use, screen time, depression, mental health, assessment integrity, and internal assessment scores were entered into an unrotated principal component analysis.",
