      "text": "The variable “frequency of social media use for entertainment purposes” was selected as the marker variable. Correlations between the marker variable and the primary constructs were minimal (ranging from 0.21 to 0.28), confirming that method bias did not meaningfully inflate observed relationships.DiscussionThis study investigated the multifaceted effects of social media use, screen time, and depression on tertiary students’ mental health, academic integrity, and internal assessment performance within the Ghanaian higher education landscape. Using multivariate, mediation, and moderation analyses, the findings not only affirmed global evidence connecting excessive digital behaviors to psychological distress and academic decline but also highlighted how these dynamics are shaped by Ghana’s unique sociocultural and institutional context. Consistent with Hypothesis 1, higher levels of social media use particularly emotionally intense engagement (e.g., doom scrolling, passive consumption, or social comparison behaviors) and nighttime screen exposure were significantly associated with poorer mental health outcomes. Students reporting frequent and unregulated digital activity exhibited lower emotional resilience, increased psychological exhaustion, and greater susceptibility to stress-related conditions. These patterns align with global findings28, which link prolonged screen engagement, especially in the evening, to disrupted circadian rhythms, impaired affective regulation, and sleep disturbances. These patterns align with global findings28and resonate with21,36,55, who reported that Ghanaian university students experiencing fear of COVID-19 and high academic stress displayed elevated future anxiety and psychological distress, demonstrating the vulnerability of tertiary students to stressors in both digital and real-world contexts.Colleges of Education operate within limited-resource environments, where support for mental health, digital literacy, and pedagogical innovation is often unevenly distributed. Students struggling with mental health challenges may face difficulties meeting academic and professional competencies, potentially affecting the quality of future educators. The findings therefore underscore the need for institutional mental health infrastructure, including accessible counseling services, wellness programs, and training for staff and student peer-support networks, to buffer the negative effects of high digital engagement. With increased reliance on online teaching, learning, and communication, clear policies on screen time management, safe social media practices, and digital wellbeing are urgently needed. Targeted interventions such as workshops on responsible social media use, structured academic screen schedules, and sleep hygiene education can mitigate the adverse psychological and academic consequences observed in this study.Notably, this study uncovered that even screen time spent on academic tasks had negative psychological consequences. This finding challenges the long-standing assumption that academic or “productive” screen time is inherently beneficial or at least neutral in terms of mental health. Instead, it suggests that the quantity of screen exposure, regardless of content, may surpass students’ cognitive and emotional processing thresholds, leading to digital fatigue and burnout. Similar findings have been reported by3,7,23,39, who documented that excessive engagement with virtual learning platforms such as long hours on Zoom, Moodle, or Google Classroom can generate a type of academic technostress characterized by low motivation, information overload, and digital withdrawal symptoms. Infrastructure-related constraints such as frequent power outages, unstable internet connectivity, and limited access to ergonomic devices may exacerbate the psychological toll of digital learning. For many students, learning online involves not only mental exertion but also logistical and emotional stress related to unreliable digital access9,16,29,32,63,64,65. Unlike their counterparts in high-income settings who often enjoy seamless connectivity and institutional tech support, Ghanaian students frequently navigate a fragile digital ecosystem, making screen-based tasks more stressful and less sustainable. Additionally, sociocultural pressures to excel academically especially in competitive university programs may intensify students’ dependency on screen-based study tools, even when they recognize the negative impact on their well-being. These insights suggest an urgent need for universities and educational policymakers in Ghana to adopt a holistic digital wellness strategy. Second, interventions should include structured digital health literacy programs to educate students on managing screen time, setting digital boundaries, and recognizing early symptoms of screen-induced distress25,36,50. By addressing both the technological and psychological dimensions of student life, tertiary institutions can foster a more resilient and ethically grounded academic environment.Hypothesis 2 investigated the mediating role of depression in the relationship between social media use and academic performance. Findings confirmed that depression significantly mediated this relationship students who reported higher social media use also reported greater depressive symptoms, which in turn predicted lower academic performance. These results align with prior findings by30,32,33,66,67,68who established a strong link between problematic social media use, depressive tendencies, and impaired academic functioning. This mediation pathway demonstrates that the effect of digital engagement on learning is not just cognitive or behavioral, but deeply psychological. Depression erodes concentration, intrinsic motivation, memory retention, and academic self-efficacy all key predictors of academic performance. In Ghana, where mental health stigma remains prevalent and access to psychological services is limited12,39,46,48, students experiencing depressive symptoms may avoid help-seeking, allowing academic decline to compound unchecked. The model’s explained variances 27% in depression and 23% in academic scores underscore the significance of emotional well-being in academic outcomes.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
