    {
      "text": "Academic institutions must revise assessment policies to better reflect the realities of digital distraction and psychological distress. At the same time, integrity-focused education should be reinforced to address the psychosocial roots of academic dishonesty. Investments should be directed toward developing national guidelines on healthy digital engagement for students, funding university wellness programs, and supporting research on digital behavior among youth in low- and middle-income settings. Finally, these findings call for a broader cultural shift in how digital engagement is perceived within student life. Rather than advocating for abstinence from technology, stakeholders should foster a culture ofintentional digital usepromoting mindfulness, boundary-setting, and balanced online activity that supports rather than undermines academic and psychological wellbeing.Implications for theoryDrawing on Social Learning Theory, the findings suggest that students’ digital behaviors both adaptive and maladaptive are socially acquired and reinforced through their online peer networks. Institutions should identify and promote student influencers, peer mentors, and digital ambassadors who embody healthy screen habits, academic integrity, and emotional resilience. Programs that foster critical reflection on digital content, peer influence, and online identity can help students become more intentional about the behaviors they imitate and internalize. Finally, these findings call for a broader cultural shift in how digital engagement is perceived within student life. Rather than advocating for abstinence from technology, stakeholders should foster a culture of intentional digital use promoting mindfulness, boundary-setting, and balanced online activity that supports rather than undermines academic and psychological wellbeing. The study also contributes to theory by refining the interplay between digital behavior, mental health constructs, and academic outcomes. The findings validate components of Social Cognitive Theory, particularly the notion that cognitive and affective self-regulation mediates the impact of environmental factors (e.g., digital stimuli) on behavioral outcomes (e.g., cheating, academic disengagement). Moreover, by distinguishing between mental health as a general psychological state and depression as a clinical outcome and mediator, the study advances conceptual clarity within digital wellness and education research. This distinction is theoretically significant in guiding future studies to disaggregate general emotional malaise from clinically salient pathways when explaining ethical and academic behaviors in digital contexts. While most existing models are drawn from high-income contexts, this research offers insights from a low- to middle-income African setting, challenging global assumptions and emphasizing localized understandings of stress, digital identity, and academic pressure.LimitationsWhile this study offers important insights, it is not without certain limitations that warrant consideration. This limits the ability to establish causal relationships between social media use, screen time, and mental health or academic performance. While correlations can be identified, it is not possible to determine whether excessive screen time leads to poor mental health and academic challenges or if individuals experiencing these difficulties are more likely to engage with social media as a coping mechanism. Third, while standardized and validated instruments were used, they may not fully capture the nuanced cultural and contextual variations in students’ digital behaviors or perceptions of academic integrity within Ghanaian Colleges of Education. Broader sampling across institutional types would also improve the external validity and contextual robustness of the findings.Data availabilityThe datasets generated and analyzed during this study focused on social media use, screen time, depression, mental health, assessment integrity, and academic performance among tertiary students in Ghana are available from the corresponding author, Simon Ntumi, upon reasonable request. Data access will be subject to institutional ethical review procedures.AbbreviationsCoE:College of educationDSM-IV:Diagnostic and statistical manual of mental disorders, fourth editionGHQ-12:General health questionnaire—12 itemsLMS:Learning management systemMTUAS:Media and technology usage and attitudes scalePHQ-9:Patient health questionnaire—9 itemsSMEQ:Social media engagement questionnaireSMU:Social media useST:Screen timeMH:Mental healthβ:Beta coefficientp:p-value (statistical significance)CI:Confidence intervalReferencesAfful, D., & Boateng, J. K. (2023). The role of Mind wandering and anxiety in the association between internet addiction and hyperactivity-impulsivity: a serial mediation model.BMC Psychol.13(1), 345 (2025).ArticleMathSciNetPubMedPubMed CentralGoogle ScholarAkar, E. Exploring the impact of social network structures on toxicity in online mental health communities.Comput. Information Systems Frontiers, 27(4), 1603-1617.Google ScholarFrison, E. & Eggermont, S. Exploring the relationships between different types of Facebook use, perceived online social support, and adolescents’ depressed mood.Social Sci. Mediating effect of digital addiction on the relationship between academic motivation and life satisfaction in university students. The Impact of Social Media on the Lifestyle of University Students: A Study in Ghanaian Universities (Doctoral dissertation, Ashesi University).ArticleGoogle ScholarPerlis, R. H. et al. Journal of Academic Ethics, 24(1), 45.Zewude, G. T., Natnael, T., Woreta, G. T., & Bezie, A. E. (2025). A multi-mediation analysis on the impact of social media and internet addiction on university and high school students’ mental health through social capital and mindfulness. The effect of social media dependence on the perceived academic performance of university students in Cameroon: the role of psychological well-being and social anxiety. Frontiers in Psychology, 16, 1598724.Google ScholarTwenge, J. M. & Campbell, W. K. Associations between screen time and lower psychological well-being among children and adolescents: evidence from a population-based study.Prev.",
