      "text": "The PHQ-9 consists of nine items, each corresponding to a core symptom of depression based on DSM-IV criteria. The PHQ-9 demonstrated excellent reliability in this study (Cronbach’s alpha = 0.88).Mental healthGeneral mental health was measured using the General Health Questionnaire-12 (GHQ-12), a validated screening instrument for detecting psychological distress and non-specific mental health issues in general populations. The GHQ-12 asks respondents to rate their recent emotional and psychological states such as feelings of strain, depression, anxiety, or happiness on a 4-point response scale. Depression, as measured by the PHQ-9, refers to a specific clinical syndrome characterized by core symptoms such as anhedonia, hopelessness, and fatigue, consistent with diagnostic criteria. In contrast, general mental health, assessed using the GHQ-12, captures a broader range of psychological well-being or distress, including subclinical or non-diagnostic emotional states such as anxiety, perceived strain, and social dysfunction. The Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9), used to measure depression, recorded a high reliability coefficient of 0.88, demonstrating excellent internal consistency. The General Health Questionnaire-12 (GHQ-12), which assessed general mental health, also performed well with an alpha value of 0.81. Mental Health, measured using the GHQ-12, is highly coherent across its subscales emotional wellbeing, social functioning, sleep quality, and resilience with strong positive intercorrelations (ranging fromr= .80 to 0.88). This approach is supported by prior psychometric research22, which demonstrates that GHQ-12 continuous scores maintain reliability and validity for parametric analyses. Regarding construct overlap, we acknowledge the strong negative correlation (r≈ − .60) between GHQ-12 well-being subscales and PHQ-9 depression symptoms. Despite this correlation, the two instruments capture theoretically distinct domains: the PHQ-9 assesses depressive symptomatology, whereas the GHQ-12 measures broader psychological distress and well-being, including social functioning, resilience, and sleep quality. B. W. The PHQ-9: validity of a brief depression severity measure.J.",
