      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 44
    },
    {
      "text": "For instance, exposure to peers or influencers who showcase excessive screen use, glorify academic shortcuts, or trivialize mental health challenges may inadvertently normalize such behaviors among viewers.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 45
    },
    {
      "text": "Moreover, SLT explains how assessment integrity may be compromised in social media environments where plagiarism, contract cheating, or “sharing answers” is portrayed without consequences, and sometimes even as humorous or clever.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 46
    },
    {
      "text": "Students may replicate such behaviors after observing them, particularly if the modeled actions appear to yield desirable outcomes (e.g., good grades, social approval, less effort).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 47
    },
    {
      "text": "This observation-behavior reinforcement cycle becomes especially potent in screen-saturated academic contexts, where the lines between social interaction and academic conduct are increasingly blurred.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 48
    },
    {
      "text": "For example, students may imitate emotional expressions (e.g., sadness, hopelessness, disengagement) they frequently encounter on social media, thereby internalizing depressive affective states over time.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 49
    },
    {
      "text": "These modeled emotional states can, in turn, shape their approach to studying, coping with stress, or maintaining academic integrity.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 50
    },
    {
      "text": "A correlational design was deemed appropriate for this study because the primary objective was to investigate the nature and strength of associations between variables without manipulating any of them10.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 51
    },
    {
      "text": "This non-experimental design allowed for the identification of both direct and indirect relationships among psychological, behavioral, and academic variables in a naturalistic educational context.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 52
    },
    {
      "text": "It is important to note, however, that mediation in a cross-sectional design has inherent limitations.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 53
    },
    {
      "text": "While statistical procedures can estimate indirect effects and provide evidence consistent with mediation, the temporal ordering of variables cannot be definitively established.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 54
    },
    {
      "text": "The findings therefore reflect associational patterns and potential mechanisms rather than confirmed causal pathways27.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 55
    },
    {
      "text": "This design allowed the researcher to statistically determine how changes in one variable were associated with changes in another, and whether psychological distress served as a bridging mechanism between technology use and academic outcomes27.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 56
    },
    {
      "text": "These institutions were purposively selected to reflect the national landscape of teacher education, capturing the diversity of student experiences in terms of geography, institutional type, and year level.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 57
    },
    {
      "text": "Public CoEs were chosen because they not only provide foundational teacher education but also exhibit high levels of digital engagement among students.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 58
    },
    {
      "text": "To ensure representative coverage, a stratified recruitment approach was employed.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 59
    },
    {
      "text": "Stratification accounted for three key dimensions: geographical location (Northern, Middle, and Southern Belts), institutional type (urban, peri-urban, and rural), and year level (first, second, and third-year students).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 60
    },
    {
      "text": "Within each stratum, student participation was facilitated through official institutional mailing lists and WhatsApp platforms, which are commonly used for academic communication and class coordination.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 61
    },
    {
      "text": "Although the study initially intended to use random selection, the final recruitment relied on voluntary participation within these channels, resulting in a mixed stratified–convenience sampling method.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 62
    },
    {
      "text": "This approach balanced the need for diversity across regions and institutions with practical considerations related to accessibility and engagement.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 63
    },
    {
      "text": "Across the sixteen regions, responses were obtained from 29 public Colleges of Education, with an average institutional response rate of 61%, ranging from 54% in urban colleges to 68% in rural institutions.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 64
    },
    {
      "text": "Participation was relatively balanced across geographical belts, ensuring that findings were not overly influenced by any single region or institutional type.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 65
    },
    {
      "text": "In total, 970 students completed the survey, exceeding the recommended sample size required to detect moderate effects in correlational and mediation analyses with sufficient statistical power.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 66
    },
    {
      "text": "Inclusion criteria were clearly defined to maintain consistency and relevance across participants.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 67
    },
    {
      "text": "Students were required to be currently enrolled in a public College of Education, at least 18 years old, possess regular access to a digital device (such as a smartphone, tablet, or laptop), be proficient in English (the medium of instruction), and have participated in at least one internal assessment during the ongoing semester.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 68
    },
    {
      "text": "The instrument was adapted to reflect the most commonly used platforms among Ghanaian students, including WhatsApp, Instagram, Facebook, Snapchat, and TikTok.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 69
    },
    {
      "text": "Items measured the frequency of engagement, duration of use, and primary motivations for use (e.g., academic networking, socializing, entertainment).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 70
    },
    {
      "text": "Responses were recorded on a 5-point Likert scale, ranging from “Never” (1) to “Very Often” (5), allowing for the quantification of usage patterns across multiple platforms.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 71
    },
    {
      "text": "(2013), and were adapted to suit the educational context in Ghana.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 72
    },
    {
      "text": "Respondents provided separate estimates for weekday and weekend usage, with options to differentiate between productive (e.g., studying, attending online lectures) and recreational (e.g., gaming, social media browsing) screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 73
    },
    {
      "text": "This approach allowed for a nuanced understanding of digital exposure.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 74
    },
    {
      "text": "Participants indicated how often they experienced each symptom over the previous two weeks using a 4-point scale: “Not at all” (0), “Several days” (1), “More than half the days” (2), and “Nearly every day” (3).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 75
    },
    {
      "text": "Total scores ranged from 0 to 27 and were categorized according to severity cutoffs (e.g., minimal, mild, moderate, moderately severe, and severe) as proposed by Kroenke, Spitzer, and Williams (2001).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 76
    },
    {
      "text": "The bimodal scoring method (0-0-1-1) was used to calculate overall scores, with higher scores indicating greater distress.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 77
    },
    {
      "text": "Although depression and mental health are often used interchangeably in everyday language, this study treats them as related yet conceptually distinct constructs.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 78
    },
    {
      "text": "The scale included 10 items that assessed both behavioral engagement in dishonest practices (e.g., copying during tests, unauthorized collaboration, plagiarism) and attitudinal tolerance toward such practices.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
