      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 133
    },
    {
      "text": "This page provided information on national mental health helplines and institutional counseling services that participants could contact should they experience emotional distress or require psychological support.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 134
    },
    {
      "text": "First, correlation analyses (Table1) provided an overview of the relationships among key constructs and subscales.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 135
    },
    {
      "text": "Missing data were minimal, ranging from 0.8% for the PHQ-9 cognitive-affective subscale to 3.2% for the late-night screen time variable.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 136
    },
    {
      "text": "Univariate outliers were identified using standardized z-scores exceeding |3.29|; for example, three cases in the academic performance subscale exceeded this threshold.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 137
    },
    {
      "text": "Multivariate outliers were detected via Mahalanobis distance (p< .001), resulting in five cases with unusually high combined scores on social media use and depression measures.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 138
    },
    {
      "text": "Normality checks indicated that all continuous variables approximated a normal distribution.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 139
    },
    {
      "text": "Skewness values ranged from − 0.41 (resilience) to 1.02 (non-academic screen time), and kurtosis values ranged from − 0.52 (social functioning) to 1.18 (emotional investment), all within the acceptable range of ± 2.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 140
    },
    {
      "text": "Histograms and Q-Q plots further confirmed that no substantial deviations from normality were present.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 141
    },
    {
      "text": "Relationships, such as between depression scores and mental health, were approximately linear, and no major curvilinear patterns were observed, supporting the suitability of linear regression modeling.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 142
    },
    {
      "text": "Homoscedasticity was assessed via residual plots and formally tested using the Breusch–Pagan test, which yielded χ2= 7.46,p= .29, indicating no significant heteroskedasticity.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 143
    },
    {
      "text": "In preliminary models where minor heteroskedasticity was observed, robust standard errors were applied to ensure valid statistical inference.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 144
    },
    {
      "text": "This suggests internal consistency within the construct of social media usage.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 145
    },
    {
      "text": "This hypothesis was tested using multiple regression analysis (PROCESS v4.0, Model 4) with 5000 bootstrap samples and 95% confidence intervals.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 146
    },
    {
      "text": "VIF values ranged from 1.51 to 2.30, well below the commonly accepted threshold of 10, and tolerance values ranged from 0.43 to 0.66, exceeding the minimum recommended value of 0.10.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 147
    },
    {
      "text": "All models yielded statistically significant results, supporting the hypothesis.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 148
    },
    {
      "text": "The explanatory power of individual subscale models ranged from R2= 0.06 (for academic screen time) to R2= 0.22 (for late night screen usage), indicating modest to moderate proportions of variance explained.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 149
    },
    {
      "text": "Importantly, when all seven subscales were entered into a combined model including all SMU and ST dimensions the overall model explained 31% of the variance in mental health (R2= 0.31, Adjusted R2= 0.30), with a significant F-statistic of 62.85 (df = 7, 962).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 150
    },
    {
      "text": "In sum, the findings provide robust empirical support for Hypothesis 1.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 151
    },
    {
      "text": "To ensure the robustness of this decision, we conducted a sensitivity analysis using the Likert scoring method (0–1–2–3) and reran all regression models.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 152
    },
    {
      "text": "The results were substantively consistent, with no meaningful changes in effect sizes or significance levels, confirming that the choice of scoring method did not alter the study’s conclusions.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 153
    },
    {
      "text": "Conducting numerous tests increases the likelihood of Type I errors, where some statistically significant findings could occur by chance.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 154
    },
    {
      "text": "Beyond mere significance testing, we placed strong emphasis on effect sizes and 95% confidence intervals (CIs), which provide a more nuanced understanding of the magnitude and precision of observed effects.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 155
    },
    {
      "text": "Reporting CIs alongside standardized regression coefficients allows for the evaluation of the range within which the true population effect likely falls, providing context beyond the p-value and mitigating the potential inflation of significance due to multiple testing.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 156
    },
    {
      "text": "These methodological steps strengthen the rigor and interpretability of the findings.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 157
    },
    {
      "text": "However, the indirect effect of SMU on academic performance via depression was also statistically significant (β = − 0.14, 95% CI [− 0.21, − 0.07],p< .001).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 158
    },
    {
      "text": "In terms of model fit and explained variance, the depression model accounted for 27% of the variance in depressive symptoms (R2= 0.27), while the academic performance model explained 23% of the variance in internal assessment scores (R2= 0.23).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 159
    },
    {
      "text": "These values indicate that the predictors in each model provide a meaningful, though not exhaustive, explanation of the respective outcomes.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 160
    },
    {
      "text": "The model was statistically significant, with an F-value of 28.32 (p< .001), indicating a strong overall fit.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 161
    },
    {
      "text": "This pattern was echoed in the reversed depression variable (interpreted as emotional well-being), which had a beta of − 0.49 (p< .001).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 162
    },
    {
      "text": "These effects corresponded to small-to-moderate effect sizes (Cohen’s f2ranging from 0.03 to 0.24), suggesting meaningful, though not overwhelming, impacts in lower screen use contexts.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 163
    },
    {
      "text": "The reversed depression variable had an even stronger association with diminished emotional well-being (β = − 0.58,p< .001).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 164
    },
    {
      "text": "At high levels of screen time, the moderating effect of screen exposure was most pronounced.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 165
    },
    {
      "text": "Likewise, the inverse relationship with assessment integrity was significant and stronger (β = − 0.44,p< .001).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 166
    },
    {
      "text": "The reversed depression metric confirmed these effects, with a substantial beta of − 0.66 (p< .001).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 167
    },
    {
      "text": "Finally, the right panel shows the impact of depression on mental health outcomes.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 168
    },
    {
      "text": "The first extracted factor accounted for 26.4% of the total variance, which is well below the commonly accepted threshold of 50%.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 169
    },
    {
      "text": "This indicates that no single factor dominated the variance and that common-method bias is unlikely to substantially affect the study’s findings.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 170
    },
    {
      "text": "Table8presents the results of this analysis.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 171
    },
    {
      "text": "In addition, a marker-variable method was applied to further examine CMV.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 172
    },
    {
      "text": "This variable was chosen because it is theoretically unrelated to core outcome constructs such as depression, mental health, assessment integrity, and academic performance, but is measured within the same survey context, making it suitable for detecting systematic method variance.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 173
    },
    {
      "text": "Importantly, the consequences of these findings are particularly salient for public teacher training in Ghana.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 174
    },
    {
      "text": "Moreover, the results have implications for national digital policy and educational technology guidelines.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 175
    },
    {
      "text": "The Ghanaian context further complicates this relationship.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 176
    },
    {
      "text": "First, there is a need to enhance digital infrastructure to ensure equitable access to stable and student-friendly technology.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 177
    },
    {
      "text": "Finally, academic calendars and assessment modalities should be reviewed to reduce excessive online workload and promote balanced digital engagement.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 178
    },
    {
      "text": "This aligns with the theory of ego depletion11,53,69,70, which suggests that sustained self-regulation demands (such as resisting temptation during online assessments) are undermined under psychological strain.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 179
    },
    {
      "text": "In a high-stakes academic context like Ghana’s tertiary institutions where academic success is closely tied to future employability students under pressure may rationalize dishonest behavior as a coping mechanism.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 180
    },
    {
      "text": "First, teacher training institutions must integrate digital well-being education into their academic and co-curricular structures.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 181
    },
    {
      "text": "This can be achieved through targeted workshops, orientation modules, and peer-led campaigns that promote healthy screen habits and emotional self-awareness.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 182
    },
    {
      "text": "Second, college administrators and the Ghana Tertiary Education Commission (GTEC) should strengthen the mental health support systems available to student-teachers.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 183
    },
    {
      "text": "Institutions must ensure the presence of trained counselors on campus, establish confidential mental health support services, and partner with regional health directorates to provide outreach programs, especially in rural-based Colleges of Education.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 184
    },
    {
      "text": "Third, academic staff must adopt pedagogical strategies that reduce digital overload.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 185
    },
    {
      "text": "Therefore, lecturers and tutors should avoid over-reliance on continuous screen-based instruction.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 186
    },
    {
      "text": "They can instead employ hybrid models with reflective journaling, small group discussions, and field-based learning to reduce digital fatigue while enhancing engagement.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 187
    },
    {
      "text": "Fourth, the study recommends the strengthening of policies and practices that uphold assessment integrity.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 188
    },
    {
      "text": "Incorporating integrity-focused modules in professional teacher training will also cultivate future educators who value and model ethical conduct.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 189
    },
    {
      "text": "Lastly, policy makers and college leadership should invest in further research and innovation around student digital health.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 190
    },
    {
      "text": "Additionally, the significant link between screen time and compromised assessment integrity suggests that traditional monitoring approaches may be insufficient.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 191
    },
    {
      "text": "This may include increasing the use of open-book assessments, take-home assignments that emphasize originality and reflection, and leveraging AI tools to detect inconsistencies in student writing patterns.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 192
    },
    {
      "text": "For policy-makers in the education and health sectors, the results support a more integrated policy framework that connects mental health promotion, digital education, and assessment ethics.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 193
    },
    {
      "text": "This underscores the importance of leveraging positive role modeling within digital spaces.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 194
    },
    {
      "text": "Interventions should not only educate but also reshape the social narratives students engage with online.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 195
    },
    {
      "text": "In this way, SLT-informed practices can disrupt harmful modeling cycles and instead cultivate a culture of ethical, mindful, and psychologically safe digital engagement.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 196
    },
    {
      "text": "It supports a more nuanced modeling of mental health trajectories among students, suggesting that broad psychological distress may not uniformly predict behavior unless channeled through specific affective disorders like depression.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 197
    },
    {
      "text": "Finally, the study invites a culturally responsive lens for interpreting digital wellness.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 198
    },
    {
      "text": "First, the study employed a cross-sectional design, which captures data at a single point in time.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 199
    },
    {
      "text": "Second, the reliance on self-reported data introduces potential sources of bias, including social desirability bias and recall inaccuracies.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 200
    },
    {
      "text": "Participants may have underreported or overreported their digital behaviors, psychological states, or assessment-related conduct, either consciously or unconsciously.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 201
    },
    {
      "text": "Moreover, because all constructs including mental health, assessment integrity, and academic performance were measured using the same survey instrument administered in a single sitting, the study is susceptible to common-method variance (CMV).",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 202
    },
    {
      "text": "To assess this, Harman’s single-factor test was conducted, and the first unrotated factor accounted for 26.4% of the total variance, well below the 50% threshold typically indicating CMV concerns.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 203
    },
    {
      "text": "Additionally, a marker-variable sensitivity analysis revealed no significant changes in the pattern or strength of associations when controlling for the marker variable, suggesting that shared-method bias was minimal but cannot be entirely ruled out.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 204
    },
    {
      "text": "Fourth, despite efforts to include diverse institutions, participation was voluntary and facilitated through institutional mailing lists and student platforms, which may have introduced self-selection bias.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 205
    },
    {
      "text": "Future studies are encouraged to employ longitudinal or multi-source designs, combining objective digital trace data, institutional records, and qualitative insights, to mitigate shared-method effects and enhance causal inference.",
      "paperTitle": "Mental health and depression as mediators between social media use screen time and academic integrity among tertiary students in Ghana",
      "doi": "10.1038/s41598-026-40285-0",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 206
    },
    {
      "text": "To protect participant confidentiality, raw data will not be publicly shared.",
