    {
      "text": "Original ResearchScreen Time and its Impact on Cardiometabolic Risk Factors among Children and Youth with Type 1 Diabetes (T1D)ShitalBhorAboli A.BhaleraoNikhilShahVamanKhadilkarAnuradha V.Khadilkar,Authors and AffiliationsJournal of Pharmacy and Bioallied Sciences18(Suppl 1):pS171-S175,February 2026.|DOI:10.4103/jpbs.jpbs_1604_25OpenAbstractIntroduction:Excessive screen time (ST) is a major sedentary behavior in children, raising concerns about cardiometabolic health. Children with type 1 diabetes (T1D) may be especially vulnerable. This study assessed associations between ST, adiposity, and cardiometabolic risk in youth with T1D.Methods:A cross-sectional study on 511 children and youth (2–21 years) with T1D attending a tertiary hospital in Western India collected demographic, clinical, dietary, activity, and self-reported ST data. Boys with higher ST had greater fat percentage, fat mass, fat Z-scores, and systolic BP. Girls with higher ST showed greater fat percentage and fat mass, with strong correlations to systolic BP.Conclusion:Prolonged ST is linked to adverse adiposity and BP outcomes in youth with T1D, underscoring the need for lifestyle counselling in diabetes care.INTRODUCTIONIn today’s digital age, early screen exposure is common among children, but excessive use raises concerns about its impact on their health and development. [1]Children and youth residing in urban areas tend to report higher screen time compared to their rural counterparts. Studies have indicated a progressive, positive correlation between excessive screen time and higher levels of adiposity during childhood. [2]Increased screen time was significantly associated with elevated cardiometabolic risk (CMR) in children and adolescents, with each additional hour of daily screen time positively correlated with an increase in CMR. [4]In children and youth having T1D with poor glycemic control, vascular dysfunction and its risk factors, such as hypertension and dyslipidemia, may manifest earlier. In a study on 235 children with T1D from our center, the prevalence of dyslipidemia was found to be 47.2%, with abnormal low-density lipoprotein cholesterol being the most common abnormality. [5]No studies, Indian or global, have examined the relationship between the duration of screentime and CMR in children with T1D; screen time as a primary exposure with CMR outcomes in T1D is still largely unexplored. The study aimed to evaluate the relationship between self-reported screen time and overweight/obesity, and to examine its association with adiposity and cardiometabolic risk factors among children and youth with type 1 diabetes.MATERIALS ANDMETHODSThis cross-sectional study included children and youth with TID aged 2–21 years, attending a tertiary hospital in Western India (Sept 2023–Apr 2024). Screen time was assessed using a validated questionnaire. Height was measured with a stadiometer, weight with an electronic scale, and body mass index (BMI) calculated (kg/m²). Height, weight, BMI, and WC were converted to Z-scores using Indian references. Overweight/obesity was defined by BMI Z-scores and central adiposity by WC. Fat %, fat mass, fat-free mass, and total body water were measured and converted to Z-scores. Stepwise regression identified predictors of fat % Z-score (hsCRP, eGDR, screen time, diabetes duration) and systolic BP (diabetes duration, fat % Z-score, screen time, physical activity).Post hocpower analysis (G*Power 3.1) confirmedn= 511 gave 80% power at α = 0.05.RESULTSAmong 511 children with T1D, 235 were boys (46%) and 276 girls (54%), with mean ages of 13.2 ± 4.9 and 13.1 ± 4.8 years, respectively. Boys reported higher average daily screen time than girls (123.3 ± 84.8 vs. 114.3 ± 83.4 minutes,P< 0.05). Rural children had slightly lower screen time than urban peers (114.8 ± 81.9 vs. 122.4 ± 86.1 minutes,P> 0.05). Screen time increased progressively with age [Figure 1].Open multimedia modalFigure 1Open multimedia modalAge group-wise and gender-wise distribution of screen time per day among children and youth with T1 DiabetesOverweight/obesity prevalence was 13.6% in boys and 14.1% in girls. Hypertension was more frequent in boys (20.8%) than girls (12.5%,P< 0.05).As shown inTable 1, 51% of boys and 45% of girls reported screen time ≥2 hours/day (P> 0.05). Boys exceeding this threshold had significantly higher fat percentage, fat mass, fat percentage Z-score, and systolic BP (P< 0.05). Among girls, screen time ≥2 hours was also associated with higher fat percentage and fat mass (P< 0.05).Open multimedia modalTable 1Open multimedia modalSexwise distribution of screen time with adiposity and cardiometabolic risk factors among children and youth with type 1 DiabetesIn girls, screen time correlated strongly with systolic BP and moderately with fat percentage Z-score. In boys, correlations were mild for systolic BP and moderate for diastolic BP [Figure 2].Open multimedia modalFigure 2Open multimedia modalSex-wise correlation of screen time with clinical, biochemical and body composition parametersMultivariate analysis [Table 2] identified disease duration, fat percentage Z-score, and screen time as significant predictors of systolic BP in both sexes (P< 0.05). Additionally, moderate-to-vigorous physical activity was a negative predictor of systolic BP in girls (P< 0.05).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen Time and its Impact on Cardiometabolic Risk Factors among Children and Youth with Type 1 Diabetes (T1D)",
      "doi": "10.4103/jpbs.jpbs_1604_25",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Elevated hs-CRP and higher screen time were linked to greater fat percentage Z-scores in boys (P< 0.05).Open multimedia modalTable 2Open multimedia modalPredictors of – (A) Systolic blood pressure (B) Fat %Z-scoreDISCUSSIONIn this cohort, nearly half of children and youth with T1D reported screen time (ST) ≥2 hours/day, with higher prevalence in boys and older participants. Prolonged ST was associated with greater adiposity and CMR, including higher fat mass, fat percentage, and elevated systolic blood pressure (BP). Disease duration, fat percentage Z-score, and ST emerged as predictors of systolic BP for both sexes, while physical activity was protective in girls. To our knowledge, this is the first study to report ST prevalence in children with T1D. [11,12]Differences may reflect cultural factors, parental supervision, and disease-specific routines, yet the finding that children with T1D exceed guidelines highlights the need for interventions. Our findings align with Horneret al.,[3]who reported positive associations between ST and CMR markers in adolescents, particularly boys. [2]showed graded associations between ST, adiposity, and insulin resistance. We also observed significant associations with fat mass and fat percentage in both sexes, though not with BMI, suggesting ST may better predict body composition than BMI alone. [14]reported a dose-response link between ST and metabolic syndrome risk in youth. [15]extended these findings into adulthood, linking prolonged ST with obesity and diabetes. The health implications of our study are significant, as children with T1D already face elevated risks of vascular dysfunction and dyslipidemia, excessive screen use may compound these risks. Importantly, we found ST to be more closely associated with body composition indices (fat mass, fat %) than BMI, underscoring the need to incorporate body composition tools in pediatric diabetes clinics. Future longitudinal studies should assess whether reducing ST can improve cardiometabolic outcomes in this high-risk group.CONCLUSIONNearly half of children with T1D exceeded recommended screen time limits, with significant associations with adiposity and cardio metabolic risk. Targeted interventions to reduce ST, particularly in boys and older youth, may help mitigate long-term cardiovascular risks.Financial support and sponsorshipNil.Conflicts of interestThere are no conflicts of interest.REFERENCES1.Goswami P, Parekh V. The impact of screen time on child and adolescent development: A review. Int J Contemp Pediatr 2023;10:1161-5.Cited HereGoogle Scholar2.Nightingale CM, Rudnicka AR, Donin AS, Sattar N, Cook DG, Whincup PH, et al. Screen time is associated with adiposity and insulin resistance in children. Screen time is associated with cardiometabolic and cardiovascular disease risk in childhood and adolescence. Brussels, Belgium: International Diabetes Federation, 2025.Cited Here5.Shah N, Khadilkar A, Gondhalekar K, Khadilkar V. Prevalence of dyslipidemia in Indian children with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes mellitus. Pediatr Diabetes 2020;21:987-1211.Cited HereGoogle Scholar6.Dhube A, Krishnaveni GV, Veena SR, Kehoe S, Coakley P, Fall CH. Physical activity and cardiometabolic risk markers in Indian children. J Pediatr Endocrinol Metab 2022;35:1010-9.Cited HereGoogle Scholar8.Gupta P, Shah D, Bedi N, Galagali P, Dalwai S, Agrawal S, et al. Indian Academy of Pediatrics Guidelines on screen time and digital wellness in infants, children and adolescents. Screen time behaviours and caffeine intake in US children: Findings from the cross-sectional National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES). Screen time and metabolic risk factors among adolescents. Arch Pediatr Adolesc Med 2010;164:643-9.Cited HereGoogle Scholar14.Jahangiry L, Aune D, Farhangi MA. Screen time and the risk of metabolic syndrome among children and adolescents: A systematic review and dose-response meta-analysis. Screen time from adolescence to adulthood and cardiometabolic disease: A prospective cohort study. J Gen Intern Med 2023;38:1821-7.Cited HereGoogle ScholarView full references listKeywords:Adiposity; cardiometabolic risk factors; screentime; type 1 diabetesCopyright © 2026 Journal of Pharmacy and Bioallied Sciences",
