      "chunk_index_in_doc": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "See:https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101Statistics from Altmetric.comRequest PermissionsIf you wish to reuse any or all of this article please use the link below which will take you to the Copyright Clearance Center’s RightsLink service.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 11
    },
    {
      "text": "Devices and associated applications have significant potential to improve young children’s early reading, support creative thinking and promote social interaction with family members living elsewhere.15Conversely, studies have demonstrated potential negative effects of increased screen time on children’s executive functioning, sensorimotor development and academic outcomes.16This suggests that differing types of screen time (eg, educational, recreational) may have variable impacts on children’s health and well-being.15–17In addition, emerging evidence suggests that volume of screen time may be important: higher levels of screen time (>1 hour/day) in USA 2–5 years old were shown to be associated with decreased psychological well-being, including less curiosity and self-control, lower emotional stability and being more difficult to care for.18Screen time in younger children has also been associated with poorer physical health19and poorer sleep when screens are used prior to bedtime.20These associations are also apparent in older children and appear to persist through adolescence both cross-sectionally and prospectively,21suggesting that it may be important to limit screen time in younger children for their wider and longer-term health and well-being.Given the prospective association between good mental health during the preschool years and in later childhood, a better understanding of how early childhood behaviours impact mental health in under 5s is required.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 12
    },
    {
      "text": "There is a relative absence of evidence about the link between screen time and mental health in UK preschool-aged children, and no study to date has assessed how types of play and screen time interact to influence mental health.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 13
    },
    {
      "text": "Moreover, although both outdoor and adventurous play have been proposed to be important for children’s mental health, no research has directly explored the association between these subtly different types of play in preschool-aged children.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 14
    },
    {
      "text": "We investigated how total time spent playing outdoors or adventurously and engaging in screen time was associated with commonly used mental health indicators (ie, Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaire (SDQ) and Positive and Negative Affect Schedule (PANAS)) in these children.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 15
    },
    {
      "text": "We hypothesised that greater adventurous play would be associated with better mental health, and more (recreational) screen time would be associated with poorer mental health.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 16
    },
    {
      "text": "Based on the British Children’s Play Survey (BCPS) (focused on children aged 5–11 years13), the BPCPS survey was designed to closely align to the BCPS, to allow comparison across datasets.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 17
    },
    {
      "text": "Detailed explanations of measures undertaken in the BPCPS are provided elsewhere.12Sample size was pragmatic and driven by available funding; it is one of the largest studies to examine preschool-aged children’s play internationally.ProcedureData were collected by YouGov through their online panel, which includes over 1 million adults living in the UK.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 18
    },
    {
      "text": "YouGov recruit panel members from a diverse range of sources to ensure diversity, and use active sampling, inviting members of their research panel via email to complete online surveys.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 19
    },
    {
      "text": "According to YouGov records, 14 250 parents had a child aged 2–4 years and were eligible to participate (figure 1).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 20
    },
    {
      "text": "On starting the online survey, participants were initially asked to indicate whether they were parents of children aged 2–4 years, and after obtaining electronic consent, respondents were provided with a link to access the survey.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 21
    },
    {
      "text": "They were advised to respond about their eldest child in the age range.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 22
    },
    {
      "text": "The survey included validated questionnaires about the mental health of children and parents, children’s play (time), including outdoor and adventurous play, childcare attendance, socio-demographic and geographical information.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 23
    },
    {
      "text": "On completion of the survey, all respondents were given YouGov reward points which could be exchanged for vouchers or payments.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 24
    },
    {
      "text": "For the present study, the participating sample was recruited to be approximately representative of the national population and then weighted based on age, gender, social class, region and level of education to the national profile of adults living in the UK (see below).Download figureOpen in new tabDownload powerpointFigure 1Flow of participants through each stage of study recruitment.MeasuresOutcome measuresChild psychological well-being was assessed using two measures: the SDQ for parents of 2–4 years old (SDQ 2–4)22 23and the Positive and Negative Affect Schedule for Children-P (PANAS-C/P).24Strengths and Difficulties QuestionnaireThe SDQ 2–4 is a 25-item screening questionnaire designed for parents and teachers of children aged 2–4 years.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 25
    },
    {
      "text": "It asks about positive and negative attributes of the child, with items combined to create five subscales: emotional symptoms, conduct problems, hyperactivity/inattention, peer relationship problems and prosocial behaviour.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 26
    },
    {
      "text": "We calculated an ‘internalising’ score by summing the emotional problems and peer relationship problems subscales, and an ‘externalising’ score by summing conduct problems and hyperactivity/inattention subscales.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 27
    },
    {
      "text": "The prosocial behaviour subscale was not used for these analyses.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 28
    },
    {
      "text": "The SDQ has been used widely in this age group and has strong psychometric properties (seewww.sdqinfo.org23).Positive and Negative Affect SchedulePANAS-C/P is a 10-item scale, with care-providers asked to rate the extent to which their child has exhibited each of 10 emotions described in the past week; five items are summed to provide a positive affect score (PANASPA) and five remaining to give a negative affect score (PANASNA).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 29
    },
    {
      "text": "The scale has good psychometric properties.24Exposure variablesChildren’s playThe Children’s Play Scale (CPS)25was developed to better understand play in school-aged children and was applied to the preschool-aged group here; after piloting with parents of preschool-aged children, no amendments were deemed necessary to capture play in younger age groups.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 30
    },
    {
      "text": "Using the CPS, parents indicated howfrequently(0–7 days) and forhow long(in hours) their child played in a range of places, when not in childcare and not playing on a screen (in a home; outside at a home; at a playground; in green space (not a garden); in the street/public places; outdoors near water; and at indoor play centres and pools.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 31
    },
    {
      "text": "Parents were asked to answer about frequency and length of time playing in each location for Autumn/Winter and Spring/Summer separately.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 32
    },
    {
      "text": "These responses were then summed to derive the number of hours per year each child spent playing in each place.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 33
    },
    {
      "text": "The CPS also has an adventurous play supplement, to capture more ‘risky’ play, which was used here.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 34
    },
    {
      "text": "This asks parents to indicate how adventurously their child played at each play location using a scale from 1 (very low levels of adventure) to 5 (maximum levels of adventure).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 35
    },
    {
      "text": "The CPS was created with input from experts and parents, including iterative revisions with the parent group.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 36
    },
    {
      "text": "While the measure has not been formally validated against time use diaries or geographical information systems, it does have good face validity (as judged by parent piloting) and reliability.25Responses were used to create two exposure variables: adventurous play and outdoor play.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 37
    },
    {
      "text": "We chose to assess both exposures to differentiate between playing outside and playing adventurously, as most of the latter occurs during the former but they are not synonymous.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 38
    },
    {
      "text": "To derive adventurous play, the time spent playing across all locations where the child was reported to play with at least a mild level of adventure (2 on the Likert scale) was summed.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 39
    },
    {
      "text": "For outdoor play, the time children spent playing (at any adventure level) across all locations where the child was reported to play outside was summed.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 40
    },
    {
      "text": "For both variables, we first derived a yearly total (in hours), which was then divided by 52 to give hours/week spent playing adventurously and outdoors.Screen timeWe applied a scale used previously with parents of preschool-aged children26to assess screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 41
    },
    {
      "text": "This asked participants to report the amount their child spent looking at a screen (eg, television screen, tablet, phone) on weekdays and weekends (in hours per day).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 42
    },
    {
      "text": "Considering the potential differential impacts on development, we asked participants to answer separately for (1) recreational/communication and (2) educational screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 43
    },
    {
      "text": "Care providers answered from: ‘No time (ie,0 minutes)’, ‘less than 1 hour’, ‘between 1 to 2 hours’, ‘between 2 to 3 hours’, ‘between 3 to 4 hours’, ‘more than 4 hours’ or ‘don’t know’.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 44
    },
    {
      "text": "The midpoint value of each category (ie, 30 min for ‘less than 1 hour’) was used to derive an average daily total in hours per day, combining weekday and weekend values (in the ratio of 5:2).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 45
    },
    {
      "text": "Children were then categorised into three groups: ‘<1 hour’, ‘1–2 hours’, ‘>2 hours’, for each of the two exposures, capturing hours per day children spent engaging in (1) recreational and (2) educational screen time.CovariatesA range of confounding variables and competing exposures was derived based on existing literature, using the Daggity software27(see directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) inonline supplemental figure).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 46
    },
    {
      "text": "Respondents completed questions about socio-demographic and geographical characteristics.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 47
    },
    {
      "text": "From these, we derived child age, child sex, child ethnicity, child location (urban/rural/town and fringe), child learning disability, child physical disability and social grade.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 48
    },
    {
      "text": "We derived the hours/week children spent in formal childcare based on parent report.26A measure of parent mental distress was derived using the Kessler 6,28summing the 6-item scale (responses on a 5-point Likert scale) to give a single score ranging from 0 to 24, with higher scores indicating higher respondent distress.28Supplemental material[bmjopen-2025-105101supp001.pdf]Data managementAll data was provided in a fully anonymised form from YouGov UK.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 49
    },
    {
      "text": "For data analysis, participants were identified by their unique anonymised identification number.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 50
    },
    {
      "text": "The dataset were password-protected and held on a personal password-protected laptop.Patient and public involvementParents of preschool-aged children were involved in the development of the study questionnaire but did not contribute to this manuscript or to dissemination of this research.AnalysisAnalyses were carried out using Stata/SE V.17.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 51
    },
    {
      "text": "We derived descriptive characteristics for the sample, and for each of the exposure and outcome variables.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 52
    },
    {
      "text": "Missing data were negligible due to YouGov collection methods, but some participants (n=148) were excluded due to how the CPS was scored (eg, where parents answered unsure/not known for adventure level).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 53
    },
    {
      "text": "Visual inspection of histograms for each outcome variable was undertaken and assumptions of normality were met.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 54
    },
    {
      "text": "We also assessed correlations between exposure variables to ensure collinearity was not present (correlation between educational and recreational screen time r=0.27; between adventurous play and recreational screen time: r=0.07; adventurous play and educational screen time r=0.09, between adventurous and outdoor play r=0.66).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 55
    },
    {
      "text": "We ran linear regression models to explore how each of the four exposure variables (ie, adventurous and outdoor play, recreational and educational screen time) was associated with four outcome variables (ie, internalising and externalising, positive and negative affect scores).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 56
    },
    {
      "text": "We fitted models using the svyset command in Stata, which allowed us to use survey weights.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 57
    },
    {
      "text": "We used DAGs (DAGitty software27) for each exposure—outcome relationship, and models were minimally adjusted for appropriate confounders and competing exposures; results are provided for direct effects only.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 58
    },
    {
      "text": "Finally, we included an interaction term between each play and screen time exposure variable to determine whether there was a differential impact of play by levels of screen time on children’s mental health outcomes.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 59
    },
    {
      "text": "Model fit was assessed using the ‘testparm’ command (compatible with the svy command in Stata), applying an adjusted Wald test, with an interaction term retained when model fit was significantly better with the term included (ie, a value of p<0.05).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 60
    },
    {
      "text": "As the adventurous play exposure variable was derived using an adventure level of 2 or more, we also conducted sensitivity analyses using an adventure level of 3 or more to determine how this influenced our exposure-outcome association.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 61
    },
    {
      "text": "Post hoc analyses were conducted to explore the association between total screen time and mental health outcomes, including testing interactions, as WHO guidelines recommended no more than 1 hour per day total screen time9but made no distinction by type.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 62
    },
    {
      "text": "Descriptive characteristics of the sample are presented intable 1.View this table:View inlineView popupTable 1Descriptive characteristics of included participants (n=1018)Parents reported that 2–4 years old spent approximately 12 hours per week playing outdoors and 22 hours per week playing adventurously (table 2).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 63
    },
    {
      "text": "Most children engaged in more than 1 hour of recreational screen time per day, but less than 1 hour per day of educational screen time (table 2).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 64
    },
    {
      "text": "When combined, 159 (14%) children had <1 hour/day; 308 (28%) had >1–2 hours/day, 320 (29%) had >2–3 hours/day and 314 (29%) had >3 hours/day of screen time on average per day.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 65
    },
    {
      "text": "Children’s mental health outcomes (mean (SD), range) were as follows: externalising score: 7.4 (3.5), 0–18; internalising score: 4.3 (3.5), 0–17; PANASPA: 19.4 (3.4), 5–25; PANASNA: 9.0 (3.3), 5–25.View this table:View inlineView popupTable 2Summary of exposure variablesAssociations between adventurous play, screen time and mental health outcomesTable 3shows model results for all analyses (including unadjusted models and models adjusted for identified confounds (see above)).",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 66
    },
    {
      "text": "There was a positive association between hours/week play outdoors and adventurous play and positive affect, such that higher levels of play were associated with higher positive affect scores in young children.",
      "paperTitle": "Are play and screen time associated with British preschoolers’ mental health? Cross-sectional findings from the British Preschool Children’s Play Survey",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-105101",
