  "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-108729": [
    {
      "text": "You are viewing from:Tezpur University Article TextArticle menuArticleTextArticleinfoCitationToolsShareRapid ResponsesArticlemetricsAlertsPDFPublic healthOriginal research‘We just give the phone so they stay quiet’: a qualitative exploration of screen time practices among caregivers of children younger than 5 years in south Indiahttp://orcid.org/0000-0002-6396-5593Samya Varadarajan1,http://orcid.org/0000-0002-4387-8564Akila Govindarajan Venguidesvarane1,http://orcid.org/0000-0003-4029-558XMuthukumar Rajamohan1,1,http://orcid.org/0000-0003-0649-5406Murugesan Krupa2,http://orcid.org/0009-0006-2440-3682Karthik Narayanan Ramaswamy31Community Medicine,Sri Ramachandra Institute of Higher Education and Research,Chennai,Tamil Nadu, India2Department of Speech Language Pathology,Sri Ramachandra Faculty of Audiology & Speech Language Pathology,Chennai,Tamil Nadu, India3Pediatric intensive care,Rainbow Children’s Hospital & BirthRight Guindy,Chennai,Tamil Nadu, IndiaCorrespondence toDr Akila Govindarajan Venguidesvarane;aki_ravi24{at}yahoo.co.inAbstractObjectiveTo explore the perceptions, challenges and strategies of caregivers in managing screen time among children younger than 5 years in the urban and rural areas of Tamil Nadu, India.DesignQualitative study using focus group discussions (FGDs).SettingRural Health and Training Centre in Vayalanallur, Thiruvallur district and Urban Health and Training Centre, Thiruvanmyur in Chennai district, Tamil Nadu, India.Participants54 caregivers (27 rural, 27 urban, 46 mothers, 6 grandmothers, 2 fathers) of children younger than 5 years whose screen time exceeded WHO age-specific recommendations participated. Six FGDs were conducted, each with 8–10 participants.MethodsFGDs were conducted in familiar community locations near the participants’ homes to ensure comfort and accessibility. The audio recordings were transcribed verbatim in Tamil and translated into English and analysed using inductive thematic analysis. Coding was manually performed by two independent researchers.ResultsSix major themes emerged: (1) circumstances leading to screen exposure, (2) perceived advantages, (3) perceived disadvantages, (4) challenges in reducing screen time, (5) methods adopted to reduce screen time and (6) strategies for sustaining reduction. Safety concerns, lack of play space and the need to manage household chores were commonly cited reasons for screen use. Although most caregivers were aware of the potential harms, screens were often used to feed or pacify children. Emotional resistance from children, inconsistent family norms and grandparents’ screen use were common barriers. Caregivers employed strategies, such as limiting access, engaging in outdoor play and enrolling children in structured activities. Urban–rural differences were minimal, and thematic saturation was achieved after six FGDs.ConclusionsCaregivers face multiple, structural and family level challenges in managing screen time among young children. Awareness and interventions need to be tailored to address family dynamics, caregiver fatigue and lack of alternatives. Health providers, community workers and policymakers should collaborate to offer structured support, promote screen-free engagement and ensure child-friendly spaces in urban and rural communities.PUBLIC HEALTHQUALITATIVE RESEARCHCommunity child healthData availability statementData are available upon reasonable request. You will be able to get a quick price and instant permission to reuse the content in many different ways.PUBLIC HEALTHQUALITATIVE RESEARCHCommunity child healthSTRENGTHS AND LIMITATIONS OF THIS STUDYThis study used focus group discussions to generate qualitative data on caregivers’ experiences and perspectives on children’s screen use.The inclusion of participants from both rural and urban field practice areas allowed the exploration of screen time practices across different caregiving environments.Data collection and analysis followed a systematic inductive thematic approach, with independent coding and cross-verification to enhance methodological rigour.This study only included caregivers of children with excessive screen time, which may provide limited insights from caregivers who had successfully reduced screen exposure.IntroductionThe use of digital media by young children has become a growing global concern. Excessive screen time during early childhood is associated with a range of negative developmental outcomes, including language delay,3 4attention problems, emotional dysregulation,5behavioural issues,6sleep disturbances7 8and obesity.9–11Growing evidence suggests that excessive screen exposure in early childhood may have effects that extend beyond the preschool years, including later difficulties with attention, emotional regulation, academic functioning and mental well-being.12 13Globally, only a minority of children aged 5 years and younger meet recommended screen time guidelines. A recent meta-analysis reported that fewer than one in four children globally adhered to screen time recommendations, with lower adherence observed in low- and middle-income settings.14In India, evidence from multiple regions suggests that excessive screen exposure during early childhood is common. More than half of children younger than 5 years exceed the recommended screen time limits, with a prevalence ranging from approximately 55% to nearly 90%.15–17Our previous study from Tamil Nadu found excessive screen exposure in >70% of children younger than 5 years, and a recent systematic review reported a mean daily screen time exceeding 2 hour among young children in India.3 18Although excessive screen exposure has been reported worldwide, the contexts in which young children are cared for differ across settings.",
      "paperTitle": "‘We just give the phone so they stay quiet’: a qualitative exploration of screen time practices among caregivers of children younger than 5 years in south India",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-108729",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Screen use among young children is shaped by multiple factors, such as caregiver work demands, parental stress, family screen habits, availability of alternative activities and the increasing role of digital media in education.5 19In many high-income settings, children’s screen use is shaped by regulated childcare systems, safer neighbourhood play spaces and greater access to organised early learning and recreational activities.20In contrast, caregiving in India often takes place within nuclear households, with mothers and grandmothers bearing the primary responsibility for childcare, limited access to affordable formal childcare and fewer safe or supervised alternatives for young children’s play. National data show that women spend substantially more time on unpaid care work than men, reflecting persistent gendered caregiving roles.21Simultaneously, limited availability of safe outdoor play spaces, traffic and safety concerns, air pollution and neighbourhood restrictions reduce opportunities for active play, especially in cities.22Evidence from multi-country and Indian studies has suggested that caregivers’ perceptions of these structural and environmental constraints strongly shape children’s activity patterns, often resulting in greater reliance on screens as an indoor alternative rather than reflecting individual caregiver choices alone.23Although international and national guidelines exist, they are largely informed by evidence from high-income settings and may not fully reflect caregiving realities in diverse Indian contexts. The coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic further accelerated children’s exposure to digital media in India, with an increased reliance on screens for online learning, communication and daily routines, even among preschool-aged Indian children.24–26While quantitative studies have documented rising screen exposure and its associated developmental risks, they provide limited insight intowhycaregivers rely on screens in everyday life and the practical challenges they face in trying to reduce screen time.Caregivers, including parents and grandparents, play a central role in shaping young children’s media habits through daily supervision, rule setting and screen use modelling. Once introduced, screen exposure during early childhood may become embedded in daily routines, making subsequent reductions difficult. A recent meta-synthesis of qualitative studies on caregivers’ perceptions of screen time identified common themes across settings; however, none of the included studies were conducted in India.27Given the differences in family structures, caregiving roles and environmental constraints, there is a clear need to explore how caregivers in Indian settings perceive, negotiate and manage screen use among young children.The current study aimed to address the literature gap by conducting focus group discussions (FGDs) among parents and caregivers of children younger than 5 years with excess screen time in the rural and urban areas of Tamil Nadu, India.Materials and methodsStudy designThis descriptive qualitative study used FGDs to explore caregivers’ perceptions, challenges and strategies related to reducing excess screen time among children younger than 5 years (figure 1). The data were analysed using an inductive thematic analysis approach. No a priori theoretical framework was applied to guide the data collection or analysis; instead, themes were directly derived from the participants’ narratives. Existing literature and WHO screen time recommendations were used to contextualise the findings during interpretation.Download figureOpen in new tabDownload powerpointFigure 1Flow of methodology. FGDs, focus group discussions; RHTC, Rural Health and Training Centre; UHTC, Urban Health and Training Centre.SettingThis study was conducted in the rural and urban field practice areas attached to a medical college in Chennai, India. The caregivers of children aged younger than 5 years were initially identified by health inspectors and community health nurses from registers with information on those younger than 5 years. Home visits were conducted to assess children’s screen exposure based on WHO recommendations.Caregivers of children with screen times exceeding age-appropriate recommendations were considered eligible for inclusion. Eligible participants included mothers, fathers and grandmothers who were the primary caregivers and directly involved in the child’s day-to-day care. Caregivers who were not regularly involved in caregiving or who were unwilling to participate in group discussions were excluded.Eligible caregivers were invited in person to participate in the FGDs and informed about the date, time and location of the discussions. Only caregivers who provided informed consent were present during the FGDs. No financial or material incentives were offered for participation in this study.Six FGDs were conducted, three in a rural setting and three in an urban setting, with 8–10 participants in each group. Caregivers unwilling to participate or unable to provide consent were excluded.Data collectionThe interview guide (online supplemental file 1) and prompts were developed in consultation with co-faculty members, paediatricians and speech and language pathologists. The interview guide was pretested among a small group of caregivers of children younger than 5 years at the rural centre during an immunisation clinic day, when parents had brought their children for routine vaccinations.",
      "paperTitle": "‘We just give the phone so they stay quiet’: a qualitative exploration of screen time practices among caregivers of children younger than 5 years in south India",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-108729",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
