      "text": "The discussions were conducted in a common space located near the participants’ residences in both the RHTC and UHTC areas, routinely used for community outreach and meetings, as caregivers preferred a familiar setting close to home to avoid disruption to household work and to remain available for childcare. The guide included open-ended questions designed to elicit children’s perceptions of screen time, challenges in limiting screen exposure, family influences and strategies used to reduce and sustain screen time. The sociodemographic details of the participants and their children were also collected.Supplemental material[bmjopen-2025-108729supp001.pdf]Each discussion lasted between 45 min and 1 hour and was audio-recorded with permission. A likely contributing factor was familiarity between the participants and the research team, as all investigators were faculty members regularly engaged in home visits and community outreach activities through rural and urban health centres. This study adhered to the Consolidated Criteria for Reporting Qualitative Research 32-item checklist to ensure methodological rigour and transparency.Data analysisThe inductive thematic analysis approach described by Braun and Clarke28was used to identify and interpret data patterns. Following each FGD, the transcripts were reviewed, and preliminary codes were developed. The analysis was descriptive in nature and aimed to capture the explicit meanings and lived experiences of caregivers regarding screen time practices. Urban–rural differences were descriptively explored by comparing themes and patterns across focus groups conducted in each setting, rather than through a formal comparative analysis. The audio recordings were transcribed verbatim in Tamil and then translated into English. A visual coding tree was not created; however, the research team systematically documented and collaboratively reviewed all the codes and categories.ResultsParticipant profileSix FGDs were conducted, three in rural settings and three in urban settings, with 54 caregivers (mostly mothers, some grandmothers and one father). The caregivers were predominantly homemakers or informal workers, with educational levels ranging from primary to postgraduate. Most participants belonged to nuclear families with 1–3 children younger than 5 years (table 1). A summary of the thematic analysis is shown infigure 2.View this table:View inlineView popupTable 1Participants’ backgroundDownload figureOpen in new tabDownload powerpointFigure 2Thematic analysis summary illustrating major themes and associated sub-themes.FGDs were conducted in rural and urban settings to capture contextual diversity. The analysis revealed similar perceptions, concerns and experiences related to children’s screen use in both the settings. Data saturation was achieved through a sixth FGD, with no new themes emerging in the final session.Thematic analysis resulted in six overarching domains that reflected caregivers’ perceptions, experiences and strategies related to children’s screen use:Circumstances leading to excess screen time.Perceived advantages of screen time.Perceived disadvantages of screen time.Challenges in reducing screen time.Methods adopted to reduce screen time.Strategies for sustaining reduced screen time.Theme 1: circumstances leading to excess screen timeCaregivers citedsafety concerns, household responsibilities and lack of outdoor play optionsas the primary reasons for screen exposure. As the toddlers were challenging to care for, some parents found it comfortable to allow their children in front of screens for safety reasons, so that they did not run around or get injured. Digital media often served as ababysitterduring chores:My child followed me to the kitchen and touched hot plates and food and injured herself, so I prefer to make her sit in front of the TV while cooking. (FGD-3, rural, mother)Lack of safe outdoor spaces was commonly reported, particularly in urban settings:There is no space to play outside. (FGD-2, urban, mother)Some caregivers linked their reliance on screens to changes in family structure. Living in nuclear families meant that caregiving responsibilities were often managed by a single adult, without the support of grandparents or other relatives during the day. In the absence of extended family members, who traditionally shared childcare, caregivers described using screens to keep children occupied while attending to household tasks or work-related responsibilities:I have to attend online meetings in the evenings related to work; I tend to give my child a phone or turn on the TV so that he does not disturb me. (FGD-5, urban, mother, field note: expressed visible distress while explaining her situation)Theme 2: perceived advantages of screen timeAlthough many participants acknowledged the risks, some sawperceived benefits, such as language learning, educational content, and support in managing daily routines:My son learns a lot of rhymes. (FGD-6, urban)Some caregivers felt that screen use helped compensate for their own limitations, especially language skills:In school, they asked us to use YouTube videos to teach certain topics.",
      "paperTitle": "‘We just give the phone so they stay quiet’: a qualitative exploration of screen time practices among caregivers of children younger than 5 years in south India",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-108729",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
