    {
      "text": "(FGD-5, urban grandmother)Digital media was also viewed as a substitute for storytelling in the absence of joint family support:My girl loves to listen to stories – but I don’t find time as I am juggling between with household chores and work – so I use YouTube videos for moral stories (FGD-4, rural, mother)A mother, in particular, mentioned, “my child eats well only while watching phone – especially while giving him vegetables it’s easy to feed him while is engaged watching videos.” (FGD-1, rural, mother).However, this view had some conflicts among others, who felt that the child was not able to taste or relish the food given and felt it was wrong to do.Some caregivers felt proud that their children were able to use touch screens and watch videos to which they did not have access as kids.Theme 3: perceived disadvantages of screen timeCaregivers expressed deep concern over behavioural changes, reduced social interaction, physical inactivity and eye-related problems associated with screen use.Some caregivers reported behavioural issues, such as aggression, tantrums and violent behaviours:My child becomes aggressive and throws tantrums when the phone is taken away. (FGD-4, rural)My child watches TV serials with her grandparents; influenced by the scenes; she began to normalise behaviours like lying to others, and scheming parents for getting what she wants. (FGD-6, urban, mother)There were concerns regarding the child’s social interaction and peer engagement:I feel my child does not interact well with peers. (FGD-5, urban, father)Although some caregivers described using screens during meals as a practical way to ensure food intake, others were concerned about the children becoming dependent on screens for eating and losing interest in food:She will eat only if the phone is on. (FGD-5, urban)Reflection on broader development of the child, such as reduced imagination and lack of interest in books, was also expressed by caregivers:One of the parents in the urban setting felt, “I feel the children these days lose the ability to imagine and think creatively when they start watching screens. It’s disappointing to see these generation children hardly showing interest in books” (FGD-2, urban, mother)Theme 4: Challenges in Reducing Screen TimeAttempts to limit screen use led toemotional outbursts, defiance and household conflict:If I turn off the TV, my son breaks the remote and cries for 20 minutes. It is challenging to calm him down (FGD-3, rural, mother)My daughter said — ‘when grandma watches TV, why do you turn off mine?’ (FGD-2, urban, mother)My child just 5 years, turned completely aggressive when I tried to get the phone from him, he threw the phone down, shattering it. (FGD-6, urban, mother)Caregivers also described the challenges arising from differing screen use practices among adults within the household, which made it difficult to enforce consistent limits:When I try to discipline my child by taking back the phone, my husband gives his phone to the child and tries to win him. (FGD-1, rural, mother)Theme 5: methods adopted to reduce screen timeCaregivers described a range of low-cost strategies for reducing screen use, including redirecting children to toys or colouring activities, encouraging outdoor play, gradually reducing screen exposure and limiting access through measures, such as password locks or temporary disconnection from television services:I explained that if she watches too much, she may have to wear spectacles. (FGD-5, urban, father)Caregivers also noted that maintaining limits was difficult when screen use practices varied among the household adults.Some caregivers suggested engaging children in extracurricular activities, such as dance, singing, art or martial arts, to reduce screen use. Other caregivers strongly opposed this approach, leading to visible tension during discussions.Theme 6: sustaining reduced screen timeCaregivers emphasised the need for structured alternatives and acknowledged the difficulty in long-term maintenance, especially during school holidays, when children spent more time at home:It is possible to reduce screen time — but only if parents are consistent and make it a habit from early on. (FGD-5, urban)Enrolment in organised classes was mainly described by caregivers who reported having access to such activities in their neighbourhoods.Caregivers also expressed concerns that screen use might increase again as children grow older because of peer influence and school-related requirements. Parents of older children described receiving homework instructions and school communications through messaging applications, such as WhatsApp, which increased their exposure to mobile phones.Although many experiences were shared across the settings, some differences emerged in how caregivers described screen use. Urban caregivers spoke more about the lack of safe outdoor spaces, neighbour-related constraints, work-from-home demands and organised extracurricular activities. Rural caregivers more commonly described safety concerns within the home environment and fewer structured alternatives for children’s engagement. Differences were also noted across caregiver roles, with mothers frequently describing daily caregiving pressures, grandmothers referring to television viewing as routine or acceptable, and fathers expressing concerns about their children’s social interactions and behaviours.",
