    {
      "text": "These observations were not uniform, and overlapping views were observed across groups.DiscussionThe present study explored caregivers’ perspectives, challenges and strategies related to digital media usage among children younger than 5 years in the urban and rural areas of Tamil Nadu. The findings highlight how everyday caregiving responsibilities, family expectations and child well-being intersect in a digital environment. Consistent with our earlier quantitative findings in the same population, the caregivers frequently acknowledged their overreliance on screens.3Most caregivers were aware of the potential harm, including sleep problems, eyestrain, reduced social interaction and limited outdoor play, which is consistent with findings from studies in other settings.7 29–33Despite this awareness, screens were often rationalised as a babysitter or pacifier that enabled caregivers to complete household work, which was consistent with other qualitative studies.5 29 34 35When interpreted through family systems and sociostructural lenses, screen use among young children in this study appeared less as an individual caregiving choice and more as an adaptive response to everyday caregiving demands. Caregiving was largely managed by mothers and grandmothers within nuclear households, with limited extended family support and minimal daytime involvement of fathers, thus increasing the reliance on screens to manage competing responsibilities. These family dynamics interacted with environmental constraints, such as limited play space, safety concerns, long work hours and the cost of organised activities, making sustained screen time reduction difficult. This framing underscores screen use as a systemic issue, rather than an individual caregiver’s failure. Urban caregivers more often described a lack of safe play spaces and neighbour-related restrictions, whereas rural caregivers highlighted difficulties in supervising children in open environments. Urban–rural differences were descriptively examined through a comparison of FGDs rather than formal analytic comparisons.Parental perceptions of benefits included educational values, learning new languages and keeping the child calm, similar to a meta-synthesis.27The contradicting statements regarding the benefits and harms of screen use shed light on the deep tensions and stress involved in parenting. The behavioural and emotional changes that children undergo while restricting screens are among the most important themes generated. Temper tantrums, physical outbursts of throwing and breaking things, arguments that evolved and threats highlighted how screens had become a ‘norm’ in most of the families.29Caregivers also expressed uncertainty and a lack of guidance on how to manage these reactions, making it difficult to sustain limits on screen use despite their concerns. Although we did not formally analyse caregiver age, differences in familiarity with digital media and physical capacity for active engagement may influence how caregivers experience and manage screen time challenges, particularly older caregivers.The caregiver-suggested strategies in this study can be broadly grouped into three practical categories: first, immediate, low-cost behavioural approaches, such as restricting access, gradual reduction and redirecting children to non-screen activities within the home; second, engagement-based strategies, including outdoor play, storytelling and structured routines to replace passive screen use; and finally, longer-term or resource-dependent options, such as enrolment in extracurricular activities, which were perceived as helpful but not universally feasible. Although caregivers made active efforts to implement these strategies, access to structured activities often depended on family income and neighbourhood availability, making them easier to adopt in urban settings than in resource-limited contexts. Engaging children during holidays and weekends was more challenging, as echoed in a study by Sigmundová and Sigmund.36Tensions between partners and differences in opinions among caregivers where multiple caregivers were involved were frequently cited as common reasons for the difficulty in consistently limiting screen use. Similar to our study, parental conflicts were a reason for high screen time in children in other studies.33 37This suggests that screen time reduction efforts should include low-cost and community-based play opportunities that are feasible across different settings. Emerging global research has raised concerns that early and prolonged exposure to digital media may have implications for later mental health and well-being, reinforcing the importance of addressing screen use early in life through preventive and caregiver-supported approaches.38A key strength of our study was the inclusion of diverse caregiver voices from multiple settings, which enhanced the transferability of the findings to similar low- and middle-income contexts. FGDs were chosen to facilitate interactions among caregivers and uncover shared norms and tensions that may not have been present in the individual interviews. Despite the study’s rigour, our sample lacked gender diversity, with minimal representation of fathers or male caregivers, which may have restricted the perspectives on intra-household decision-making. The study setting in our own field area could have introduced a selection bias, as caregivers had better access to health and were more knowledgeable. Familiarity between the participants and the research team may also have influenced how caregiving practices and challenges were described, potentially shaping the openness or framing of responses during discussions.",
      "paperTitle": "‘We just give the phone so they stay quiet’: a qualitative exploration of screen time practices among caregivers of children younger than 5 years in south India",
      "doi": "10.1136/bmjopen-2025-108729",
      "source": "openalex",
