    },
    {
      "text": "Our study exclusively included caregivers of children with excessive screen time, and the perspectives of those who successfully maintained limited screen exposure were not captured. Including such caregivers may have provided additional strategies, stronger counterarguments and more diverse solutions to the challenges discussed.Future studies can be directed towards a longitudinal qualitative approach to track changes in caregivers’ attitudes and perceptions as the child grows. The inclusion of fathers and other family members is critical to better understand family dynamics and tensions. In addition, involving caregivers who have successfully limited screen time for their children can help identify effective and practical strategies that may not have emerged in the present study. Intervention studies on structured caregiver programmes provide better evidence for scalable solutions.ConclusionThis study highlights the multifaceted and culturally rooted challenges that caregivers face in managing screen time among children younger than 5 years. Voices from the community emphasise that screen use in early childhood is not only a matter of awareness but also of circumstance, support and available alternatives. Interventions should be designed with a family systems lens and implemented through accessible platforms, such as well-baby clinics, community health workers and early childcare centres.At the policy level, urban planning must prioritise the creation of child-friendly spaces, such as parks and playgrounds, especially in low-income neighbourhoods. Community-based programmes led by non-governmental organisations, self-help groups and government-supported Anganwadi centres should be leveraged to limit the screen time for young children. These efforts, if sustained and context-sensitive, can help reverse early screen dependency and foster healthier developmental environments for young children in India.Data availability statementData are available upon reasonable request. Transcripts are not publicly shared to protect privacy.Ethics statementsPatient consent for publicationConsent obtained directly from patient(s).Ethics approvalThis study involves human participants and was approved by the Institutional Ethics Committee of Sri Ramachandra Institute of Higher Education and Research, Chennai (IEC-NI/18/SEP/66/53). Written informed consent was obtained from each participant. Participation was voluntary, and confidentiality of responses was maintained throughout the study. Participants gave informed consent to participate in the study before taking part.AcknowledgmentsWe would like to thank all the primary caregivers of children under 5 years who took part in this study, for sharing their views and personal experiences during these difficult times, without which this study would not have been possible. We also would like to thank health inspectors and public health nurses of RHTC and UHTC for arrangements to conduct FGD.References↵World Health Organization.Guidelines on physical activity, sedentary behaviour and sleep for children under 5 years of age.Geneva,2019.Available:https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/311664↵GuptaP,ShahD,BediN,et al.Indian Academy of Pediatrics Guidelines on Screen Time and Digital Wellness in Infants, Children and Adolescents.Indian Pediatr2022;59:235–44.doi:10.1007/s13312-022-2477-6OpenUrlCrossRefPubMed↵VaradarajanS,Govindarajan VenguidesvaraneA,RamaswamyKN,et al.Prevalence of excessive screen time and its association with developmental delay in children aged <5 years: A population-based cross-sectional study in India.PLoS ONE2021;16:e0254102.doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0254102↵KrupaM,BoominathanP,RamananPV,et al.Relationship Between Screen Time and Mother-Child Reciprocal Interaction in Typically Developing Children and Children with Autism Spectrum Disorders.Indian J Pediatr2019;86:394–400:394.doi:10.1007/s12098-018-02844-wOpenUrlPubMed↵NiiranenJ,KiviruusuO,VornanenR,et al.Children’s screen time and psychosocial symptoms at 5 years of age - the role of parental factors.BMC Pediatr2024;24:500.doi:10.1186/s12887-024-04915-8↵MaS,LiJ,ChenEE.Does Screen Media Hurt Young Children’s Social Development? Longitudinal Associations Between Parental Engagement, Children’s Screen Time, and Their Social Competence.Early Educ Dev2024;35:10–25.doi:10.1080/10409289.2022.2151401OpenUrl↵Govindarajan VenguidesvaraneA,VaradarajanS,RajamohanM,et al.Screen time and sleep problems in South Indian preschoolers: a community-based cross-sectional study.BMJ Open2025;15:e109376.doi:10.1136/bmjopen-2025-109376↵KaurN,GuptaM,KiranT,et al.Sleep Problems and Their Associations with Digital Screen Exposure Among 2 to 5 Years Old Children in Chandigarh, a North Indian Union Territory.Journal of Indian Association for Child and Adolescent Mental Health2022;18:45–53.doi:10.1177/09731342221096488OpenUrl↵DavisRE,ColeSM,BlakeCE,et al.Eat, play, view, sleep: exploring Mexican American mothers’ perceptions of decision making for four behaviors associated with childhood obesity risk.Appetite2016;101:104–13.doi:10.1016/j.appet.2016.03.005OpenUrlPubMed↵EkambareshwarM,EkambareshwarS,MihrshahiS,et al.Process evaluations of early childhood obesity prevention interventions delivered via telephone or text messages: a systematic review.Int J Behav Nutr Phys Act2021;18:10.doi:10.1186/s12966-020-01079-9OpenUrlPubMed↵StiglicN,VinerRM.Effects of screentime on the health and well-being of children and adolescents: a systematic review of reviews.BMJ Open2019;9:e023191.doi:10.1136/bmjopen-2018-023191↵JourdrenM,BucailleA,RoparsJ.The impact of screen exposure on attention abilities in young children: a systematic review.Pediatr Neurol2023;142:76–88.doi:10.1016/j.pediatrneurol.2023.01.012OpenUrlPubMed↵PiszkaM,KwapienE,BrasseP,et al.The Impact of Screen Time on the Health of the Pediatric Population: Short- and Long-Term Consequences for Lifestyle, Ophthalmology, and Mental Health.Cureus2025;17:e96944.doi:10.7759/cureus.96944↵McArthurBA,VolkovaV,TomopoulosS,et al.Global Prevalence of Meeting Screen Time Guidelines Among Children 5 Years and Younger.JAMA Pediatr2022;176:373.doi:10.1001/jamapediatrics.2021.6386OpenUrlCrossRefPubMed↵KaurN,GuptaM,MalhiP,et al.Prevalence of Screen Time Among Children Aged 2 to 5 Years in Chandigarh, a North Indian Union Territory.J Dev Behav Pediatr2022;43:e29–38.doi:10.1097/DBP.0000000000000964OpenUrlPubMed↵DasBK,SharmaS,KhoundM,et al.Physical Activity and Screen Time Pattern among Infants, Toddlers, and Preschool Children: A descriptive Study from Northeast India.Indian J Community Med2025;50:1069–72.doi:10.4103/ijcm.ijcm_505_24OpenUrlPubMed↵JohnJJ,JosephR,DavidA,et al.Association of screen time with parent-reported cognitive delay in preschool children of Kerala, India.BMC Pediatr2021;21:73.doi:10.1186/s12887-021-02535-3OpenUrlPubMed↵KhobragadeAW,ShenoyMS.Screen Time Among Under-Five Children in India: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis.Cureus2025;17:e85458.doi:10.7759/cureus.85458↵KwK,YkK,JhK.Associations between Parental Factors and Children’s Screen Time During the COVID-19 Pandemic in South Korea.Child Psychiatry Hum Dev2023;54:1749–58.doi:10.1007/s10578-022-01366-zOpenUrlPubMed↵UNICEF Office of Research – Innocenti.Where do rich countries stand on childcare?Florence,2021.Available:https://www.unicef.org/innocenti/reports/where-do-rich-countries-stand-childcare↵UNICEF Data.",
