  "10.1111/cch.70256": [
    {
      "text": "This study aimed to examine the association between Fijian caregivers' and children's attitudes, subjective norms and perceived behavioural control and their or their child's adherence to physical activity and screen time guidelines. METHODS: We investigated Fijian caregivers and children aged 5-17 years attitudes, subjective norms and perceived behavioural control towards meeting PA and ST guidelines through an online survey. Caregivers (OR = 1.4, CI 1.003-1.862) and children 5-8 years (OR = 16.5, CI 1.227-222.665) who believed being active would make them happier were more likely to meet PA recommendations compared to those not meeting PA recommendations.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "BACKGROUND: Only half the children in Fiji meet both physical activity (PA) and screen time (ST) recommendations.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Understanding factors associated with meeting these recommendations is important to inform the development of policies and programmes to encourage healthy behaviours.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "The survey explored these factors and their associations with meeting the Asia-Pacific Integrated 24-h Activity Guidelines for Children and Adolescents.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "RESULTS: A total of 1015 caregivers and 699 of their children completed the survey.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Caregivers who set ST restrictions (OR = 1.5, CI 1.252-1.816) and believed ST rules helped their child meet recommendations (OR = 1.3, CI 1.052-1.505) were more likely to have children who met ST recommendations versus not meet ST recommendations.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Children aged 5-8 and older children/adolescents aged 9-17 who had easy access to screens were less likely to meet ST recommendations (OR = 0.5, CI 0.224-0.984) and (OR = 0.5, CI 0.324-0.802), respectively, compared to those who did meet ST recommendations.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Children aged 5-8 years who believed it was important to follow the guidance of religious leaders were more likely to meet ST recommendations (OR = 5.4, CI 2.423-12.002) compared to those who did not meet the ST recommendation.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "CONCLUSION: Communicating through trusted community figures (such as teachers and religious leaders for younger children and ministries for caregivers) is recommended for future initiatives to increase adherence to guidelines among children and adolescents in Fiji.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    },
    {
      "text": "These initiatives should also emphasise the link between PA and both happiness and academic performance, while exploring ways to support caregivers in enforcing ST restrictions.",
      "paperTitle": "Attitudes, Norms, and Control: What Is Shaping Fijian Children's Physical Activity and Screen Time Behaviours?",
      "doi": "10.1111/cch.70256",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 9
    }
  ],
  "10.1002/oby.70181": [
    {
      "text": "OBJECTIVE: This study aimed to prospectively identify the independent, mutually adjusted, and interactive associations of screen time and physical activity with BMI and overweight/obesity risk in adolescents. RESULTS: Mean daily screen time was 6.1 (±5.2) hours and mean step count was 9265 (±3227) steps in Year 2; 32.7% were classified with overweight/obesity in Year 4. In Poisson regression models examining the exposures mutually adjusted for each other, high screen time (> 8 h/day) was associated with higher risk of overweight/obesity (risk ratio [RR], 1.09; 95% CI, 1.02-1.16; p = 0.013) compared with low screen time (0-4 h/day).",
      "paperTitle": "Associations of Screen Time and Physical Activity With Body Mass Index in Early Adolescence: A Prospective Cohort Study",
      "doi": "10.1002/oby.70181",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "METHODS: This study analyzed prospective data from 5356 US adolescents in the Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development (ABCD) Study, Year 2 (2018-2020, ages 11-12) and Year 4 (2020-2022, ages 13-14).",
      "paperTitle": "Associations of Screen Time and Physical Activity With Body Mass Index in Early Adolescence: A Prospective Cohort Study",
      "doi": "10.1002/oby.70181",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "In multivariable linear and Poisson regression models, respectively, low step count (1000-6000 steps/day) was associated with higher BMI percentile (coefficient [B], 3.27; 95% CI, 1.54-4.99; p < 0.001) and higher risk of overweight/obesity (RR, 1.23; 95% CI, 1.07-1.40; p = 0.003) compared with high step count (> 12,000 steps/day).",
      "paperTitle": "Associations of Screen Time and Physical Activity With Body Mass Index in Early Adolescence: A Prospective Cohort Study",
      "doi": "10.1002/oby.70181",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "No significant interactions were observed between screen time and step count.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations of Screen Time and Physical Activity With Body Mass Index in Early Adolescence: A Prospective Cohort Study",
      "doi": "10.1002/oby.70181",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "CONCLUSIONS: These findings suggest that obesity prevention efforts should consider both greater physical activity and less recreational screen use as behavioral interventions.",
      "paperTitle": "Associations of Screen Time and Physical Activity With Body Mass Index in Early Adolescence: A Prospective Cohort Study",
      "doi": "10.1002/oby.70181",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    }
  ],
