      "text": "However, evidence on the association between screen time (eg, television viewing, smartphone use, video gaming) and health outcomes among university students remains inconsistent. OBJECTIVE This systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to quantify the association between screen time duration and physical and mental health outcomes among university students 17 years or older. METHODS A systematic search of PubMed, PsycINFO, CINAHL was searched using a combination of subject headings and free text for studies published up to December 31, 2024. Observational and experimental studies (using baseline data) were included if they assessed screen time duration and physical or mental health outcomes in university students, were published in English, and provided sufficient data to compute effect sizes. Radom-effects meta-analyses were conducted to pool correlation coefficients. RESULTS Of 18,349 nonduplicate records screened, 685 full-text studies were assessed for eligibility, and 51 studies met the inclusion criteria, comprising 55 independent samples and 110,331 participants (72,520/110,331, 65.5% female). Longer screen time duration was significantly associated with poorer physical health outcomes (30 samples; r=0.211, 95%CI 0.159-0.261) and mental health (25 samples; r=0.167,95%CI 0.081-0.251). Significant between-study heterogeneity was observed. Meta-regression analyses indicated that study quality significantly moderated associations with physical health. CONCLUSIONS This systematic review and meta-analysis found small to moderate significant correlations between screen time and poorer physical and mental health among university students. While many included studies were of high quality, heterogeneity in how screen time is measured, variability in health outcome instruments and operational definitions, as well as risk of bias, limits causal inference and the direct translation of findings into practice. The findings nonetheless reveal screen-related behaviors may represent an important and potentially modifiable correlate of student health. Future research should prioritize longitudinal designs and standardized measurement approaches to strengthen causal inference and inform evidence based digital health strategies within higher education settings.",
