ight/obeseUnderweightOverweight/obeseUnderweightOverweight/obeseUnderweightAdj. OR (95%CI)p-valueScreen time1–2 h0.83 (0.61–1.13)0.240.23 (0.18–0.30)<0.0012.42 (1.40–4.19)<0.0010.25 (0.14–0.43)<0.0010.17 (0.11–0.28)<0.0010.08 (0.05–0.11)<0.0012–4 h35.69 (25.35–50.25)<0.0014.01 (2.95–5.48)<0.00188.58 (47.35–165.68)<0.0014.40 (2.38–8.11)<0.00111.88 (7.23–19.51)<0.0011.52 (0.96–2.38)0.28>4 h28.80 (17.82–46.54)<0.0010.42 (0.16–1.09)0.0769.45 (28.27–170.62)<0.0011.47 (0.37–5.41)0.619.22 (4.83–17.62)<0.0010.08 (0.02–0.32)<0.001<1 h111111Adjusted odds ratios for overweight/obese and underweight status with screen time duration, adjusted for other risk factors, stratified by total population and age groups (6–11 and 12–19 years). The asterisk (*) denotes statistically significant standardized path coefficients in the structural equation model (p< 0.05).The structural equation model (SEM) analysis, conducted with a sample size of 3,920, examines the relationships between diet (mixed vs. vegetarian), screen time, gender (female vs. male), physical activity, and BMI outcomes (normal/underweight vs. overweight/obese), explaining 28.1% of the variance in BMI (McFadden’s Pseudo R2).",
