    {
      "text": "The scores of the questions ranged from 0 to 50, and the final categories were assigned an ordinal scale: SEL A (37–50 points), SEL B (22–36), SEL C (13–21), SEL D (8–12) and SEL E (≤ 7 points).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 39
    },
    {
      "text": "SEL A represented individuals with the highest level of resources, while SEL E corresponded to those with the lowest.Survey administrationThe survey was administered by IPSOS Opinion y Mercados SA through face‒to‒face interviews with the selected households.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 40
    },
    {
      "text": "The respondents used the iField application (IPSOS Group SA) on a portable device to respond to the survey [26]; this application allows determination of the geolocation of the interviewers during field work, quality control of the data in real time, and assurance of adherence to the sample design described.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 41
    },
    {
      "text": "We extracted the point prevalences and their corresponding 95% confidence interval (95% CI).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 42
    },
    {
      "text": "Finally, we obtain three CNP estimates for three observation points that were depicted in a bar graph.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 43
    },
    {
      "text": "The data for the first point, the most current one, were estimated with the methodology described above for February 2025.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 44
    },
    {
      "text": "The data for the second and third points were obtained from our previous study based on a survey conducted on November 24 and 25, 2022 [11].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 45
    },
    {
      "text": "Age is reported as the mean and 95% confidence interval (95% CI).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 46
    },
    {
      "text": "Categorical variables are reported as absolute counts and weighted proportions with their 95% CIs.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 47
    },
    {
      "text": "This prevalence estimates used the total survey population as the denominator.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 48
    },
    {
      "text": "In addition, we estimated these prevalences across strata defined by sex, age group, urban or rural residence, macro-region, socioeconomic status, and frequency of screen use.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 49
    },
    {
      "text": "Weighted proportions yielded population-representative estimates for adults aged 18 years and older living in Peru in February 2025.To evaluate the association between the independent variables and CNP, we used the chi-square test with second-order Rao‒Scott correction.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 50
    },
    {
      "text": "These stratification variables were selected to evaluate their role as potential confounders in the association of interest.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 51
    },
    {
      "text": "We defined the reference category as “No, I have never had neck pain or I have had it very rarely”.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 52
    },
    {
      "text": "The model included variables that showed statistically significant associations with CNP (p < 0.05) and the exposure in the crude analysis.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 53
    },
    {
      "text": "Results were expressed as odds ratios (ORs) with their corresponding 95% confidence intervals (CIs).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 54
    },
    {
      "text": "Nagelkerke’s pseudo-R-squared was estimated to evaluate the goodness of fit of the model.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 55
    },
    {
      "text": "We verified the assumption of independence of the observations and the absence of multicollinearity with the variance inflation factor (in which VIF > 10 indicated multicollinearity).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 56
    },
    {
      "text": "Although the dependent variable is depicted with an ordinal scale, the ordinal logistic regression model did not meet the proportional odds assumption according to the Wald parallel lines test (F Wald = 3.418, p = 0.001), so we instead selected multinomial logistic regression [28].Inferential analysis was performed with the survey data analysis modulesvyin STATA version 16.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 57
    },
    {
      "text": "The described analyses were performed on a subpopulation of participants who provided valid answers to the questions (using thesubpopcommand); that is, we excluded those who answered “not specified” to the questions that assessed CNP and screen time.Ethical aspectsThe study protocol, including the use of verbal informed consent, was approved by the Institutional Research Ethics Committee of the University of Piura (file No.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 58
    },
    {
      "text": "CIEI.0039.01).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 59
    },
    {
      "text": "Trained interviewers obtained verbal informed consent from all participants after providing an explanation of the study objectives and before data collection.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 60
    },
    {
      "text": "Participants indicated their willingness to participate by responding “yes” or “no,” and these responses were recorded in the iField application on a portable electronic device.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 61
    },
    {
      "text": "The survey was anonymous, and the statistical analysis was performed using an anonymized database.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 62
    },
    {
      "text": "The survey did not include minors (<18 years old).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 63
    },
    {
      "text": "This report was prepared in accordance with the STROBE guidelines for cross-sectional studies.ResultsSample characteristicsA total of 1208 people with a mean age of 40.34 years (95% CI: 39.34–41.34) were interviewed.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 64
    },
    {
      "text": "Of these, 15.2% were 60 years or older, and 51.6% were women.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 65
    },
    {
      "text": "A total of 13.9% of the respondents reported that they do not look or rarely looked at electronic device screens, whereas 12.2% reported looking at screens for more than 8 hours/day.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 66
    },
    {
      "text": "These differences were not statistically significant (p = 0.525).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 67
    },
    {
      "text": "The prepandemic prevalence values were lower than those post pandemic: 6.1% for CNP daily, 6.3% for CNP once a week on average and 10.2% for CNP once a month on average (Fig 1A).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 68
    },
    {
      "text": "Among those who did not view screens, 58.4% did not report CNP.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 69
    },
    {
      "text": "This trend was not observed in the other age groups.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 70
    },
    {
      "text": "Additionally, residents of the eastern macroregion had higher odds of daily CNP (OR = 2.96, p < 0.001) compared with those living in Metropolitan Lima.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 71
    },
    {
      "text": "These prevalences are slightly greater than those reported at the end of social restrictions imposed due to the COVID-19 pandemic [11].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 72
    },
    {
      "text": "Concurrently, the proportion of individuals who reported not viewing electronic device screens decreased from 24.6% in November 2022 to 14.0% in 2025.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 73
    },
    {
      "text": "Screen viewing levels were clearly associated with CNP and its presentation pattern.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 74
    },
    {
      "text": "However, we must emphasize that the GBP defines neck pain as pain located from the occiput to the first thoracic vertebra that lasts for at least one day.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 75
    },
    {
      "text": "However, in the period following the end of social restrictions and up to two years later, the increase was less pronounced and non-significant.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 76
    },
    {
      "text": "In the abovementioned study [11], the prepandemic prevalence could have been affected by recall bias.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 77
    },
    {
      "text": "However, the occurrence of an unusual historical event—such as the onset of mandatory social restrictions due to a pandemic—could have made it easier for respondents to recall what happened from that point backward with greater precision [30].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 78
    },
    {
      "text": "Another explanation is that a sedentary lifestyle and increased work at home contributed to a marked increase in the prevalence of CNP at different frequencies.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 79
    },
    {
      "text": "The return to normality could have slowed the rate of increase in the prevalence of CNP, and the slight increase identified could be explained by other causes.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 80
    },
    {
      "text": "This association was independent of age and sex [11].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 81
    },
    {
      "text": "Similar findings have been consistently reported in studies conducted among students and various occupational groups included in a meta-analysis.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 82
    },
    {
      "text": "In contrast, the proportion of individuals who spend more than 8 hours per day viewing screens has remained relatively stable at approximately 12%.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 83
    },
    {
      "text": "This increase is likely attributable to lifestyle changes and the growing reliance on technology for both work-related and recreational purposes.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 84
    },
    {
      "text": "This result is consistent with the findings at the population level in 2022 [11] and from a population study in Spain in 2017 [34].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 85
    },
    {
      "text": "Another mechanism involved is related to inflammation, in which oestrogens have been proposed to contribute to inflammatory pain [35] through the activity of Toll-like receptor 4 (TRL4) and its signalling cascades [36].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 86
    },
    {
      "text": "In addition to biological factors, social factors and lifestyle play important roles, especially because of the greater sedentary lifestyle and greater burden of depression observed in women.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 87
    },
    {
      "text": "Evidence indicates that interventions based on physical activity and exercise may be beneficial in reducing chronic pain [37].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 88
    },
    {
      "text": "Likewise, it has also been suggested that CNP may be associated with depression, mediated by factors such as reduced physical activity and poor sleep quality [38].Our results also revealed a clear association between age and CNP.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 89
    },
    {
      "text": "These age-specific differences may reflect confounding by age.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 90
    },
    {
      "text": "By 2024, 97% of the population of Peru aged 19–24 years were internet users, followed by those aged 25–40 years (92%), and daily internet access is usually achieved with a cell phone [39].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 91
    },
    {
      "text": "A magnetic resonance imaging study demonstrated that excessive mobile phone use among adults aged 18–44 was associated with a higher likelihood of cervical disc degeneration, independent of sedentary occupational activity and age [40].",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 92
    },
    {
      "text": "In Peru, this region includes departments of the Amazon, where, owing to economic and climatic factors, motorcycles are the primary means of transportation.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 93
    },
    {
      "text": "By 2023, national data in Peru indicated that up to 52% of the households in these departments owned a motorcycle.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 94
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiological studies are needed to confirm how riding motorcycles increases the risk of musculoskeletal disorders in residents of the Peruvian Amazon.This study has several limitations.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 95
    },
    {
      "text": "First, both the exposure and outcome variables were self-reported.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 96
    },
    {
      "text": "Nonetheless, the self-reported assessment of CNP is particularly susceptible to recall bias, as respondents were asked to report symptoms occurring over the previous six months.",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 97
    },
    {
      "text": "Furthermore, precision in exposure measurement could be enhanced by differentiating the context of use (occupational versus recreational) and the type of device employed (tablets, smartphones, or computers).",
      "paperTitle": "Screen time and chronic neck pain in Peru: A comparative population-based cross-sectional study in the COVID-19 post-pandemic period",
      "doi": "10.1371/journal.pone.0344257",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 98
    },
    {
      "text": "Given the nature of these variables, reverse causality cannot be ruled out.",
