  "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490": [
    {
      "text": "Importance: Understanding the within-person association between daily screen use and sleep can help clarify whether this association reflects potentially causal effects or confounding factors. Objective: To synthesize evidence on the within-person association between daily screen use and sleep among youth and to examine whether this association varies by screen type, sleep assessment method, or timing of screen use. A small significant positive within-person correlation was found between screen time and sleep onset (r = 0.079; 95% CI, 0.010-0.149; P = .03), indicating later bedtimes on days with increased screen use. Conclusions and Relevance: Per the results of this systematic review and meta-analysis, daily screen time has a small but significant within-person correlation with later sleep onset; however, short-term daily fluctuations in screen time appear to have minimal impact on sleep duration, efficiency, or quality.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Data Sources: Ovid MEDLINE, PsycINFO, SPORTDiscus, and Scopus were searched from inception to August 22, 2025, with forward and backward snowball searching.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Data were analyzed in October 2025.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Study Selection: Studies reporting on children, adolescents, and young adults aged 3 to 25 years that assessed sleep, measured screen time, and examined daily within-person associations between screen time and sleep were analyzed.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Data Extraction and Synthesis: Random-effects meta-analysis with cluster-robust variance estimation estimated pooled within-person correlations.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Subgroup analyses examined moderating effects of screen type, assessment method, and timing.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Main Outcomes and Measures: The primary outcome was within-person correlations between screen time and sleep outcomes, including total sleep time, sleep onset, sleep onset latency, sleep efficiency, wake after sleep onset, and subjective sleep quality.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "Results: A total of 25 studies reporting on 4562 participants were included.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "No significant associations were observed for total sleep time, sleep onset latency, sleep efficiency, wake after sleep onset, or subjective sleep quality.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    },
    {
      "text": "Moderator analyses revealed that the association between screen time and subjective sleep quality significantly differed by timing, with screen time after bedtime showing stronger negative correlation (r = -0.092) vs daily (r = -0.026) or evening (r = -0.005) assessments (P = .007).",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 9
    },
    {
      "text": "No other significant moderator effects were observed.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "Screen time may delay bedtime but is not inherently detrimental to other aspects of sleep health in youth, contrasting with between-person studies showing stronger adverse associations.",
      "paperTitle": "Within-Person Association Between Daily Screen Use and Sleep in Youth",
      "doi": "10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.6490",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 11
    }
  ],
  "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298": [
    {
      "text": "Digital parenting guidance often fails to reflect early caregiving realities, particularly for first-time parents navigating screentime. Drawing on two interview rounds and three focus groups with 23 Australian first-time parents of children aged birth-to-four, and informed by a socio-ecological approach, we explore how parents interpret and reconcile conflicting guidance in everyday care. Rather than restriction-first messages, policy and practice should advance developmentally informed, parent-centred strategies. Parents frequently relied on online and peer advice, adapting digital practices to household pragmatics and values.",
      "paperTitle": "“No screens before two”: How first-time Australian parents negotiate enduring digital media guidelines",
      "doi": "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Although professional guidelines have shifted from rigid time limits toward co-engagement and content quality, these shifts are inconsistently communicated and poorly absorbed into public discourse.",
      "paperTitle": "“No screens before two”: How first-time Australian parents negotiate enduring digital media guidelines",
      "doi": "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Meanwhile, technopanic narratives fuel confusion, guilt, and social judgement.",
      "paperTitle": "“No screens before two”: How first-time Australian parents negotiate enduring digital media guidelines",
      "doi": "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "While some studies link extended screen use with adverse outcomes, we examine how correlational findings become blanket prescriptions in early caregiving.",
      "paperTitle": "“No screens before two”: How first-time Australian parents negotiate enduring digital media guidelines",
      "doi": "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Reflexive thematic analysis identified five interconnected challenges: rigid and inconsistent guidelines; the limits of daily caregiving; technopanic and anxiety; contradictory, overwhelming online information; and the emotional toll of digital decision-making.",
      "paperTitle": "“No screens before two”: How first-time Australian parents negotiate enduring digital media guidelines",
      "doi": "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Despite awareness of updated recommendations, many continued to cite older, time-based rules for their clarity and perceived legitimacy.",
      "paperTitle": "“No screens before two”: How first-time Australian parents negotiate enduring digital media guidelines",
      "doi": "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Overall, discourse should move beyond fear-based messaging toward approaches that recognise caregiving realities and counter technopanic.",
      "paperTitle": "“No screens before two”: How first-time Australian parents negotiate enduring digital media guidelines",
      "doi": "10.1080/17482798.2026.2644298",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    }
  ],
  "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x": [
    {
      "text": "We aimed to estimate the potential reduction in radiologists' workload by replacing one of the two radiologists with AI in screen-reading mammograms in BreastScreen Norway. CONCLUSION: Implementation of AI in mammographic screening has the potential to reduce the screen-reading workload for breast radiologists. Clinical relevance Implementing AI was shown to have limited potential in saving radiologists' time in screen-reading mammograms.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "OBJECTIVE: There is a lack of breast radiologists in Norway and in Europe.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) offers an alternative to solely human readers and has demonstrated promising results in cancer detection in mammographic screening.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "MATERIALS AND METHODS: BreastScreen Norway targets about 680,000 women aged 50-69 who are invited biennially.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "The participation rates for each screening round are about 75%.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "All the screening mammograms are independently read by two radiologists.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "We collected information about the number of radiologist positions from all 16 breast centers in the country in 2024, while the number of screening examinations performed and the time spent on screen-reading and consensus were extracted from the screening database.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "We used 1 min for each screen-reading to estimate the screen-reading workload performed by the radiologists and calculated the time saved if one reader were replaced by AI.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "RESULTS: Screen-reading required a total of 6.5 man-years in BreastScreen Norway.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 8
    },
    {
      "text": "Implementing AI as one of the two readers is thus able to reduce the screen-reading workload by 50%, from 6.5 to 3.3 man-years.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 9
    },
    {
      "text": "The workload reduction corresponds to a reduction from 9% to 4.5% of the total workload for radiologists at the Norwegian breast centers.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "The reduced screening volume is of moderate influence on the overall workload for breast radiologists.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 11
    },
    {
      "text": "KEY POINTS: Question How much radiology time is expected to be saved if AI were used as one of the two readers of screening mammograms?",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 12
    },
    {
      "text": "Findings Use of AI as one of the two readers, reducing the screen reading volume by 50%, was of moderate influence on the total workload for breast radiologists.",
      "paperTitle": "How much radiologist time can be saved by implementing AI in screen-reading mammograms?",
      "doi": "10.1007/s00330-025-12273-x",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 13
