  "10.1145/3789624.3789637": [
    {
      "text": "This study investigates a gamified real-time assistance concept for forest harvesters, integrating augmented reality (AR) and LiDAR-based data visualization through a head-up display (HUD). A field test was conducted in Ilomantsi, Finland, employing a John Deere 1170G harvester equipped with an external LiDAR scanner and an in-cabin holographic HUD. The system successfully projected tree positions and estimated diameters onto the HUD during the felling of 60 trees.",
      "paperTitle": "Real-time Assistance with AR HUD Screen in the Forest Harvester Machine",
      "doi": "10.1145/3789624.3789637",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "The approach draws from gamification principles—enhancing engagement, feedback, and situational awareness—to support operator decision-making and ergonomics.",
      "paperTitle": "Real-time Assistance with AR HUD Screen in the Forest Harvester Machine",
      "doi": "10.1145/3789624.3789637",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Real-time tree detection and diameter estimation were achieved through LiDAR data processing with a YOLO-based convolutional neural network.",
      "paperTitle": "Real-time Assistance with AR HUD Screen in the Forest Harvester Machine",
      "doi": "10.1145/3789624.3789637",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Operator workload, assessed using the NASA-TLX method, indicated low cognitive strain and positive usability feedback.",
      "paperTitle": "Real-time Assistance with AR HUD Screen in the Forest Harvester Machine",
      "doi": "10.1145/3789624.3789637",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "The results demonstrate the feasibility of real-time holographic visualization in dynamic forest environments but highlight calibration and information flow challenges.",
      "paperTitle": "Real-time Assistance with AR HUD Screen in the Forest Harvester Machine",
      "doi": "10.1145/3789624.3789637",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Further improvements are needed in system setup, eye-position alignment, and data visualization to optimize ergonomic benefits and decision support in mechanized harvesting operations.",
      "paperTitle": "Real-time Assistance with AR HUD Screen in the Forest Harvester Machine",
      "doi": "10.1145/3789624.3789637",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 5
    }
  ],
  "10.1177/20501579261454068": [
    {
      "text": "Within this broader research context, previous research has shown that the various uses of the internet have become integrated into everyday life experiences, making digital technologies increasingly embedded in young people's daily routines and affecting domains such as time management, subjective experiences of time, and time perception. Using a comprehensive quantitative design, the current study explored how the continuous use of hyperconnected technologies (especially smartphones) shapes students’ subjective perception of online time and behaviors related to mobile phone use (perceived and excessive use) in students’ daily lives. The results show that different patterns of internet use are associated with distinct ways of experiencing and managing everyday time, which in turn relate to variations in perceived and excessive mobile phone use. Overall, the findings shed light on experiential mechanisms linking internet use, subjective time perception, and mobile technology practices, contributing to a more nuanced understanding of hyperconnected technologies in students’ daily lives.",
      "paperTitle": "Between Screens and Time: Subjective Online Time Perception and Digital Technology Use Among University Students",
      "doi": "10.1177/20501579261454068",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "A growing body of research has attempted to understand the impact of social media platforms and digital technologies on younger generations’ daily lives and everyday experiences.",
      "paperTitle": "Between Screens and Time: Subjective Online Time Perception and Digital Technology Use Among University Students",
      "doi": "10.1177/20501579261454068",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Extensive questionnaires were completed by 732 undergraduate and postgraduate students.",
      "paperTitle": "Between Screens and Time: Subjective Online Time Perception and Digital Technology Use Among University Students",
      "doi": "10.1177/20501579261454068",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "The findings also show how subjective online time perception contributes to differentiated experiential outcomes, highlighting the role of usage purpose in shaping digital practices.",
      "paperTitle": "Between Screens and Time: Subjective Online Time Perception and Digital Technology Use Among University Students",
      "doi": "10.1177/20501579261454068",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    }
  ],
  "10.1016/j.ecresq.2026.07.001": [
    {
      "text": "The aim of the present study was to examine the decision-making processes that lead parents to allow screen time for their children. In line with the framework of the Theory of Planned Behavior, findings indicate that parents' subjective norms about screen time are linked to their attitudes toward children’s screen time. These results suggest that children’s age and subjective norms might play an important role in children's screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Behind screen time: An examination of parents’ decisions on young children’s screen time",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.ecresq.2026.07.001",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "To this end, screen time among children aged 1 to 48 months was examined through a survey of 129 parents using an online questionnaire.",
      "paperTitle": "Behind screen time: An examination of parents’ decisions on young children’s screen time",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.ecresq.2026.07.001",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "A more positive attitude toward children’s screen time was in turn linked to a higher intention to permit screen time, which was associated with a higher level of children’s screen time.",
      "paperTitle": "Behind screen time: An examination of parents’ decisions on young children’s screen time",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.ecresq.2026.07.001",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Perceived behavioral control was not found to play a strong role in this process.",
      "paperTitle": "Behind screen time: An examination of parents’ decisions on young children’s screen time",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.ecresq.2026.07.001",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Moreover, the age of the child was linked to more favorable subjective norms and attitudes toward screen time as well as to all other aspects of the decision-making process.",
      "paperTitle": "Behind screen time: An examination of parents’ decisions on young children’s screen time",
      "doi": "10.1016/j.ecresq.2026.07.001",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    }
  ],
  "10.5281/zenodo.21936625": [
    {
      "text": "This research examines how exposure to emotional content on social media, beyond time spent online alone, influences young adult mental health, with a focus on sleep as a behavioral pathway and content-aware feedback as a potential intervention. Study 2 used an experimental design in which participants were randomly assigned to view social media posts varying in emotional tone; exposure to negative content led to worse immediate mood and greater intentions to delay sleep. Compared with time-only feedback, content-aware feedback provided by Mooditer significantly increased awareness of emotionally risky exposure and strengthened intentions to regulate nighttime social media use.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time",
      "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21936625",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Using a multi-study design integrating behavioral science, statistics, and data science, four studies were conducted.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time",
      "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21936625",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Study 1 analyzed survey data from 503 young adults (ages 18–25) and found that exposure to negative emotional content was significantly associated with higher depressive symptoms, with sleep quality mediating this relationship.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time",
      "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21936625",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Studies 3 and 4 introduced and evaluated Mooditer, a privacy-preserving application that analyzes and summarizes users’ emotional exposure patterns.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time",
      "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21936625",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "Together, these findings suggest that emotional characteristics of social media content play a critical role in young adults’ well-being and highlight the potential of targeted feedback tools to support healthier digital behavior beyond conventional screen-time limits.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time",
      "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21936625",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 4
    }
  ],
  "10.4324/9781003751236": [
    {
      "text": "Beyond Screen Time Guilt: The Role of TV and Video in Early Child Development by Cary Bazalgette offers a fresh and insightful perspective on babies’ and toddlers’ relationship with moving image media. Forget the guilt-inducing screen time rules and dire warnings about developmental harm – this book reveals what’s really happening when your little one is captivated by their favourite show. Drawing on meticulous observational research and 35 years of media education expertise, Cary Bazalgette uncovers the remarkable ways in which children under three actively engage with film and television. It transforms how we understand the relationship between young children and moving image media, offering a refreshing and informed alternative to screen-use guidelines.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time Guilt",
      "doi": "10.4324/9781003751236",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 0
    },
    {
      "text": "Bazalgette critiques the flawed research behind screen time rules, exposing the limitations of surveys and experimental studies in this context.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time Guilt",
      "doi": "10.4324/9781003751236",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "She provides vivid examples of typical behaviours, such as spontaneous laughter, frightened responses, and apparent \"addiction,\" while analysing the learning challenges that young children eagerly embrace.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time Guilt",
      "doi": "10.4324/9781003751236",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "The book also emphasises the value of shared viewing experiences and offers detailed insights into how specific TV episodes foster learning.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time Guilt",
      "doi": "10.4324/9781003751236",
      "source": "openalex",
      "yearPublished": 2026,
      "chunk_index_in_doc": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "With its clear style, good humour, and evidence-based reassurance, Beyond Screen Time Guilt is an essential read for parents, early childhood practitioners, and students.",
      "paperTitle": "Beyond Screen Time Guilt",
      "doi": "10.4324/9781003751236",
